const e={31:["Young Master Hua, watching the performance with sheer delight, raised his wine cup to toast Ziyun and the rest of the company. The entire table had no choice but to drain their cups in unison, collectively praising Pincai and Zipei for an acting performance so divine and marvelous that it lay far beyond the reach of ordinary theater players.",`A moment later, the two men stepped down from the stage. Zipei immediately pointed at Wenze and scolded, "You really do not know good from bad. I exhausted myself on that stage, yet you sit down here chattering and endlessly picking apart my performance." Wenze cried foul at the top of his lungs, "When did I ever critique you? Why are you picking such blind faults with me? I was merely having an idle chat with Master Jingyi." Cixian chimed in, "It truly was just idle chat. Furthermore, you sang so superbly that we could not stop praising you; what room could there possibly be for criticism?" Young Master Hua laughed, "From what I heard them say, you truly resembled Yan Poxi on stage. If you had been born into a woman's body, you would certainly be a restless one." Zipei retorted, "Well, isn't this fine! You force me onto the stage, and then you make a laughingstock of me." Xu Ziyun then turned to Pincai and asked, "Brother Wei, your mastery of music is truly exquisite. I must ask for your guidance in the future. When you have the leisure, please do come visit my humble garden." Pincai nodded repeatedly in agreement, "I have never had a proper teacher; I learned everything simply by listening. This was my very first time performing on a stage. Please, do not laugh at my poor efforts." Following this, the group played drinking games and guessed fingers, making merry for quite some time until the clock struck midnight. Ziyun remarked, "It is only the autumn equinox; the nights should not feel this short." Cixian replied, "It does feel quite long. If you were to try sitting here all alone until this hour, you would find it quite unbearable." Ziyun said, "We have already crossed into the midnight hour of the fifteenth day, and dawn will soon be upon us. Let us clear the feast and stop the opera. We can talk a while longer, and when dawn breaks, we shall disperse." Zhang Zhongyu interjected, "The city gates are already open by now; those who wish to leave may do so." Young Master Hua said, "What is the rush? Dispersing at the hour of Chen will not be too late." He then ordered the feast cleared and the opera halted, as the servants arranged the tea sets and brought in freshly brewed fragrant tea. Ziyun looked around and noticed that Qinyan was missing, seeing only Shanzhi leaning wearily against a screen. When Young Master Hua inquired about Qinyan's whereabouts, Shanzhi replied, "He was feeling unwell, so he has gone to sleep." The truth was that whenever Qinyan found himself amidst such bustling crowds, it stirred the heavy cares in his heart, leaving him feeling deeply sorrowful. Moreover, seeing Feng Zipei and Pincai performing together on stage, he felt a strong disdain for them. Thus, he had asked Shanzhi to plead illness on his behalf and quietly slipped away.`,`Young Master Hua summoned the dans once again. Without their stage robes or hats, and dressed entirely in their everyday attire, he had them sit in a row according to their age. He then declared, "I know that beyond the operatic arts, each of you possesses your own unique excellence—whether it be in poetry, painting, calligraphy, or the playing of string and bamboo instruments. Both today and last time, the wine and drama have delayed us, preventing you from displaying your true talents. The hour is still early. Those who excel in poetry, feel free to compose a few lines; those who excel in painting, brush a few strokes. There is no need for false modesty." The dans remained silent. Ziyun and Wenze stood up and said, "Splendid, splendid! Let us assign the tasks." Turning to Huifang, he announced, "Meixiang excels in poetry, Yaoqing is skilled in painting, Jingfang is a master of the sword dance, Xiangwan possesses beautiful calligraphy, Peixian is adept at composing lyrics, Ruixiang has a gift for riddles and witty banter, Shouxiang is masterful with the vertical flute, and Xiaomei plays the mouth-organ beautifully. It is only a pity that Yuyi has fallen ill, for he plays a wonderful rendition of 'Wild Geese Alighting on Level Sand'." Hearing this, Young Master Hua ordered him to be roused from his sleep and told Shanzhi to fetch the pipa. The servants brought out brushes, inkstones, and musical instruments, setting them up at various stations around the room. Cixian said, "Let me act as the marshal and assign the roles: First, I call upon Yuyi and Shouxiang to blend the melodies of the qin and xiao. Next, Yaoqing shall paint a scene, while Meixiang, Xiangwan, and Peixian improvise poems to match the view and inscribe them upon the scroll. Then, Shanzhi and Xiaomei shall engage in a musical contest with the sheng and pipa. Following that, Ruixiang will present a few lantern riddles and share a jest. Finally, Jingfang will perform a sword dance, with the blade glinting bright as the wind rises, evoking the fierce spirit of the Yuyang drum. What say you all to this?" Everyone applauded the arrangement, and the dans stepped forward to perform in turn.`,"Left with no other choice, Qinyan drew his delicate brows together and began to tune the strings. Beside him, Shufang followed the musical score and began to blow softly upon the xiao. Qinyan's mind was profoundly troubled, and his fingers were out of practice; thus, he ground out a rendition of 'Wild Geese Alighting on Level Sand' that sounded harsh and halting.",`The melody of the xiao, however, flowed with a gentle and peaceful grace. Young Master Hua shook his head and commented, "The sound of the qin is poor, but the xiao is quite lovely." Ziyun interjected, "The qin is inherently difficult to master; we must still give him credit." Cixian added, "I imagine you have not practiced in a long time, and so the skill has grown rusty." Qinyan replied, "I have not touched the instrument for half a year. Just now, I nearly forgot the third phrase of the fourth movement. Shouxiang's flute playing, however, is even better than it was before." Shufang explained, "I study diligently under my master. Master Jingyi tutors me in the flute every third day without fail, which is why I have not grown rusty." Qinyan fell silent. Reflecting on the past and comparing it to the present, a wave of melancholy washed over him, and tears nearly slipped from his eyes. He could only step back and stand quietly aside. Cixian and Ziyun looked on, their hearts filled with a deep and tender pity for him.`,"Meanwhile, Yuan Baozhu had spread a piece of fine silk across the painting desk, pondering deeply on what subject he ought to paint.",`He thought for a long while but could not settle on an idea. Huifang and Sulan stood before him, whispering, "What are you going to paint? Tell us, so we might fix upon a theme and begin drafting our poems in our minds." Baozhu, still lacking a clear direction, pulled them over to the railing to discuss what would be best to paint. Time was pressing, so he could not employ the meticulous gongbi style; yet, merely dashing off a few strokes of orchids or bamboo would not suit the grand occasion. Huifang suggested, "I have thought of a theme, though I am unsure if it aligns with your vision. I believe you should paint a small handscroll. Use black ink to sketch a few pavilions and towers, adding thickets of elegant bamboo. Arrange the near and far distances carefully, and paint a moon high above. Then, use indigo washes to create a few drifting clouds and mist. This perfectly captures the atmosphere of tonight. We could title it 'A Picture of Fine Wind and Bright Moon on a Beautiful Night.' What do you think?" Hearing this, Baozhu was overjoyed. He discreetly bowed to the others in gratitude and returned to his seat. Emboldened, he applied three parts meticulous detail to seven parts freehand expression, sweeping his brush across the silk in the grand manner of Wang Lutai.`,"Cixian and the others dared not draw too close, fearing they might disturb him and spoil the painting. Watching carefully from a distance, Cixian noted that Baozhu's brush moved with swift, unfettered grace, and a look of bright satisfaction had already begun to dawn on his face. Over on the other side, Huifang and his two companions clustered closely together. Li Yulin bowed his head in deep concentration, while Sulan continuously slipped a string of fragrant prayer beads through his fingers. Huifang kept his eyes fixed on Baozhu's brushwork, quietly offering him subtle guidance.",`In less than half a shichen, the painting was complete—a small, horizontal scroll spanning just over two feet. Young Master Hua, Ziyun, and the rest gathered around, their praise unceasing. Young Master Hua was absolutely delighted, praising him loudly: "It is truly a marvel that he could achieve such a feat. It is no wonder that lately everyone speaks so highly of their talent and beauty; they put those old, money-grubbing xianggong of the past to utter shame." Cixian lightly touched up a few spots, and it truly became a masterful painting. Young Master Hua then turned to Huifang and asked, "I trust you have your inscribed poems ready?" Huifang replied, "We have them, but I fear they are rather poor." Standing by the desk, he found a piece of fine stationery and wrote down a seven-character quatrain. Young Master Hua read it aloud:

'On this fine night of lanterns and moon, we admire the autumn light,
While the clamor of silk and bamboo echoes from both wings of the hall.
I wager that Consort Chang'e in her palace fears the lonely silence,
And so sent the wind to waft her down into this splendid hall.'

When Young Master Hua finished reading, he slapped the table and cried out in sheer admiration. Cixian, Wenze, and Ziyun were equally effusive in their praise, remarking, "You all have been making merry all day, yet he has captured the entire essence of it in a mere twenty-eight characters. He has said all there is to say, with meaning still lingering in the air. Even we could not have achieved this." Young Master Hua read the poem twice more, his voice full of sighs of admiration.`,`Wenze said, "It is truly superb, though the third line might benefit from a slight refinement of a few characters." Huifang humbly replied, "Please, I invite you to correct it." Wenze suggested, "Perhaps we could change it to 'I imagine Chang'e must fear the lonely silence.' It gives the poetic mood a more remote and ethereal quality." Everyone agreed that the change was excellent. Zhongyu and Pincai were secretly astonished; they never expected such profound talent from all of them. They had previously suspected them of using ghostwriters, but seeing them tested in person today proved beyond a doubt that their skills were genuine. Only Feng Zipei paid no attention to the literary proceedings. Seeing a large plate heaped with osmanthus blossoms on the table, he gathered a handful, asked a page boy for a red thread, and holding one end while the boy held the other, he began threading the blossoms one by one until they formed a large, fragrant flower ball. Tossing it lightly in his hands a few times, he walked over and hung it upon Young Master Hua's lapel. The Young Master plucked it off, inhaled its sweet scent, and laughed, "You went through such painstaking effort to string this together; you should enjoy it yourself." Zipei took it back and wandered off to fiddle with a pipa. Sulan and Yulin had also finished writing their pieces. First, they looked at Sulan's poem:

'The golden goblets brim with rich jade nectar,
The autumn light is as warm and gentle as a face of spring,
The gates of Chang'e's palace unlatch tier upon tier,
As the radiant light pierces through twelve folds of pearl blinds.'`,`Young Master Hua offered the same high praise, saying, "Their craftsmanship is equally matched; it is as if the great poets Yuan Zhen and Bai Juyi have appeared in the same age." Ziyun added, "I would also suggest changing two characters. The 'Chang'e' in the third line repeats the word from the previous poem. Would it not be better to change it to 'The gates of the Cold Moon palace unlatch tier upon tier'?" Sulan agreed, "Indeed, that is a wonderful improvement." At first, Ziyun had worried that Sulan's skills might fall short of Huifang's, but only now did his heart truly settle. They then looked at Yulin's lyric, composed to the tune of 'A Trace of Sand'. The lyric read:

'Tender dances and drunken songs fill the deep courtyard,
Amidst embroidered curtains and brocade screens, the fragrance is soft.
Three thousand guests with pearled shoes,
Gather the worthies together.
If the moon has a heart, let it linger and stay,
If man has a heart, let him not depart.'

'Listen not to the crowing of the morning cock,
With its chaotic, early cries.'

All who read it beamed with smiles, praising it even more fervently, saying, "Such tender sentiments flowing from a fragrant mouth; it comes alive on the paper. How could one not be completely captivated by it?" Young Master Hua said, "It is truly excellent, but I would like to swap a few characters: change 'Gather the worthies together' to 'Meet with the immortals,' and change 'chaotic, early cries' to 'it is only the third watch.' Does that sound agreeable?" The crowd voiced their unanimous approval. Cixian urged them to quickly copy their final drafts onto the scroll. Huifang and Yulin both wanted Sulan to write it out for them, but Young Master Hua would not allow it, insisting they each write their own. After another round of admiration and praise, they sat quietly to listen to Shanzhi's pipa and Chunxi's sheng. Shanzhi sat at an angle, her fingers striking the sandalwood frets like rain showering upon plantain leaves, the melody flowing like water over a river shoal. A chaotic, vibrant spring filled the hall, perfectly complemented by the rising and falling tones of the sheng. Every person listening felt their face light up with joy, a deep thrill running through their limbs. After they finished a suite of 'Moon Rises High', the room erupted once more into praise.`,`Once the instrumental performances concluded, it was time for Guibao's poetic riddles. Guibao asked, "Am I to present riddles for others to guess, or shall others present them for me?" Young Master Hua replied, "I will give you one to guess." He then recited offhand:

'Green ripples break into a shallow, uneven smile,
This year, the spring has arrived somewhat late.
I say the poetic inspiration at Bashang bridge feels thin,
It cannot compare to the time of the night boat at Red Cliff.'

Guibao thought for a moment and then laughed, "The Young Master speaks of the four classic themes: Wind, Flower, Snow, and Moon. It is truly well-crafted." Young Master Hua praised him, "What a sharp mind to guess it on the first try." Feng Zipei piped up, "I will tell one for you to guess: Before it is used, it is wrapped in a thousand layers; when it is used, its body is shattered and its bones are broken. Who knew that with a belly full of a black heart, it could still soar into the clouds?" Guibao laughed, "That is a firecracker." Young Master Hua teased, "Such an illogical riddle, and yet you expect people to guess it." Zipei countered, "How is it illogical?" Young Master Hua laughed, "A firecracker naturally must make a loud bang. What use is a mute firecracker that makes no sound?" The crowd erupted in laughter. Pincai then said, "I also have an illogical riddle to offer for your guidance. See if you can guess it." He recited:

'It shakes the earth and shocks the heavens, raging like thunder,
Once it departs, who knows when it will ever return?
Like heaving a great sigh upon the ancient battlefields,
When do the warriors of old ever return from war?'

Guibao laughed again, "That is also a firecracker." Zhang Zhongyu noted, "He was just mocking Zipei's riddle for being mute, which is why he made sure his very first line started with a loud noise." By now, the dawn breeze was drifting softly through the air, the morning bells were tolling, and the eastern sky was turning white. Young Master Hua urged Lanbao to perform his sword dance. Lanbao rolled up his double sleeves, drew a gleaming blade, and struck an opening stance. The movements were rhythmic and powerful, filling the room with blinding flashes of light, until at the very end, there was nothing but a streak of cold gleam, and the man himself seemed to vanish into the air. Everyone cheered wildly in admiration. By the time Lanbao finished his dance, the sky was flushed with the crimson light of dawn, and the morning sun was just about to rise. Today being the Mid-Autumn festival, everyone had their own affairs to attend to, and so they all rose to take their leave. Young Master Hua could not rightly detain them any longer, so he straightened his robes and prepared to see his guests out.`,"Ziyun and the others distributed assorted trinkets among the dans as rewards, and the guests all scattered their separate ways. Young Master Hua escorted them all the way out to the main vestibule before turning back. Only Feng Zipei, who was thoroughly exhausted, had already fallen fast asleep on a couch in the Quiet-Green Studio. Pincai also retired to his own room. Young Master Hua instructed the page boys to attend carefully to Feng Zipei, while he himself finally retired to the inner chambers.","The dans arranged to leave the city together, but we shall pause that thread for now.","The fifteenth day came and went. On the morning of the sixteenth, Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing brought Shi Nanxiang along to visit Ziyu. Since recovering from his illness in the middle of the seventh month, Ziyu had spent twenty-eight days resting and recuperating, and he had now grown strong and healthy once more.","Knowing that Qinyan had fallen into the Hua mansion and could not escape, Ziyu harbored a deep desire to see through the vanity of the mortal world. He completely completely withdrew from the world of song and dance, shutting himself away in a solitary room, finding his only companionship among tea bowls and incense burners. Though he claimed to have seen through worldly attachments, in truth, his emotional ties were far from severed; whenever his thoughts drifted to Qinyan, tears would stream down his face, a sorrow he could not ease. He took a brief walk to Wang Wenhui's home on the fifteenth, where he agreed to accompany Wang Xun and Zhongqing to visit Tian Chunhang and Gao Pin at noon the following day. Ziyu had already eaten breakfast and was waiting in his study. Soon enough, Shi, Yan, and the others arrived. Nanxiang took a seat and chatted with Ziyu, while Zhongqing and Wang Xun proceeded into the inner chambers to greet Madam Yan, sitting for a brief moment before returning. After drinking a cup of tea, they urged Ziyu to depart with them.","Outside, the carriage was already hitched. Ziyu did not even change his clothes, but Yun'er, fearing he might catch a chill, wrapped him in several layers of cotton garments. They boarded the carriage and headed straight for the residence of Chunhang and Gao Pin. Upon inquiring, they learned that both men had already returned to their quarters, so the group disembarked and walked straight inside. From within, they could hear Gao Pin's boisterous laughter mingled with the delicate, melodic voices of companions. Upon entering Chunhang's study, they found Su Huifang and Li Yulin inside. Seeing the four men arrive, Gao Pin and Chunhang were overjoyed and quickly offered them seats, while the two xianggong, Su and Li, also stepped forward to exchange greetings.",`After exchanging a few pleasantries, Zhongqing inquired about the civil service examinations. Both Chunhang and Gao Pin seemed quite pleased with their prospects. Zhongqing remarked, "I have already seen Xiangfan's essays, and I am certain of his success. Zhuoran, you must bring out your essays for us to read; I imagine they possess an excellence that towers above the rest." Gao Pin waved his hand dismissively, "No, they are poor, quite poor. There is no need to look at them." Wang Xun protested, "Nonsense! Even if they are poor, you must show them to us anyway." Nanxiang added, "With these three topics, Zhuoran is bound to shine. Even without seeing them, I know they will be fine." Ziyu chimed in, "Still, we should take a look. In my humble opinion, there are several different approaches one could take; some annotations are fitting to rely on, while others are not." Gao Pin confessed, "On that day, my mind was suddenly overcome by a dense fog, and I could not write a single character. When night fell, I just scribbled whatever came to mind, finished the exam paper, and considered the matter settled. Though I do have a draft of the first essay, I have no idea where I crumpled it up and tossed it." With that, he went into his room and managed to find it. The group read it over, noting that his poetry drafts were also attached. Nanxiang read halfway through and then stopped. Wang Xun remarked, "The composition is incredibly superb, though it is perhaps a bit short. It looks to be no more than four hundred words." Ziyu observed, "The brushwork is mature and the tone is remarkably elevated; such quality is truly rare in the examination halls." Gao Pin nodded at Ziyu and said, "Yuxiang truly has a discerning eye." Zhongqing pressed, "Zhuoran, based on your own judgment, how do you rate this essay? Give us your honest verdict." Gao Pin replied, "You could call it good, or you could call it bad. Regardless, the examination halls do not judge solely on literary merit; passing is no mere fluke, and failing is no great injustice." Zhongqing then turned to Nanxiang and asked, "How do you rate Xiangfan's chances?" Nanxiang answered, "I believe Xiangfan will undoubtedly top the list. As for Zhuoran, he might run into a careless examiner, or he might just unexpectedly take first place—one simply cannot say." Zhongqing shook his head in silence. Gao Pin snatched the manuscript back, tore it to pieces, and declared, "Success or failure is preordained; there is no point in debating it. Let us talk of other things. One way or another, I will pass this exam just to prove it to you." The group fell silent, realizing that Gao Pin was genuinely vexed, and quickly changed the subject to idle chatter.`,`Huifang then recounted the events of the previous evening at the Hua mansion, detailing how they had composed poems and painted scenes. The group listened with great delight, asking them to recite the poems aloud. Everyone offered their praise, singling out Yulin's lyric as the most masterfully crafted. Gao Pin remarked, "Beneath a strong general, there are no weak troops. Looking at Peixian's opening lyric, could those hack scholars in their square caps outside ever produce such work?" Ziyu agreed, "Indeed. Even Young Master Hua's edits were brilliantly done." He then asked a few questions about Qinyan, and Yulin and Huifang recounted the details of his quiet departure. Upon hearing this, Ziyu fell into a frozen daze. Suddenly, Gao Pin's page boy entered to summon him, announcing that a guest had arrived to see him. Gao Pin hurriedly left the room and did not return for a very long time. Nanxiang asked, "Who could be talking for so long?" Chunhang explained, "Zhuoran has been acting rather odd lately. He receives an endless stream of visitors, but they are not his usual acquaintances; they are all dreadfully vulgar and unrefined people. The other day, my page boy saw his servant bring in several packets of silver. When asked about it, he claimed he didn't know who sent them." Zhongqing connected the dots: "Ah, that explains it. Zhuoran is completely broke, so he naturally has to resort to the business of selling his essays. That explains why his exam piece was so hastily scribbled; why else would he rush it like that?" Nanxiang nodded in agreement, "Exactly. If you listen to him say, 'I will pass this exam just to prove it to you,' the truth of the matter becomes perfectly clear." Just then, Gao Pin returned after seeing his guest off, and everyone abruptly ceased their chatter.`,`Huifang broke the silence, "It is truly a rare occasion that all you gentlemen happen to be gathered here. How about I play the host today and invite you all to a feast? What do you say?" Wang Xun agreed, "That sounds excellent." Gao Pin teased, "Xianggong, you aren't going to hit us up for subscription funds, are you?" Huifang laughed, "You caught me! I truly am seeking subscriptions." They all thought he was merely jesting and readily agreed. Huifang ordered a servant to arrange for a grand feast to be brought over from a nearby restaurant. The servants helped set everything out, and Huifang ceremoniously seated his guests. The group expressed their surprise, "Meixiang, you are acting so solemnly today. Are you genuinely asking for subscriptions?" Huifang replied, "I warned you earlier. When have you ever known a xianggong's wine to come for free?" The room erupted in laughter as they took their seats. Gao Pin remarked, "It is only a pity that we are missing one guest." When Huifang asked who was missing, Gao Pin replied, "Today, we really should not be without Pan San." Huifang spat in mock disgust, then poured several rounds of wine, with Yulin helping to serve the drinks and dishes.`,`Huifang then drew several sheets of paper from his boot, which looked like a formal essay, and handed them to the guest of honor, Shi Nanxiang, saying, "Master Zhujun, this is the very reason I am asking for subscriptions today. Please read it, and then I shall explain." Confused, the others crowded around to look. The title read: 'Biography of Master Fragrant Snow'. Huifang then instructed his servant to bring in a small bundle. He untied it and presented the contents: it was the 'Posthumous Writings of Fragrant Snow', consisting of two volumes of intertwined poetry and prose. Nanxiang read the biography aloud, line by line. Only when he finished did they realize that this man was Huifang's former teacher, who had instructed him in both letters and opera. He had originally been a scholar, but after failing the examinations, he burned his brushes and inkstones and joined a theatrical troupe to teach opera. He had produced a wealth of magnificent writings throughout his life. When Huifang first arrived in the capital, he sought him out and was treated like his own flesh and blood; every skill and art he possessed was taught to him by this master. The master had passed away on a certain date, and his coffin, belonging to a traveler, had no home to return to, so it was temporarily lodged at the Shoufo Temple in the southern part of the city. Now, his widow and young son had traveled to the capital in search of him, enduring endless hardships along the way, only to discover upon their arrival that Fragrant-Snow had been dead for many years. When Huifang learned of this, he emptied his purse to assist them, providing two hundred taels of silver. Beyond covering their travel expenses, this was enough to help manage their household. He had also beseeched Xiao Cixian to paint a portrait and gather poems in his honor. The master's son, Yuanzuo, was thirteen years old. Though he was too poor to attend school, he was blessed with brilliant natural talent and an extraordinary memory; he never forgot anything he heard. Whenever he lingered near a schoolhouse to listen to the lessons, he memorized them perfectly, and had already learned half of the Five Classics by heart. Huifang's intention was to implore the famous scholars gathered here to write poems, tomb inscriptions, or biographies in order to honor the master's hidden virtues, bring his unacknowledged brilliance to light, cover his past transgressions, and enrich the future of his descendants. As the group read the texts, Huifang elaborated on the story, and when he reached the most heartbreaking parts, he could not help but weep softly. The listeners were deeply moved by the tale. They all stood up in unison and declared, "Such profound righteousness is rarely seen in people today. We shall gladly wash our hands and respectfully write pieces to praise even a fraction of his merit. With such an outstanding pupil and so bright a son, even though Fragrant-Snow died in obscurity, he surely harbors no lingering regrets." Chunhang and Ziyu were particularly vocal in their unceasing praise. Nanxiang asked, "Did you write this biography yourself?" Huifang replied, "It contains only the raw facts; it lacks literary polish." Zhongqing suggested, "It is quite good, though having Xiangfan refine it would make it perfect." He then added, "I shall compose an elegy for him." Wang Xun volunteered, "I shall write a few elegiac poems." Nanxiang declared, "I shall compose his tomb inscription." Chunhang proposed, "We can expand upon his original draft, and I will write a completely new biography. How does that sound?" Huifang replied, "That would be even better; this draft was merely an outline and cannot be used properly." Ziyu lamented, "You have all claimed the major literary pieces; what is left for me to write? I suppose I can only compose a piece of praise." Gao Pin said, "A piece of praise is wonderful, but I shall write a funeral ode, as the tone is far more heavy and mournful." Zhongqing then suggested, "Why don't we gather all of his former disciples, and when twilight falls and the streets are quiet, we can go and offer a sacrifice to him, and also pool together more funds for their journey home. What do you say?" Chunhang and the others wholeheartedly agreed that this was a brilliant idea. Huifang immediately fell to his knees and kowtowed in deep gratitude, startling the gentlemen, who quickly rushed to help him up. From that moment on, everyone treated Huifang with the respect due to a solemn and righteous friend, refraining entirely from their usual lighthearted jesting. Nanxiang asked, "On what day will the soul depart?" Huifang replied, "At the midnight hour on the thirtieth, during the deep night of the twenty-ninth." Nanxiang said, "Then we must complete these essays as soon as possible. We will have them carved into a printed collection, print dozens of copies, and give them to him to take home. As for the subscription funds, let each man contribute according to his means. Perhaps we can also ask Du Xiang, Jingyi, and Qianzhou to contribute a share as well.`,`We shall gather everyone on the night of the twenty-ninth, at the second watch, and proceed there to perform a single sacrificial rite to conclude the matter. As for the rest of his disciples, Meixiang, you may arrange that yourself." Everyone voiced their agreement: "Excellent." Huifang, however, protested, "The sacrifice is entirely unnecessary, and I truly dare not accept such an honor. Furthermore, the temple is cramped and has barely any room to accommodate everyone. The gift of your calligraphy is already a tremendous glory; how could I dare expect a sacrificial ceremony as well? Besides, there are many gossiping eyes outside, and I fear this might bring unwarranted criticism upon all of you." Nanxiang dismissed his concerns, "There is no harm in it. He was also a man of the scholarly circles, and people are aware of that. Let us decide when the day draws nearer. Looking at Xiangfan, it seems he cannot do without this gesture, and there is no harm in the rest of us following his lead." Moved by Huifang's plea, which stirred deep feelings of pity and melancholy within them, the group found little joy in further merriment. By the time the first watch arrived, they quietly dispersed.`,'The following day, Nanxiang and Zhongqing wrote letters to Ziyun, Qianzhou, and several others. A few days later, the subscription funds arrived, along with several verses and songs. Nanxiang and Zhongqing tallied the contributions: Ziyun gave twenty-four taels, Wenze gave sixteen, and Cixian gave twelve, bringing the total to fifty-two taels. Zhongqing remarked, "There are six of us sharing the burden, so at eight taels each, we can easily round it up to a hundred taels, which will be more than enough." Nanxiang agreed, "It is plenty." They sent out more letters, and within a few days, all the funds were pooled together. The poems, essays, and posthumous works had already been successfully carved into blocks, and one hundred copies were printed at a cost of sixty taels, all of which Huifang paid for entirely on his own.',"Among the pupils in the flower-register who had studied under Fragrant-Snow, four still remained: Yuan Baozhu, Wang Guibao, Jin Shufang, and Lu Sulan. Whether they had studied painting or poetry, all were his advanced disciples. These four pupils pooled together an additional one hundred taels. Combined with Huifang's contribution, the total came to four hundred taels. This generous sum would easily provide the mother, son, and their old servant with ample funds to hire a boat and travel comfortably back home via the canal.",`On the twenty-eighth, Zhongqing visited Nanxiang to discuss the plans for the following day. He noted, "I imagine there are a few who will be unwilling to go. Yong'an is always timid and fearful, constantly worrying that his strict father might find out. As for Yuxiang, it goes without saying; we certainly cannot ask him to go out to a ruined ancient temple in the middle of the night." Nanxiang offered a solution: "I've actually thought of an idea. Since we are undertaking this endeavor, we need not do it solely for the sake of the sacrifice. We can use it as a pretext for a stroll in the countryside. Why don't we bring wine and go during the day? After offering our libations, we can head over to Brook Autumn Mound and the Haoran Pavilion to spend some time chatting with the xianggong. Wouldn't that be wonderful?" Zhongqing agreed, "That is an excellent idea; I hadn't thought of that. If Yong'an and Yuxiang cannot join us, the four of us will suffice." They then proceeded together to Chunhang's residence to finalize the arrangements, instructing him to prepare food and wine and to wait for them there at noon.`,"The next morning, Nanxiang and Zhongqing rode out of the city on horseback. When they arrived at the gates of the Shoufo Temple, they dismounted and had the grooms tie their horses to the side. They noticed five or six carriages already parked there. Stepping through the gates, they found the ancient temple incredibly desolate. The grass grew knee-deep, mules grazed freely, the walls and wells were broken and crumbling, and the statues of the Buddha were tilted and in disrepair.",`They entered the Amitabha Hall, but saw no one. Looking toward the Great Shrine Hall, they saw that the western corner had completely collapsed. The wind rustled violently through the trees, piling dead leaves in great heaps, creating a scene of absolute desolation. Finally, a man emerged from behind the hall. Zhongqing recognized him as one of Huifang's servants. Upon seeing them, the servant stood respectfully with his hands by his sides. Zhongqing asked, "Where are they?" The servant replied, "They are in the back. Please allow me to lead the way." They walked past the main hall and through a western gate, arriving at a dangerously dilapidated loft that lacked both doors and windows. Passing through there, they found three small huts packed tight with coffins—perhaps twenty or thirty in total. In front of one particular coffin stood a small table, lit with incense and candles. They deduced that this must be the one.`,"In the eastern courtyard, they passed through another small door into a space with three or four small rooms. Chunhang, Gao Pin, Huifang, and their party were inside, accompanied by an old monk. Chunhang and Huifang stepped out to greet them.",'Nanxiang remarked, "What a grim and dreadful place this is. It is no wonder some refused to come." Huifang hurriedly dragged a wooden bench over and invited them to sit. Zhongqing said, "Now that everyone is here, let us pour our libations and then head straight over to Brook Autumn Mound for our stroll." Huifang immediately ordered the sacrificial feast to be laid out in the room. Huifang offered the incense, Sulan poured the libations of wine, Shufang held the pitcher, Baozhu arranged the dishes, and Guibao burned the paper offerings. Chunhang, Nanxiang, and Gao Pin bowed respectfully in unison, while the five dans kowtowed repeatedly in solemn gratitude.',"Feeling utterly restless in the oppressive gloom, they hurriedly ordered their servants to pack everything up, handed the old monk a string of cash, and swiftly left the temple grounds. Nanxiang and Zhongqing remounted their horses, while the rest boarded their carriages. With the porters carrying their loads on poles, they headed straight for Brook Autumn Mound. The sparse woods and yellowing leaves painted a picture of absolute bleakness and desolation.",`After traveling a little over a li, they arrived at the foot of the mound. It stood tall and imposing, resembling a small mountain, with a Buddhist temple perched upon its slopes. At the very summit stood a grand structure named the Haoran Pavilion. Looking out from its vantage point, they could see the city walls and surrounding villages stretching for dozens of li in every direction—a truly captivating view. Chunhang sighed, "Witnessing such desolation today has left my heart heavy with sorrow. But arriving here, I feel my vision suddenly clear and expand." Gao Pin remarked, "I have actually never been to Brook Autumn Mound before. I assume Zhujun has visited here in the past?" Nanxiang replied, "This is my first time as well. I only knew of it because I chanced upon an ancient poem titled 'Inscription on Jinqiu Mound' a few days ago. It is quite a far journey; who would ever come all the way out here?" Zhongqing noted, "It is a pleasant change, actually. When one spends every day amidst the noise and bustle, it is necessary to seek out the quiet and cold now and then." Nanxiang teased, "The Shoufo Temple was certainly cold enough! Jiantan, you once said that only those with pure hearts can pierce the silence, and only those with detached wills can probe the depths of the unknown. Tell me, would you dare to lodge in that temple for a few nights?" Zhongqing laughed, "If I were forced into such a position, I would have no choice but to lodge there. On a night when the moon is bright and the wind is calm, perhaps some fox spirits or ghosts might come by for a visit—that wouldn't be so bad." Gao Pin remarked, "Jiantan always delights in contrary talk." Sulan shivered, "If I were alone, I wouldn't dare set foot in there even in broad daylight." Guibao added, "There are so many broken and rotting coffins in there. I am certain the place must be haunted at night." Shufang reasoned, "It is amazing that the monk, with only one novice and an altar attendant, is entirely unafraid. If there truly were ghosts, how could the monk remain so perfectly unharmed?" Huifang chimed in, "When have you ever seen a ghost eat a man? Just the other day, I heard the monk say that whenever the wind howls and the rain is dark, or in the deep hours of the night, strange cries and weeping are a common occurrence." Baozhu asked, "Have any of you heard about the ghost haunting the Yiyuan garden?" Huifang shook his head, "No." Sulan asked eagerly, "What kind of ghost is it?" Baozhu recounted, "There was a page boy named Chunxi in the Osmanthus Hall who had a great fondness for fruit. Whenever there was a banquet or a flower-viewing party, he would gather the leftover fruit and hide it in a large jar. One night, a large monkey discovered his stash and came to steal the fruit. Chunxi was asleep, but he heard the loud clatter of fruit scattering across the floor. He called out, but received no answer. He took his lamp and went out to look, but could see nothing. He went back to sleep, only to hear the noise again. When he came out to investigate a second time, the monkey had hidden itself inside a large warming brazier.`,`"Chunxi grabbed a knife and inadvertently walked right up to the brazier. The monkey panicked, leaped up with the brazier resting on its shoulders, and bolted out the door. In the chaos, Chunxi's lamp went out, he dropped his knife, and he began howling about ghosts like a madman. Qing'er, who lived across the way, heard the commotion and ran out, only to crash right into the fleeing monkey. Feeling something huge and furry, he was knocked flat on his back. The noise woke many people, but when they arrived, all they found was a large brazier lying on the floor, leaving everyone completely puzzled. Chunxi swore he had seen a yellow-haired ghost over five feet tall, while Qing'er insisted it was a green-faced, sharp-fanged demon that had slapped him right across the face! They debated the matter for two whole days. It wasn't until dusk on the third day, when they saw the monkey sneaking back in to steal more fruit, that the truth was finally revealed. If they hadn't discovered it, the rumors would have reached the masters' ears for certain!" The group erupted in laughter. Huifang had prepared two tables of vegetarian dishes and four kinds of snacks, which they cooked in the temple's borrowed kitchen. The nine of them spread out cushions on the ground and sat in a circle. Chunhang and Huifang had been friends for half a year, developing a bond rooted in deep mutual respect. Seeing Huifang's incredible generosity today, and witnessing his profound loyalty to his past, Chunhang revered him even more. This led Chunhang to recall his own desperate circumstances from the previous autumn and spring, when he had been utterly destitute and on the verge of dying in a ditch. Had it not been for Huifang's help, his brilliant talents would have amounted to nothing, and he never would have survived to see this day. Facing the bleak, withering west wind, surrounded by the desolate autumn scenery, with misty rain threatening to fall and dark clouds hanging heavy in the sky, a profound grief welled up from within him, and tears began to stream down his face. The others were completely puzzled by his sudden weeping, but Huifang, guessing the reason, soon found his own eyes brimming with tears. When Baozhu and the others asked what was wrong, Huifang could no longer hold back his sorrow. Remembering his own days of hardship, and realizing that without Fragrant-Snow's guidance, he surely would have perished ten times over, he broke down and wailed aloud, his cries piercing the hearts of everyone present.`,'Feeling a sudden, overwhelming sickness in his heart, Nanxiang downed a massive bowl of wine and let out a long, howling cry toward the distant mountains. The wind rose from all sides, violently shaking the trees. Gao Pin remarked, "Look at you all, some weeping and others laughing. Every one of you harbors such heavy burdens in your chest. I, Gao Zhuoran, am the only one with a chest utterly empty, like a citizen of the legendary gutless nation.',`"Sun Deng's famous whistling was merely an expression of frantic eccentricity, and Ruan Ji's deep sorrow was just a draining of his spirit. When the ancients climbed high places and composed their prose, they bound their grief to their words. Why don't we improvise some verses today to ease our minds?" Nanxiang agreed, "A fine idea! You start us off." Gao Pin declared, "For something truly stirring and mournful, nothing suits better than composing to the tune of 'Congratulations on the New Cool'. I have the opening lines ready right here." He then chanted aloud:

'Do you know the ways of the world or not?
Through the ages, seas turn to mulberry fields,
One cannot bear to cast a glance back.'

Gao Pin continued:

'Only the poets preserve their bright inspiration,
Singing wild songs day after day, cup of wine in hand.'

Shi Nanxiang followed:

'Autumn stands solemn amidst shredded clouds and ruined willows.`,`'Testing our horses on the border moors, gazing idly out.'

Yan Zhongqing added:

'I ask the Golden Terrace,
Do you still long for the tread of the kirin?
The souls have departed,
Who is left to stand guard?'`,`Tian Chunhang sighed:

'For so long, I have drifted aimlessly at the world's edge.
Sharing the dawn and dusk,
Over a chessboard and tea bowls,
With but two or three true friends.'

Gao Pin declared:

'The dead have been gone for a thousand autumns,
What is the use of speaking of undying fame?'

Shi Nanxiang mourned:

'Grief piles up in my chest as heavy as a mountain.
Singing by the autumn graves, letting our verses serve as tears.'

Yan Zhongqing concluded:

'Listen to the wailing that shatters the Qin man's earthen jar.
Let us drink our fill,
And pay no heed to the call of dawn.'

Tian Chunhang finished the verse. They chanted it aloud together, breaking into roaring laughter. As the rain threatened to pour, they lost all desire to linger and hurriedly packed up their belongings. Nanxiang caught a ride in Huifang's carriage, while Zhongqing rode in Sulan's, and they journeyed back home. By the time they arrived, a fine, scattered drizzle had begun to fall. To know what happened next, you must listen to the following chapter.`]},l=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations31:e},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),t={32:[`To tell the story, the autumn rain fell continuously, turning the roads to mud. It rained for seven or eight days straight, and only cleared up on the eighth day. The examination compound was scheduled to post the results on the tenth, but the announcements started early on the morning of the ninth. All the candidates who had taken the exams were restless; during these days, they lost their appetite and sleep. Even Gao Pin and Chunhang were no exception. On the night of the eighth, Chunhang went to bed too early and couldn't sleep. He got up again and went to Gao Pin's room, finding him still awake, and the two began to share their thoughts. Chunhang sighed and said, 'I've always cared little for fame, so it doesn't matter much whether I pass or not, but I'd feel I've let Su Meixiang down, wasting half a year of his expectations. Although exam degrees aren't the most precious thing, throughout history, famous scholars and talented men have almost without exception started their careers this way.' Gao Pin said, 'It's hateful that this year's examiners have strictly avoided everyone. If we don't pass this time, it will be too depressing. If there's no news by this time tomorrow, we can give up hope.' Chunhang said, 'We won't have to wait until this time tomorrow; if no one comes by lighting time, all hope is gone. But judging by the two hexagrams we cast the day before yesterday, it seems both of us have a chance.' Gao Pin said, 'Divining your fortune in an exam year is the most unreliable thing. The first time I took the exams in Jiangning, someone cast a Plum Blossom hexagram for me and got the fifth line of the Tai hexagram. He said I didn't even need to ask, I was guaranteed to place first. The text for that line is "King Wen marries off his sister; there is blessing and supreme good fortune", what more could you say? And the commentary even said "He fulfills his wishes perfectly." Chunhang said, 'Exactly!' Gao Pin said, 'Not only that, but that year was the year of Yiwei. Think about it: the character "Yi" from King Wen's name, and the character "Mei" (sister) without the woman radical, doesn't that make the characters "Yiwei"? I was ninety percent sure I'd pass, but who knew spirits only know how to trick people? Don't you think it's ridiculous?' Chunhang said, 'The human mind is the most perceptive. Whatever the heart desires, the signs reflect it. This was a sign arising from your own mind, not a sign from the hexagram itself.' The two brewed tea and chatted, only going to sleep near the fifth watch, and got up as soon as it was dawn.`,"Meanwhile, Su Huifang was worrying about Chunhang and spent a sleepless night. By the time he rose, it was already mid-morning. He hurriedly washed and dressed, and sent someone outside to inquire if any announcements had been made. The person left. Afterwards, a capital official sent someone to ask Huifang to accompany him for a double-ninth festival outing. Huifang, having no mood for it, declined and said he had gone into the city. After a good while, the clock struck noon, and the person sent to inquire returned, saying: 'Outside, over forty names have already been announced. Master Tian's *Title Register*'s name is not yet among them, but that man named Gui has passed in thirty-fourth place.' Huifang asked, 'Which man named Gui?' The servant replied, 'The one who lives outside the alley, Master Ye's son-in-law, who runs the brothels.' Hearing this, Huifang felt quite indignant and said: 'How bizarre! Even a pimp has passed, what is the world coming to? Looking at it this way, there's no need to speak further.' In his heart, he wanted to go over to Chunhang's place, but feared it would be too awkward to face him. He thought of waiting for the results before going, but his heart was burning with anxiety, perhaps even more so than Chunhang's. A while later, Baozhu sent someone to ask for news, and then Sulan and Yulin also sent people to inquire, making Huifang so restless he could neither sit nor stand. He wanted to go to the theater but feared getting entangled with someone, so he lay sullenly on the kang, picked up a leisurely book to distract himself, read two pages, and put it down again.","Nearing the middle of the afternoon, there was still no news. Feeling bored and restless, they suddenly saw a servant walk in carrying a box in his hands, upon which sat a plate of date cake. He came in and said, 'Tailor Hu sent this, and has something to ask you in person.' Huifang said, 'What does he have to say? Since he delivered it himself, just accept it.' Tailor Hu then walked in and bowed. Huifang offered him a seat. Tailor Hu said, 'You're free at home today, not going out for a walk? The double-ninth outings outside are quite lively. It's also the day the provincial graduates are announced. Third Master Pan's son-in-law has passed, and it's so lively; the shop is packed with people, and they gave out a hundred strings of cash as reward. A family outside the alley here also passed, I often make clothes for him. Master Gao staying at Hongji Temple also passed in eighty-first place. Over a hundred names have already been announced outside the city.' Hearing this, Huifang hurriedly asked, 'Master Gao at Hongji Temple passed? There is also a Master Tian's *Title Register* staying in the temple, did he pass?' Tailor Hu said, 'I haven't heard, but he probably passed too.' He then turned to Huifang and said, 'My Master Su, I have a favor to ask of you: my third son is named Sanxi. He's idling around the shop. I tried to teach him the trade, but after three or four months he can't even hold the scissors, and he asks for forty or fifty coppers a day to buy candy and fruit. How can I afford to keep him? His appearance is quite clean; although he cannot compare to the young men in your troupe, he is certainly not bad. He also has a clever mind; he can't learn needlework, but he can learn acting. He's listened to so much Luantan opera that he can sing quite a bit. I think acting is ten thousand times better than being a tailor. I beg you, sir, to do a good deed and give me a hand. Pick a day for Sanxi to come and formally take you as his master. Please, sir, do not refuse. If I send him to another troupe, I'd feel bad because he's still young, and the child wouldn't be able to bear the beatings and scoldings.","You old folks have a kind heart, pitying the child. In the future, I don't expect him to be like you old folks, able to bring glory to the ancestors, dressing brightly, with plenty to eat and wear, and knowing a few wealthy patrons. That alone would be enough to satisfy me. What good is being a tailor? You have no capital of your own, you buy materials on credit from the shop so the cost is high, and you have to advance money for people. When you send out the bill, people complain it's too expensive. After all the discounts and deductions, it just drags on in debt. This shop is barely keeping open; with five or seven apprentices working, eating plain rice and sour vegetable soup, the daily expenses still come to two strings of cash. How much profit can there be?",`If you are willing to take him as an apprentice, in a couple of years I won't be a tailor anymore, and I'll be just like an old master.' Huifang was extremely annoyed hearing this, and said, 'I'm about to change professions and stop singing operas, why would I take on an apprentice? Besides, I don't know how to teach people. If your son wants to learn opera, he's better off going to a Luantan troupe; he could be on stage in two months. We who sing Kunqu opera study our whole lives and still don't get people to say a good word about us. We spend so much money a month just to buy a few operas, what's the point of him learning that?' Tailor Hu continued to chatter on, and only left after a good while. It was already time to light the lamps. Huifang let out a long sigh and couldn't resist calling for a carriage to go to Chunhang's place, first to congratulate Gao Pin. When he arrived at Hongji Temple, it was quiet and desolate. He went in and first saw Gao Pin's servant, and asked him. The servant replied, 'An announcement did come just now, but our master says he's afraid it's a mistake, we don't know why.' Huifang walked inside and saw Gao Pin and Chunhang sitting across from each other playing chess. They invited him to sit. Chunhang's mind was triggered, and he swept the chess pieces aside, saying, 'I lose, no need to keep playing.' Gao Pin also stopped. Huifang asked, 'Zhuoran has already placed high in the exams, why are you both acting like this?' Gao Pin laughed, 'How should we act when we pass? Let's wait until Xiangfan's announcement comes to celebrate.' Huifang said, 'It's all the same, both of you are definitely passing.' Gao Pin said, 'The announcement did come just now, but it doesn't quite match up; it says it's a student from the Imperial Academy from Jiangnan.' Huifang said, 'In my view, it's not a mistake. There's unlikely to be someone else with your exact name.' Gao Pin said, 'It's hard to say. We have to look at the actual posted list to be sure.' Chunhang remained silent, and Huifang could only offer words of comfort. A moment later, Shi Nanxiang and Yan Zhongqing burst in. Nanxiang said, 'Here come the bearers of glad tidings, quick, prepare the celebration wine! Meixiang, are you here too?' Chunhang said, 'By now almost all the names must be announced; there won't be time to prepare anything, what is there to celebrate?' Zhongqing said, 'Only about a hundred and eighty names have been announced so far. Zhuoran has passed in eighty-first place. Do you think that's too low, and feel a bit wronged by it?' Gao Pin said, 'I fear it's not me. Didn't you see the slip says "Jiangnan Imperial Academy Student"?' Nanxiang and Zhongqing said together, 'That's a clerical error, it happens all the time.' Chunhang said, 'Don't doubt it, Zhuoran has definitely passed.' Nanxiang said to Gao Pin, 'You should get dinner ready. We'll eat while we wait. If the announcement doesn't come, how about we all go see the results board after the third watch? When everyone has passed, the two of you must treat us to ten days of feasting.' Before the two could answer, Huifang said, 'Rightly said, rightly said! Let's do that; I'm getting a bit hungry too.' Gao Pin and Chunhang knew that someone would surely come with the news today, and had already made arrangements; they cleared the table and brought out the food. Nanxiang wouldn't let them eat right away; he insisted they drink with him first. Though Gao Pin voiced doubts, he was already overjoyed inside, and drank with open-hearted cheer. Chunhang was usually easygoing, but this time he couldn't relax; Huifang felt the same. Nanxiang said, 'Don't worry, Xiangfan will definitely be among the top five. If he isn't fourth or fifth, I wouldn't dare claim any understanding of literature. That year in Hubei when I had my stroke of luck, I gathered a few friends and laid out a grand banquet to wait for the news. We waited until the third watch and nothing came; we were furious. Those people thought it looked hopeless and left. It was only near the fourth watch, just as we were about to sleep, that the news finally came; to my great fortune, I was the top candidate! You've both sat for exams two or three times; don't you know the top five are filled in last?' Zhongqing said, 'Wasn't I fooled by the news-bringers last time? I was the top candidate on the supplementary list, but they announced I was the second overall candidate [Nanyuan]. I tipped them generously, and the next morning they simply didn't show up. Only when I saw the board did I realize it was merely the supplementary list, leaving me to realize I had rejoiced over nothing for half the night.' They chatted and drank, and after the second watch there was still no news. Even Shi and Yan began to feel that Chunhang's chances were looking shaky.`,"They were just about to eat when they suddenly heard a clamor of voices bursting in from outside, giving everyone a fright.","They heard someone shouting, 'Great joy to Master Tian's *Title Register*! He has passed as Nanyuan (second overall candidate).' Hearing this, Chunhang was overjoyed beyond words; he threw his chopsticks aside and hurried from his seat, and Huifang too was ecstatic. Everyone was delighted and quickly looked at the slip: 'Successful Candidate, Second Place: Tian Chunhang, age twenty-three, from Shangyuan County, Jiangnan, Attached Tribute Student.' Only then did they relax. The news-bringers asked for their reward; Huifang had brought some banknotes and handed them to Chunhang. Chunhang first gave them ten strings of cash, saying, 'Come tomorrow morning with the people who brought the news for Master Gao, and you can collect your reward together.' The others said, 'Tomorrow the two masters won't be giving out just ten or twenty strings; they'll be giving generous rewards of hundreds of strings.' Gao Pin said, 'That's right, come tomorrow.' Chunhang was so happy he was in a daze, but Gao Pin couldn't put his mind at rest, suspecting the news-bringers might be deceiving him, and just sat there blankly. Huifang laughed, 'Over two hundred names have been announced already. If everyone is suspicious, is it all just a lie?' Zhongqing said, 'There's no need to doubt. It's already the third watch, the city gates are open. Have your servant take a fast horse and check the board first. We won't go back either; why don't you just order more wine?' Chunhang and Gao Pin said, 'Very well.' So they sent someone to check the board, while bringing out more wine and food.",`Now they all drank freely; joy was abundant and worry was scarce. They played finger-guessing games and drank until after the fifth watch, when the man sent to check the board finally returned, saying, 'Master Tian's *Title Register* is indeed correct; he is the second name on the board.' This sentence stunned Gao Pin, who urgently asked, 'What about me?' The man replied, 'The eighty-first name is someone called Gao Pinsan, forty years old, from Shanyang County, Huai'an Prefecture, Jiangnan, Imperial Academy Student.' Gao Pin was so furious he almost fainted, spitting out a 'Bah!' The man then took out a copy of the List of Successful Candidates. They all looked closely, and indeed, Gao Pin's name was nowhere to be found. Huifang laughed, 'I don't know the others who passed, but I do know these two: one is Ye Maolin's son-in-law, known as "Brothel Gui", and this thirty-fourth name, Gui Zirong, is him. The other is Pan the Third's son-in-law, called "Palanquin Hua". His father is Hua the Third, nicknamed "The Beard", who started out carrying biers in a funeral parlor, then made a fortune and opened several parlors himself. This sixty-third name Hua Zhonggui is him.' Gao Pin read through the first sheet of the list again, a slight look of pleasure crossing his face, and then he couldn't help sighing: 'Ah well, fame and fortune are predestined. Since we are all friends here, I will speak plainly. I sold a provincial graduate degree for one thousand six hundred taels of silver. This "Palanquin Hua" is the one I took the exam for. Zhang Zhongyu was the middleman, and tomorrow I will go and collect the money.' Chunhang, Nanxiang, Zhongqing, and Huifang all scolded him. Gao Pin said, 'I know this shouldn't be done, but I had a thought: either I wouldn't pass this time, or I might actually achieve both fame and fortune. Besides, I needed to take a trip back south; I have several urgent family matters to attend to, and being completely broke would be unbearable. Now it's fine. Although I can't have the honor of being Xiangfan's examination classmate, at least I don't have a funeral-bier carrier for a senior generation "uncle", or call a prostitute my "aunt". This is a blessing in disguise. I see that Xiangfan has not only gained this uncle and aunt, but also an excellent father-in-law.' Chunhang laughed, 'Say what cutting things you will. But what examination year doesn't have a few scoundrels passing? How do you know you won't be classmates with such people next time? As for this "father-in-law" title, who might that be?' Huifang chuckled. Zhongqing said, 'Didn't you hear just now? The funeral-bier carrier's son, Hua Zhonggui, is Pan the Silversmith's son-in-law. Going by the relationship between examination classmates, isn't he your father-in-law?' Chunhang laughed, 'I won't associate with him as a classmate. I'll just recognize Zhuoran as my classmate.' Gao Pin laughed, 'In that case, tomorrow I'll just call Pan the Third "father-in-law", how's that?' This made everyone roar with laughter.`,"Shortly after, the sky grew completely light and the red sun rose. Chunhang needed to go out to pay his respects to his examining master and the chief examiner, and everyone dispersed. Afterwards, there were meetings with classmates and celebratory banquets, keeping them busy for half a month.",`Chunhang had been passed by the fourth-room examiner, Sun Lianggong. Upon their meeting, Lianggong was extremely pleased that he had placed Nanyuan. He had long heard of Chunhang's reputation, as even Ministers Liu and Wang, though they hadn't met Chunhang, had seen his writing and calligraphy in their sons' studies and greatly admired him, regretting only that they had not secured him themselves. From then on, Chunhang formed a generational friendship with Wenze and Wang Xun, growing even closer. Only the Sun brothers were difficult to get along with, though they had to maintain some interaction, keeping it purely formal. Gao Pin collected the silver for taking the exam as a substitute, receiving a total of one thousand six hundred taels. Zhang Zhongyu, as the middleman, had negotiated two thousand four hundred taels with the Hua family, taking an eight-hundred-tael cut and also demanding a two-hundred-tael "thank you" fee from the Hua family, thus netting a thousand taels for himself to enjoy. He then bought a regular command post through his provincial registry title and took up duties in the local office. Gao Pin returned to Suzhou on the second of October. Feeling lonely in the temple, Chunhang was invited by Wenze to his home, and Wang Xun also wanted him to stay; Chunhang often slept over at both places. With Huifang still keeping him company as usual, he felt content and happy, studying and discussing with Wenze, Zhongqing, and the others, writing poetry and prose in his spare time, and preparing for the Palace Examination. Nanxiang also practiced writing Palace Examination essays for a few days, but then stopped; we will leave that aside for now.`,"Now we must recount a separate incident. On the night of the fourteenth of August, Wu the Big Fool was summoned to the Board of Punishments and questioned regarding the forging of a deed to mortgage for money. Gui Zirong, urgently needing to borrow money, had consulted with the Big Fool to use the latter's house deed as collateral, promising to share the proceeds. The Big Fool had long since mortgaged the deed, so he had to forge a new one for Gui Zirong. They borrowed six hundred strings of cash, of which the Big Fool took two hundred. Unbeknownst to them, this moneylender was good friends with the previous one. One day, while discussing accounts, they mentioned Wu the Big Fool's house. The first moneylender asked about the address and orientation, realizing it was the very property mortgaged to him, and said, 'That deed is fake. The Big Fool mortgaged the house to me the year before last for eight hundred strings, with Xingsheng Candle Shop as guarantor. He's currently four months behind on the interest, and I was just about to go have a word with him. How could he mortgage it to you again?' The second man grew anxious and quickly found his guarantor to confront the Big Fool. But the Big Fool spent his days wandering in a daze around the theaters, and the clumsy servant at his home was clueless. The man searched for over ten days without catching a glimpse of him, and the Big Fool returned home completely unaware. In desperation, the man filed a lawsuit at the Board of Punishments. Wu the Big Fool was a student of astronomy; his grandfather had been an official, and his great-uncle a prominent dignitary. But the family had declined, leaving only a few qing of barren land and some dilapidated rooms. Fortunately, the deed was a 'white deed' (unofficial), and he hadn't forged an official seal. The Big Fool's maternal uncle was currently a department director at the Board of Punishments and offered some protection. The Big Fool wanted to implicate Gui Zirong, but unfortunately the house was his, the deed was in his name, and it had nothing to do with Gui Zirong. He had fallen into a huge trap, was stripped of his astronomy student status, and ordered to repay the money within a set time. This was simply his bad luck.","However, on the day Wu the Big Fool was arrested, a runner named Lu Sheng, who lived near Gui Zirong and recognized him, suddenly remembered something. Seeing Gui Zirong reported as a successful candidate, he recalled that on the night of the fourteenth of August, he had clearly seen Gui Zirong drinking at Wu the Big Fool's residence. He wondered: 'On the fourteenth, the scholars were taking their exams in the compound. Why wasn't he inside, and how did he manage to pass?' He thought and thought, but couldn't make sense of it. One day, he ran into a scrivener named Ge Fengshi, the sixth child in his family, a man from Shaoxing who was very good at stirring up trouble.","That day was the third of October. Runner Lu walked into a small teahouse in front of the yamen and saw Scrivener Ge drinking tea inside, a small yellow cloth bundle beside him. He was wearing a padded silk robe from Guizhou under a black jacket, head down, eating a baked wheat cake. The runner approached, bowed, and said, 'Mr. Ge, sitting here alone?' Ge Fengshi acknowledged him. Runner Lu sat down opposite him, and the waiter brought another bowl of tea. Runner Lu said, 'You have some free time today; I suppose you're not on duty?' Runner Lu replied, 'Not on shift these next few days. Mr. Ge, you're always so busy, you must be making a fortune lately. You're a man on the rise, soon to be promoted to principal clerk. Will you look after us then?' Ge Fengshi sighed, 'Old Lu, you're an old hand in the yamen, don't you know our hardships? To become a principal clerk will take at least seven or eight years; do you think that's easy? Unlike other yamens where there's room to maneuver, what does a principal clerk in this circuit yamen even amount to?' Runner Lu said, 'Still, being a principal clerk is good. Look at the situation of Principal Clerk Huang and Principal Clerk Zhang: brand new, solid houses, fine carriages, big mules fed on dry hay and silver-mane feed. Look how radiant they appear, how respectable their clothes are. Not to mention, they'll eventually get a minor official title. To live without worrying about food and clothing is a rare thing.' Ge Fengshi nodded, having finished his cakes, and then asked, 'Do you want some snacks?' Runner Lu said, 'I've already had fried cakes and sweet porridge. But there's something I don't understand, and since I rarely run into you, it's a good time to ask for your advice.'","Scrivener Ge asked, 'What is it you don't understand?' Runner Lu said, 'We have a neighbor named Gui, a southerner, who married into Wu the Big Fool's family and lives there. I often see him coming and going.'","'My house is less than an arrow's flight from the Wu family, in the same alley, but let's leave that aside for now. Tell me, are the dates for the annual provincial examinations fixed, or can they be changed?' Scrivener Ge replied, 'The provincial exams? Across the empire, the first session starts on the eighth of August and they come out on the tenth. The second session starts on the eleventh and they come out on the thirteenth. They go in again on the fourteenth and finish the exams on the sixteenth. This is the same in every province. The metropolitan exams start on the eighth of March, and it's the same pattern.' Runner Lu asked, 'You say they go in for the second session on the fourteenth of August. What time is roll call, and what time are the gates sealed?' Scrivener Ge said, 'Roll call is always early morning, and the gates are sealed around noon or early afternoon.' Runner Lu asked, 'By the second watch of the night on the fourteenth, are there still candidates who haven't gone in?'",`Scrivener Ge said, 'How could they wait until the second watch? This year roll call was very fast; for the second and third sessions, the gates were already sealed by noon. Anyone still outside at the second watch on the fourteenth had violated the rules in the first or second session and had their names posted outside, so they wouldn't be allowed in. You might think they just hadn't gone in yet.' Runner Lu nodded, 'So that's how it is. What does it mean to "violate the rules and have your name posted outside"?' Scrivener Ge said, 'Why are you asking about all this? Having your name posted means perhaps burning your exam paper, or staining it with ink, or failing to finish and handing in a blank paper. Papers with these flaws are not sent to the copyists; the candidate's name is posted outside and they are barred from re-entering.' Runner Lu asked, 'According to you, can someone whose name is posted outside still pass?' Scrivener Ge said, 'You're a bright one! If they've been posted outside and haven't finished the exams, how could they pass? Even the chief examiner's own son couldn't pass.' Runner Lu said, 'I had heard that you can't pass without finishing the exams. The neighbor I just mentioned, named Gui Zirong, has now passed highly in thirty-fourth place. Yet, on the fourteenth of August at the second watch, when I went to summon Wu the Big Fool, I clearly saw Gui Zirong there. He even came forward to ask what was going on, talked at length, and seemed extremely anxious.'`,"'At the time I didn't pay it much mind. Later, when I saw he was announced as a provincial graduate, I knew I hadn't mistaken him. Thinking it over carefully: he didn't enter the examination compound, so how could he have passed? Tell me, what do you make of it?' Scrivener Ge said, 'That is strange. Perhaps you misidentified him, or remembered the date wrong; maybe it was the night of the thirteenth.' Runner Lu said, 'Even if he were burnt to ashes I'd recognize him, I couldn't be mistaken. As for the date, I have the warrant to prove it, and the next day was the Mid-Autumn Festival, so I definitely wouldn't misremember. What do you think the reason is?' Scrivener Ge said, 'This is truly strange.' He thought carefully for a while and asked, 'Do you know his background?' Runner Lu said, 'That I don't know. He puts on a very good front, they say he's the Wu family's son-in-law. As to which province he's from, I have no idea.' Scrivener Ge said, 'Investigate carefully. If he really didn't enter the compound, then this is an enormous matter, for he must have had someone take the exam in his stead. If you verify it, wait a few days and I'll go with you to see him and see what happens. If we play our cards right, we could make some good money.' Runner Lu said, 'That's exactly what I was thinking.' The two agreed on a plan, Scrivener Ge paid for the tea, and they went their separate ways.","A few days later, Runner Lu investigated the matter thoroughly. Gui Zirong had driven out a wet nurse because she had stolen a banknote and two pieces of silver jewelry; the master had caught her and thrown her out.","Gui Zirong, wary of the many eyes outside the city, had hidden himself inside the city to watch plays. The guests he invited were all close confidants, so he hadn't kept it from them. Among them was 'Ma the Muslim', who acted as his middleman and hired a man from Zhejiang, a stipendiary student in mourning for a parent, promising him a thousand taels of silver with an initial payment of one hundred taels for his literary services.","Gui Zirong, lacking funds, only paid forty taels, and to this day hasn't paid another cent. The middleman, Ma the Muslim, skimmed ten taels from that, so the stipendiary student only received thirty taels for passing the provincial exam for him. Now he makes a scene at Ma the Muslim's place every day, and even smashed down his front gate.","Gui Zirong hid at home and refused to come out, but rumors had already begun to spread outside. Runner Lu, having learned the whole story clearly from the wet nurse's mouth, went and told Ge. Scrivener Ge told Runner Lu to go to Gui Zirong and 'borrow' a thousand taels. Gui Zirong spat in his face, and Lu promptly yelled out the whole story for everyone to hear. With the secret out, Gui Zirong couldn't silence him and had no choice but to have a huge row with him. Unable to extort him, Runner Lu told everyone he met that he was going to sue him. Scrivener Ge drafted a petition for him and submitted it to the Board. When Ma the Muslim heard of this, he informed the stipendiary student, and the two fled into the night to parts unknown. The Board held two hearings; unable to deny it, Gui Zirong had to confess the truth. He was stripped of his degree and imprisoned, pending the capture of the substitute before final sentencing.","When this case broke, it caused a stir among the many failed students and scholars, who beat the drum and attacked him, bringing to light all of Gui Zirong's affairs outside the city as well. The Board treated it as a huge joke. An elderly official, fond of playful writing, composed a judgment in the parallel prose style, which spread throughout the entire city.","From then on, Gui Zirong became known as a 'beast in human clothing'. One day, Wenze's servant copied down the judgment from outside and brought it to show Wenze, just when Nanxiang and Zhongqing happened to be there. As they read it, it said: 'Upon investigation of Gui Zirong: His family originally belonged to the scholarly class, his father having grasped the osmanthus branch [passed the exams]; but his heart is addicted to the stench of copper, and his nature loves to dally with flowers. Wandering aimlessly in the capital, he deceives the weak; shrinking his head in a mean alley, he embraces a lewd prostitute. He indulges his desires wantonly in the realm of wind and moonlight, and plunders wealth beneath the mandarin-duck quilt. Though his buttocks have skin, they are fit to be bored through; his face has no shame, how can he understand disgrace? Greedy for the joys of wine and food, he revels in the chaos of the brothels. Using false pretenses with his associates, the jade tree is often buried in the rear courtyard; entirely lacking in modesty, the autumn washing block is pounded again by the front stream. Having already borne dirt in the mud, how can he be fit to seek fragrance in the moon's cave? Though he claims the rabbit was his previous incarnation, he forgets the turtle is of the same breath; playing the fox to perfection in flattery, he proves himself a pitiful insect. Wu the Big Fool's ruined house is gone, yet he must still pay debts; Ma the Muslim's front gate is smashed, no need to ask about the middleman's fee. Placing Zhang's hat on Li's head, he tries to substitute human effort for the power of Heaven. Though the substitute has dived like a fish out of sight, the demands for bribes still clamor like sparrows. How could the runner have known the whole inverted truth, causing a ruckus in the neighborhood? The students all expose his dark secrets, presenting a flurry of petitions. It is fitting to first strip him of his scholar's robes, erasing the lingering charm of his cut sleeve; then severely whip and beat him, bringing out the hidden pain of the driven awl. According to the law, he shall be exiled to the malarial frontiers, pending the arrest of all involved; this shall be written in large characters to warn the literati and appease public anger. Thus judged!' Everyone read it and laughed endlessly. Zhongqing said, 'This is heaven's justice made manifest, retribution without fail. If that runner hadn't kicked up a fuss, who would have known? For such a filthy creature to be counted a provincial graduate truly insults so many people.' Chunhang said, 'Nothing in the world goes right these days. Look how many slip through the net, while many others are wrongly accused. The other day Yaoqing was saying that the man who is closest to our classmates and teaches him painting, a native of Nanjing named Jin Su, is fundamentally a true scholar who is open and upright, elegant and unworldly. Because he was staying at the house of a certain high official when he first arrived in the capital, and wasn't careful about his conduct, his servant had an affair with one of the women of the household. The official wrongly suspected this gentleman of a Sima Xiangru and Zhuo Wenjun affair, and so he was dismissed. Unexpectedly, this high official, clear-sighted in blaming others but blind to his own faults, harbored a grudge and used this as a pretext to build a separate case against him, implicating several other true scholars who were all stripped of their degrees. Wouldn't you say that's a gross injustice?' Wenze said, 'Such things are not uncommon. Look at men like Tang Liuru and Wu Hancha, whose names live on today; even though they were stripped of their status, what harm did it do them? But behavior like Gui Zirong's, I fear, is truly unmatched.' Chunhang said, 'Hard to say. Look at that merchant's son, that relative of a servant: leaving aside their lack of learning, can they be considered of clean background? It's just a matter of who is lucky and who isn't.' Just as they were speaking, Shi Nanxiang's servant came in and said, 'Please return home, Young Master, the Master has been appointed Circuit Intendant.' Hearing this, Nanxiang took his leave of everyone and went back first. To know what happened next, listen to the explanation in the following chapter."]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations32:t},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),a={33:["It is said that Censor Shi had been appointed as the Daoming Intendant, and Nanxiang accompanied him to his new post, planning to return for the metropolitan examination the following year. The various scholars and renowned actors saw them off, lingering for a few days to reminisce. Time flew by, and soon it was mid-December. With Nanxiang and Gaopin out of the capital, Ziyu found himself missing two close confidants. Last month, Grand Secretary Wang visited Madam Yan, proposing that Ziyu should be married either by the end of winter or early spring.","Madam Yan replied that she could not make the decision alone; she must send a letter to Jiangxi and wait for a reply before settling the matter. Because of this, Ziyu seldom visited the Wang residence. Recently, Xu Ziyun had filled a vacancy, which added public duties to his work in the yamen, preventing him from spending every day in the garden.","That day, the weather was mild and clear; the snow had melted, and the wind was calm. Ziyu wanted to visit Pincai to ask for news about Qinyan.","After breakfast, he reported to his mother, took a carriage into the city, but before traveling half a mile, he began to hesitate. He thought Pincai might not even be home. The more he thought about it, the less enthusiastic he felt, so he said, 'I won't go. Let's head out of the city and go back!' Yun'er reined in his horse, the driver turned the carriage around, and they left the city. Suddenly, several carriages blocked the road, with a herd of camels squeezed among them. The drivers shouted loudly, unable to clear the way. Ziyu lowered the curtain of his carriage as it pulled alongside another. Looking through the glass window, he saw the person inside turning to look at him as well—it was Baozhu. Seeing him, Ziyu couldn't help but smile. Baozhu asked, 'Where are you coming from? And where are you going?' Ziyu replied, 'I'm coming back from the city; I'm not going anywhere.' Baozhu suggested, 'Why not come to my place for a chat? We haven't seen each other in two months.' Ziyu thought it was still early to return and that it might be a good chance to relax, so he said, 'Very well!' One carriage moved aside, with Ziyu in front and Baozhu following. They arrived at the entrance, got off their carriages, and Baozhu led him inside.","It was Ziyu's first time visiting. Upon reaching the inner courtyard, he saw three main rooms facing forward, with the west room serving as a study. A plaque hung above, reading “Little Langgan Studio.” Entering, Ziyu felt a fragrant breeze that was untainted by dust; two pots of daffodils were in full bloom. On the table sat an antique bronze vase holding a sprig of heavenly bamboo and two sprigs of wintersweet, while over there were two pots of Tang flowers. The calligraphy and paintings on the walls were all authentic works of past masters, certainly not the typical pieces of common officials. In a small alcove, on the left hung a horizontal scroll of Baozhu's portrait leaning against bamboo, and on the right hung four small screens featuring flowers painted by his art teacher, Jin Su. Upon seeing this, Ziyu sighed softly, 'There is indeed fortune and misfortune in the world. To think that such a great scholar has met this fate—heaven truly envies talent!' He turned to Baozhu and added, 'I have heard that the way you treat this gentleman, and the way he treats you, is no less than the devotion Huifang showed to Xiangfan. Moreover, since his misfortune, you have grown closer, fully providing for his travel expenses. Such a heart is exceedingly rare and puts the so-called scholars of our time to shame. This gentleman is also deeply grateful.' As Ziyu spoke, Baozhu remained silent; his eyes reddened, and he let out a long sigh. 'We are both wanderers in this world; why must we have known each other before meeting?' he quoted, unable to hold back his tears. Ziyu's unintentional words had touched upon Baozhu's secret sorrows. Realizing his abruptness, Ziyu smiled and pointed to the bamboo outside the window, saying, 'In the freezing winter, comparing this gentleman to the Tang flowers shows his natural elegance, holding true like the pine and cypress.' Hearing this, Baozhu broke into a smile through his tears, and Ziyu felt relieved. He then remarked, 'Time passes so quickly; in the blink of an eye, it's already the end of the year. Time truly flows like water.' Baozhu agreed, 'Indeed, the new year is approaching. The other day, Jiantan mentioned that right after the new year you will be celebrating—will you invite us to your wedding feast?' Ziyu replied, 'It hasn't been decided yet; we'll wait for the letter from the elders.' Baozhu then said, 'I just got two nice dishes today; would you do me the honor of dining here? If so, I'll invite Xiangwan over, and we can catch up.'","Ziyu hesitated, saying, 'If I dine here, I'll be late returning. There was a heavy snowfall the other day; you must have collected some snow water. Why don't we boil snow to brew tea, and invite Xiangwan for a refined conversation—wouldn't that be nice?' Baozhu smiled, 'Very well, you are indeed different from others.' He sent someone to invite Sulan while ordering the brazier to be moved outside and the tea stove brought over, along with the complete set of tea utensils. Ziyu saw a large bronze tray placed on the floor, upon which sat an antique bronze tea stove. The stove was over a foot high, round like a bushel, with tripod legs; it had two openings and a small, round mouth. After adding some glowing coals from the brazier and some raw charcoal, a lively fire roared up. Another servant presented a large azure porcelain bowl and an engraved Yixing clay teapot with an overhead handle, filling it with snow water. Ziyu admired the sight, remarking, 'Even before the tea is brewed, seeing this tea set is enough to clear the mind and quench the thirst.' Just then, Sulan arrived, and everyone exchanged greetings. Sulan smiled at Baozhu, 'You are so elegant today; surely it is for a refined guest. But with a vulgar person like me joining, I hope I don't spoil the elegance.' Baozhu replied, 'You are extremely elegant yourself.' Sulan asked Ziyu, 'Why have you been staying indoors lately? Have you seen Yunong?' Ziyu answered, 'No. How could Yunong come out now? I never expected he would find his place there.' Baozhu smiled, 'I fear that place isn't where Yunong belongs. With his aspirations, how could he endure being wronged for long?' Ziyu replied, 'I heard they treat him very well; what wrongs could he suffer?' Baozhu said, 'Being treated well is one thing, but Young Master Hua is not entirely considerate. Even Duxiang's treatment couldn't win Yunong's heart; how could that place compare to Duxiang's?","“The situations are completely different. That place strictly follows the rules at every step, which is inconvenient for someone accustomed to leisure. On the fourteenth of the eighth month, I saw Yunong come out to serve; it was clearly forced, a matter of having no other choice.” Sulan added, “Now when he sees us, there is a strangeness about him; it feels like his heart is always clouded with a lingering melancholy.” Ziyu listened and could not help but sigh. When Baozhu saw the water boiling, he took a jade tea caddy from the curio cabinet, blended four famous teas, and brewed it himself, placing the lid on. He then brought out three Ding-kiln porcelain cups, divided the tea into three portions, filled the caddy with boiling water again, and covered it. Ziyu remarked, “You went to the trouble of preparing it yourself; it must have been exhausting.” Baozhu said, “Taste it; how is the flavor?” Ziyu and Sulan took a couple of sips and felt the fresh fragrance fill their mouths, seeping into their hearts and spleens. They both exclaimed, “This tea is excellent, and it doesn't taste like just one kind.” Baozhu revealed, “I combined various fine teas into one bowl.” Ziyu said, “No wonder it is so fragrant and delicious.” Baozhu then brought out a fruit box to accompany the tea. Ziyu noted, “This is even better than wine.” The three chatted idly for a while before Sulan asked Ziyu, “Have you seen your family friend Wei Pincai lately?” Ziyu replied, “I haven't seen him in two months. I specifically meant to visit him today. I had already entered the city, but I figured he is often out and about, so I suddenly lost interest and turned back; that's when I ran into Yaoqing.” Baozhu gave a charming smile and said, “You were wrong; you should have gone. Even if Pincai wasn't home, the person in your heart never goes out; if he knew you were there, he would surely come out to see you.” Ziyu remained silent. Sulan asked, “Do you not know about Wei Pincai's recent affair?” Ziyu questioned, “What affair?” Sulan laughed, “This Wei Pincai used to incite people to cause trouble for Yunong, and I hated him for it. But when Yunong moved in there, nothing much happened. I see that he isn't a great villain, just petty in his opinions. Little did he know that after ruining others before, he is now being ruined by someone else, and he won't have the face to see anyone in the future.” Startled, Ziyu quickly asked, “What shameful thing happened?” Baozhu, also unaware, chimed in to ask what occurred. Sulan explained, “Wei Pincai wasn't good to begin with, but it's hard to make true friends nowadays; many have the faces of men but the hearts of beasts. Do you really not know that Wei Pincai visited a prostitute and was caught by the ward official, who sent him to the Ministry of Punishments?” Astonished, Ziyu asked, “Such a thing happened! How did it go to the Ministry of Punishments?” Sulan replied, “I heard it from Zhang Zhongyu. Zhongyu is now a commander, so he knows about this; it happened four or five days ago.","That day, Wei Pincai invited Lord Fu Third to drink at Rongguan's residence. Lord Fu suddenly remembered something and went into the city first. Pincai stayed behind, instructing Rongguan to fetch a young woman named Yu Tianxian to spend the night at Rongguan's place. Approaching the second watch, they were still drinking and singing. An official named Yu Taisun came for a night inspection; he recognized Pincai, having shared meals and watched plays with him before. Pincai felt relieved seeing it was Official Yu and invited him to sit, but Yu refused. Pincai started joking with him, but the official's face turned serious as he said, “Old Wei, we cannot joke today; do you not understand that official business must be handled officially?” Pincai thought he was still joking and replied, “What official or private business? Don't pull rank as a ward official; even high officials drink with prostitutes occasionally. Just sit down.” He tugged at him, but the official snorted and declared, “Never mind you; tonight, even if the Censor-in-Chief were sitting here, I would arrest him.” No sooner had he spoken than several soldiers pulled out chains, bound Pincai, and dragged him outside. Two more secured Rongguan and Yu Tianxian. Poor Wei Pincai, in a brand-new outfit, was tied to the back of the carriage and made to run behind it. Upon reaching the official's quarters, he was interrogated impartially. Fortunately, Pincai's servants found a clerk and negotiated a bribe of one thousand six hundred strings of cash, signing a promissory note and finding a guarantor before unlocking his chains. They fabricated a story, claiming Wei Pincai was Li Sancai, that Rongguan had hosted a meal that day, and that coincidentally, Rongguan's married sister had returned to visit her brother. They insisted there was no communal drinking, only a misunderstanding of men and women mixing.","Having established the facts, they were promptly released.' Ziyu said, 'Since it's settled, why send it to the Ministry?' Sulan replied, 'I heard a patrolling censor discovered the official was extorting bribes, altering testimony, and releasing people privately, so he escalated the case and sent it to the Ministry of Punishments.' Baozhu added, 'So Wei Pincai is now in prison? He deserves it. But why hasn't Young Master Hua taken care of him?'","Sulan said, 'According to Zhongyu, they are keeping it a secret from Young Master Hua, and besides, he used a fake name. It's likely he's just lost face, and it won't amount to a major crime. I also heard Wei Pincai recently purchased a minor official rank; if convicted, that title might be stripped as well.' Hearing this, Ziyu felt anxious for Pincai and repeatedly said, 'This is terrible! Even our friend Li has been messing around outside. Over the summer, he went to a brothel and was even stripped of his clothes. All his relatives and friends know, and it caused quite a scandal. I never expected Wei Pincai to get caught up in something like this.' Sulan commented, 'It's good he suffers a bit; the retribution is swift. Who told him to use the coachmen to torment others? Now he's getting it paid back with interest.' Ziyu laughed again.","The three of them chatted for quite a while. Seeing it was getting late, Ziyu bid the two farewell and headed home.","Upon reaching the main room, he saw Madam Yan, who appeared displeased. Ziyu dared not ask and stood dumbly to the side. Madam Yan said, “A letter from your father has arrived. He knows everything you've done and says I haven't disciplined you properly. See for yourself.” She handed the letter to Ziyu. Before he even read it, he was already terrified and speechless. He walked over to the window, respectfully held the letter, and read it through. His cheeks flushed bright red; he remained silent, just looking at Madam Yan. Seeing him in this state, she truly felt pity but forced a cold smile, saying, “Since you know to be afraid, wouldn't it have been better not to do those things in the first place! Whether you learn to be good or not is up to you; either way, I cannot follow you when you go out. If you continue to misbehave, your father may not spare you when he returns.” Ziyu could only reply repeatedly, “Yes!” not daring to sit down or leave. Madam Yan, finding it inconvenient to comfort him, could only ask if he had seen Wei Pincai today.","Hearing this, Ziyu hesitated, appearing as if he wanted to speak but couldn't. When Madam Yan asked again, Ziyu said, “I didn't see him, but I received word that Wei Pincai has gotten into some trouble and was reported; the day before yesterday, he was detained in the Ministry of Punishments prison.” Startled, Madam Yan hurriedly asked, “Who told you this? What happened, and where did you hear it?” Ziyu casually replied, “It was an acquaintance, Zhang Zhongyu, a relative of Wei. He wasn't very clear, but it sounds like he was caught drinking with prostitutes by the ward official.” Hearing this, Madam Yan cursed, “What a despicable creature! Doing such shameless things—what will happen now? Is the Hua family not going to intervene?” Ziyu said, “I heard Wei spends a lot of time outside the city, and he used a fake name, claiming his surname is Li, likely keeping it from the Hua family. Others say he recently purchased a minor official rank. Even though he claims to be Li Sancai, people know he is Wei Pincai.” Madam Yan's face turned red with anger. After a pause, she said, “Great, they are all useless. Even Li is never home; who knows what he does outside, probably nothing proper, and I can't even scold him. As for Pincai, I assume he knows the details.” Ziyu replied, “According to Li, he hasn't seen Pincai in two or three months; they have grown quite distant recently.” Madam Yan said, “But what about Pincai's situation? Although he may not be worth much pity, he is still the son of your father's old friend. We need to find out the truth.” She called for a maid to fetch Mei Jin, who soon arrived and stood below the steps. Madam Yan explained Pincai's situation, instructing him to go to Elder Wang and ask him to keep an eye on things, or perhaps put in a good word at the Ministry. Mei Jin replied, “I will go at once. But although Young Master Wei's matter is small, he is already locked up, and even his family isn't allowed to bring him food. I don't understand why it's so strict. I fear Elder Wang may not be willing to intercede. Perhaps someone from the Hua family will assist him.” Madam Yan asked, “Do you know the specifics of what happened?” Mei Jin said, “I heard people talking about it yesterday.” He then detailed Pincai's situation. Madam Yan responded, “Even so, we must do our part. Go to Elder Wang's place first, and we can discuss whether it's possible later.” After Mei Jin left, Madam Yan sneered, “This is what comes from liking to visit those young masters' homes.” Ziyu's face turned bright red with shame, and he could only sit down on a stool below. He accompanied Madam Yan for dinner, then returned to his study. From then on, Ziyu was filled with fear and dared not entertain any wild thoughts for several days.","Mei Jin arrived at the Wang residence. Wenhui called him in and asked the reason for his visit. Mei Jin explained the situation, and Wenhui sneered, saying, 'That Wei Pincai—I knew the moment I saw him that he was nothing good. Your master insisted on keeping him; fortunately, he has left now. How can I possibly intervene? Besides, I have no acquaintances in the Ministry of Punishments. Go back and pay your respects to the madam, and tell her I can only ask someone else to try his luck.' Mei Jin returned and reported exactly what was said. Madam Yan was at a loss and could only let things take their course. Meanwhile, in prison, Pincai promised Rongguan and Yu Tianxian a large sum of silver, instructing them to align their testimonies with his. He told them to claim that the official had invited him to drink at Rongguan's residence and had called for the woman, Yu Tianxian. During the drinking, the official asked Pincai for a loan of one thousand taels; when Pincai refused, an argument ensued.","Official Yu had brought soldiers with him beforehand; they immediately chained them up and took them back to his quarters. They then fabricated the charge of being caught during a night inspection, extorted bribes for their release, and forced them to sign promissory notes. Yu Tianxian also testified that Official Yu frequently visited her home to smoke opium and drink; half a month earlier, he had sent an invitation for a share of the money, but when it didn't arrive, he harbored a grudge and set a trap to arrest them.","After the arrest that day, he extorted another five hundred strings of cash to alter his testimony and release them. Rongguan's testimony was identical. The Ministry held two hearings, and their testimonies matched. Young Master Hua had already learned of this; initially wanting to ignore it, he felt uneasy and had to send someone to the Ministry of Punishments to put in a good word, which resulted in a much lighter sentence. Official Yu was dismissed from his post, Pincai received twenty strokes of the cane, Yu Tianxian was expelled from the jurisdiction, and Rongguan was released to go home, thus concluding the case.","Pincai returned to the city triumphant, claiming he had won the lawsuit, and went straight back to the Hua residence.","Upon seeing him, the gatekeepers offered many words of comfort. Pincai boasted, 'I didn't suffer the slightest injustice. Those officials are all on good terms with me, and the prison warden is a close friend; everything was thanks to them. They took great care of me these past few days. I wonder if the young master knows about this?' The crowd could only reply that they didn't know.","After Pincai entered his room, groups of people came to inquire about him, but he saw no one sent by Young Master Hua. Pincai genuinely believed he was unaware of the matter, which put his mind at ease. On the third day, Lin Shanzhi entered, holding a large package wrapped in both hands, resembling silver. He placed it on the table and said, “This is a gift from the young master.” With that, he turned and walked away. Pincai hadn't even managed to say the words “thank you” before he was long gone. Seeing this unusual behavior, Pincai felt suspicious and looked at the silver package. On it was written: “Farewell gift, two hundred taels.” His heart skipped a beat, and after a moment of deep thought, he understood, but for a moment, he couldn't decide what to do. He wanted to wait for Shanzhi to return and clarify things.","However, after waiting two days, no one came, not even the usual acquaintances Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei. After pondering for half the night, he finally made up his mind. Early the next morning, he wrote a letter of thanks: first expressing his gratitude, then stating that there were matters at the Mei residence requiring him to return and manage the household affairs. It was difficult to refuse, so he had to leave temporarily and would return in the spring. After writing, he went to the gatehouse and told the gatekeeper to deliver the letter. About half an hour later, the gatekeeper returned, saying: “The young master has read your letter and says returning to the Mei residence is appropriate. The young master is busy and cannot see you off personally.” Pincai still harbored hopes of reconciliation, but hearing these words, his heart turned to ash. He could only say: “Many thanks to the young master and all the gentlemen for your care over the past half year. I will move out today and won't be able to bid farewell in person.” The gatekeeper agreed and left. With no other choice, Pincai packed his luggage and belongings, asking the steward for a large carriage to load them. He had a horse and carriage, two young servants, a cook, and a driver; together, they left the city and temporarily stayed at an inn, planning to find a new residence later.","Pincai had only been in the Hua residence for ten months, yet through deceit and trickery outside, he had amassed quite a sum of money. Aside from his regular stipend, Young Master Hua had bestowed many gifts upon him. Pincai spent over four hundred taels to buy a minor rank of the ninth grade, and squandered another two thousand on clothes and extravagance. By this time, excluding the two hundred taels from the previous day, he still had three hundred taels left, along with various trinkets. Fortunately, the rank he purchased placed him high on the list, meaning he could be selected within about half a year. Consequently, his courage and confidence grew, vastly different from before. After staying at the inn for two days and finding it too noisy, he rented the house at Hongji Temple where Chunhang used to live. With a grand carriage and horses, he began to live extravagantly. He didn't even visit the Mei residence. Rongguan and Yu Tianxian visited frequently; Pincai distributed a hundred taels between them and gave them miscellaneous trinkets. Engaging in daily drinking and singing, he naturally drew close to a group of like-minded individuals.","Meanwhile, Lord Fu Third, upon hearing of Pincai's trouble, spent several days inquiring at the Ministry but found no way through. He only felt relieved after learning that Young Master Hua had interceded on his behalf. Later, hearing that Pincai had left his patron, his concerns returned, and he intended to invite him home. One morning, he went out of the city early to find Pincai and saw a group of people making a racket at the temple gate. As Lord Fu Third stepped down from his carriage, he noticed a man in a blue cloth old sheepskin coat and a worn autumn hat, in his thirties, cursing vulgarities. Lord Fu Third heard him say, 'So this is how friends act; fetching things all day, pouring tea and water, lighting fires and heating the bed—what didn't I serve you with? You promised a hundred strings, and this is what happens. Now you refuse to acknowledge it, just giving me thirty strings. You think there's any good done in official circles? Crossing the river and burning the bridge—pray you don't come back in. Wait till next time; you'll see.' Hearing this, Lord Fu Third knew it was a jailer from the Ministry of Punishments and walked in with a frown. Pincai's men saw him and immediately announced his arrival. By the time Lord Fu Third entered the courtyard, he heard the clattering of castanets. A servant opened the wind door, and Pincai and Rongguan came out to greet him. Rongguan stepped forward, bowed slightly, and reached out for his hand. Lord Fu Third gave him a pinch, and Rongguan twisted his hand back. Lord Fu Third scolded, 'What are you messing around for, little rabbit?' Breaking free, he hurriedly greeted Pincai, asking how he was, and said, 'Congratulations! Congratulations! Those past few days I was truly worried, but not knowing anyone in the department, I couldn't come see you. Once you returned to the city, I was just about to visit, but then you left your patron. My friend, how could I not worry about you? You are a stranger here, with no family or old friends; what are your plans now? Will you seek another patron, or live outside the city? Otherwise, come to my humble home; we can look after each other during the New Year, to avoid the lonely atmosphere of the temple.' Pincai replied, 'Thank you for your kindness, Third Brother. But it's more convenient for me to stay outside the city; I have a few matters to attend to. If I move into the city, it would be inconvenient. Perhaps I will trouble you next year.' Lord Fu Third asked, 'Are your travel expenses enough to get by?' Pincai answered, 'I can manage for a few months.' Lord Fu Third advised, 'You should be more frugal. You're used to the luxuries of the Hua residence; if you try to live like that now, it will be troublesome.' Pincai agreed, 'Naturally, I must cut back. For instance, these two animals are excessive, but I can't do without them. A hired carriage costs one string and six hundred cash a day. Calculated properly, the expenses are actually limited.' Lord Fu Third wanted to take Pincai out for a meal, but Pincai suggested, 'Let's eat here,' and ordered a few more dishes to be prepared. Lord Fu Third countered, 'Let's go to a restaurant; it saves you the trouble.' Before Pincai could answer, Rongguan interjected, 'You're so foolish; today is the twenty-fifth of the twelfth lunar month—are there any restaurants open? They're all closed for the New Year to collect debts.' Lord Fu Third laughed, 'That's right; my mind has been so unsettled these past two days I even forgot the date.' Pincai asked, 'What's troubling you that you fear not making it through the New Year?' Lord Fu Third explained, 'It's not about the New Year; that's unimportant. You forgot about my Zhili prefecture position; it's now up for final selection. The day before yesterday, two vacancies opened: one in Hubei, the other in Guizhou. Hubei is good, while Guizhou is extremely harsh. It should have been my turn for Hubei, but someone who was demoted submitted their credentials, and I fear it's his now. I heard Guizhou doesn't yield enough in a year; what should I do? I thought about seeking the officials at the Selection Department for advice; do you think it's possible to negotiate?' Pincai replied, 'The monk here is a monastic official; his brother is a clerk in the Ministry of Personnel's Selection Department. Perhaps we could ask this monk to negotiate; it might be reversible.' Lord Fu Third agreed enthusiastically, 'Very good; it's inconvenient for me to speak to him directly. Please talk to him; if it succeeds, I will reward him handsomely.' Pincai nodded, 'This monk is easy to talk to. He hardly counts as a monastic; he's adept at eating, drinking, whoring, and gambling, plays and sings well, and even specializes in treating syphilis—he's quite a character.","“Everyone calls him Master Tang, but he insists on being called Brother Tang.” Before Pincai could finish his sentence, the wind door rattled, and a head wearing a brown felt hat with trimmed edges and a thick mustache popped in, then quickly withdrew. Pincai called out, “Brother Tang, come in and sit down.” The man replied, “I’ll come back in a moment.” Pincai insisted, “Please, come in. This guest is the Master Fu Third I mentioned; he wants to meet you.” Monk Tang then pried open the wind door and walked in. Pincai and Fu Third stood up, and Monk Tang, with a face full of smiles, said, “So this is Master Fu Third; today, this humble monk is truly fortunate to behold such an esteemed person.” Fu Third replied, “I have long heard of you,” and they shook hands. The monk sat down heavily on a chair and scrutinized Fu Third from head to toe. Fu Third noticed that this monk looked quite peculiar: short in stature, wearing a fine green crepe sheepskin monk's robe tied with a yellow silk sash, grey fleece-lined shoes on his feet, his face etched with sinister lines, and possessing a pair of lecherous eyes. In his hand, he held a white jade snuff bottle, which he offered to Fu Third, who in turn presented him with an agate bottle. After taking a sniff, the monk asked, “Does Master Third live in the city? You may not know me. Years ago, my master was very close to your respected father. When your father patrolled the southern city, he often visited our small temple; he loved playing go and frequently played with my master. Didn't you notice that pair of couplets next to the master's shrine? They were personally written by your father, carved, and sent over. That was twenty-nine years ago. Master Third, in which yamen are you serving now to receive such congratulations?” Fu Third replied, “I served as a secretary in the Ministry of Revenue for a few years. This year, following the regulations, I purchased a position in the Zhili prefecture, and now I am about to leave the capital.” The monk asked, “Which province have you been assigned to?” Fu Third said, “It’s not decided yet; there are currently two vacancies: Hubei and Guizhou. I just have to try my luck.” The monk confidently stated, “Master Third, it will definitely be Hubei. My ancestral home is Hubei. Since you coincidentally met me today, it will undoubtedly be Hubei; there's no need to say more.” With that, he burst into hearty laughter. Pincai interjected, “You should eat here; there's another matter I need to discuss with you.” The monk agreed. Pincai pulled him into the room to talk for a while; Fu Third could hear clearly as the monk repeatedly said, “It's easy; leave it to me to save face, don't worry, don't worry.” When they came out, the monk said to Fu Third, “Master Third’s happy affair, Master Wei has just explained. I will send someone to fetch my brother to discuss it. I guarantee it will be handled properly; Master Third won't need to worry a bit, leave it all to me.” Fu Third expressed his thanks. Suddenly, a young monk, about sixteen or seventeen years old, entered through the wind door. He was exceptionally handsome, wearing a green silk grey squirrel cap, a lotus-colored floral crepe fox-fur monk's robe, a silk sash around his waist, and bright red trimmed shoes. He brought a water pipe to pack tobacco for his master. The monk didn't even offer it to the guests and started smoking. Upon seeing him, Fu Third was thoroughly captivated, his eyes lingering on the boy. Rongguan, standing behind Pincai, gestured playfully at Fu Third, prompting Fu Third to smile and ask, “Brother Tang, is this your disciple? I feel like I've seen him before.”","The monk boasted proudly, 'My disciple is named Deyue; he is fifteen this year. He's decent at reciting sutras and singing, but he's quite mischievous, so I never allow him out. Master Third, where might you have seen him?' Master Fu Third, eyes crinkling with a smile, said, 'Let me think.' After a moment's thought, he suddenly laughed heartily and said, 'Bah! I remembered wrong; I thought it was the nun from the Great Compassion Convent. The resemblance is striking.' Pincai roared with laughter, while the young monk blushed deeply. Monk Tang chuckled, 'Master Third, you jest.' Pincai added, 'If you dressed him as a nun, no one could tell. Our Brother Tang here is a man of true pleasure; whatever he eats, wears, uses, or plays with is of the finest quality.' Monk Tang replied, 'Amitabha, what is good about being a monk? While my senior brother was alive, he kept me restrained, but now I am much more comfortable than before. Originally, there was a Master Tian living in this room for a year, and he had companions visiting every day. If I occasionally came by, my senior brother would endlessly nag that I shouldn't go over. It's laughable how my senior brother, who never ate, drank, or spent money, tightly hoarded that rent, only to have it all swindled away by his nephew. When he died, he was left empty-handed, and I had to arrange his funeral. Life is short; one should enjoy eating and playing. Master Third is no outsider; nowadays, monks are all meat-eating and wine-drinking monks, what pure rules do they keep? I never hide the truth; I genuinely dabble in eating, drinking, whoring, and gambling.' Fu Third laughed heartily and said, 'You truly are a straightforward man.' The three chatted for quite a while. Seeing the young monk was truly adorable, Fu Third couldn't help but drool. He also noticed him and Rongguan sitting on the same bench, whispering intimately to each other.","It was already noon when Pincai's servants arrived to set the table and arrange the cups and chopsticks. Fu Third said, 'Please don't go to any trouble.' Pincai replied, 'There isn't much to eat.' Thus, they took their seats as host and guests, and Fu Third invited Deyue to sit as well. Monk Tang ordered Deyue and Rongguan to pour the wine. Seeing the table filled with small fruit plates, Fu Third remarked, 'What is all this? Take them away; just keep four plates, that will be enough.' He requested to keep the shredded pheasant, ham, pumpkin seeds, and almonds. Rongguan protested, 'Wait, wait!' He snatched a plate of oranges and grabbed a handful of kumquats, saying, 'If you don't like them, others do.' Nine dishes were served consecutively, all very tasty. Rongguan picked up a meatball and tossed it into Monk Tang's mouth, which the monk swallowed whole. Rongguan tossed another, and the monk ate that too. Rongguan quipped, 'Two balls weighing eighteen catties; those who eat meat have no use for them, but those who are vegetarian, please enjoy.' Fu Third and Pincai burst into laughter, and Monk Tang chuckled, 'It's fine for me to eat, but if you ate them, you wouldn't be able to handle it. Never mind eighteen catties; could you even swallow one weighing four taels?' With that, he reached over and pinched Rongguan twice. Rongguan glared, snatched off his felt hat, and rubbed his bald head, asking, 'Look, everyone, what does this resemble?' Monk Tang retorted, 'It looks like a cock; do you like it?' Rongguan crumpled the felt hat and said, 'Look at this, what does this resemble?' Fu Third chimed in, 'Rongguan is always so mischievous; don't make Master Tang hit you.' Monk Tang hastily smiled and said, 'No harm, no harm! Just a joke, it's nothing.' He leaned in closer to Rongguan's ear and whispered, 'It looks just like your rear flower. With my head sometimes in front of you and sometimes behind you, it gives you a little advantage, doesn't it?' Rongguan put the felt hat back on him and retorted, 'You bald thief.' After a few cups of wine, Deyue's face turned red, making him appear even more charming. Fu Third commented, 'Rongguan, look at Deyue; how refined he is.' Rongguan teased, 'He's nice; are you daring to take him as a disciple?' The group joked around for a while until Monk Tang's craving for opium struck. He set up the lamp right there in Pincai's place and smoked for a bit; they didn't disperse until late afternoon.","Fu Third entered the city, having entrusted Monk Tang with the important matter once more, while Rongguan also returned home. What happens next will be revealed in the following chapter."]},g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations33:a},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={34:[`After seeing Fu Third out, Pincai had Monk Tang send someone to invite Tang's younger brother over. Pincai had just stepped back indoors when Li Yuanmao barged in, declaring, "I've finally tracked you down today! There isn't an establishment I haven't scoured, only to find you've moved here." Pincai replied, "I've only been out of the old place for a few days. Being tangled up with various affairs, I haven't had the chance to call on you and Yuxiang." He then inquired, "How has Yuxiang been lately?" Yuanmao answered, "He's well enough. Last month, the Wang family wrote to the old master, planning to have Yuxiang's marriage finalized sometime in the second or third month of next year. As for my own match, the Sun family keeps pressing the issue—after all, neither of us is getting any younger. I've sent a letter home to the elders, but there has yet to be a reply.`,`For half a year now, they haven't sent a single copper. Today is already the twenty-fifth; from the looks of it, even if a letter is dispatched before the year's end, it might not arrive in time. What am I to do? At present, I owe over forty strings of cash to the restaurants, and miscellaneous expenses will require another twenty to thirty. I have pawned all my garments, but I must redeem at least two decent outfits to make my New Year calls. You must think of a way to help me out." Pincai said, "To be frank—and surely you must know this—my recent entanglement with those extortionists cost me a veritable mountain of money. Now I'm living idly here; if we're speaking of cash, I truly do not have a single coin. Calculating it all, I've spent no small amount this year; who could have foreseen today's straits? Furthermore, I have scarcely any clothes left; apart from borrowing left and right, I have nothing left to pawn." Yuanmao retorted, "Why are you feigning poverty? Is it that you fear I won't repay you if I borrow? This time, when the elders send word for me to manage my wedding, there will be at least five hundred taels of silver. If you lend me forty taels now, or perhaps a hundred strings of cash, I can tide myself over. Otherwise, I might as well be dead. Be a good fellow, be a good fellow! Don't make things difficult." With that, he offered two deep bows. Pincai gave a cold laugh and said, "This is truly bizarre. Why don't you think about it—I haven't taken an official post, nor have I struck it rich, so what makes you assume I'm flush with funds? Let me tell you: you only have a few dozen strings in debt, while I am burdened with several hundred. If you don't believe me, let me show you." He then pulled several account slips from the lining of his boot. Li Yuanmao took them and examined them closely; the tailor's bill was the largest, amounting to over two hundred strings, while the accounts for restaurants and merchants came to another two hundred, plus a few dozen in sundry debts, totaling over five hundred strings. Yuanmao exclaimed, "How did you rack up so much all at once? This is outrageous!" Pincai replied, "There are still some who haven't even sent their bills yet. At this moment, counting the debts, my living expenses here, and the money needed for the New Year, I will need a full eight hundred strings just to scrape by. Right now my hands are empty; I have only a few fur coats which I still need to wear, so they cannot be pawned. I can barely fend for myself—how could I possibly dive into a well to save someone else? Perhaps you could figure something out for me instead? Could you discuss this with your two brothers-in-law?" Yuanmao let out a sigh and said, "Don't even mention those two 'treasures.' They eat and drink for free every day, and I've never seen them play the host even once. Take the eldest Sun, for instance—he owes a fair amount himself and has been hiding away these past couple of days; I'm afraid he might even come asking me for money." Li Yuanmao babbled on with much disjointed talk, and Pincai had no choice but to keep him for dinner. Entering Pincai's room, Yuanmao rummaged out the opium gear and insisted on smoking. He lit the lamp, puffing away with a bubbling racket that left Pincai thoroughly agitated. By the second watch, Pincai urged him to leave, but Yuanmao remained rooted to the spot. Pincai said, "If you go back too late, the gates will be locked; what will you do then? Best go back quickly—it's getting late." Yuanmao declared, "I think I'll rest here for the night." Pincai protested, "I only have one set of bedding; how can it accommodate the two of us!" Yuanmao replied casually, "No matter, you take a large quilt and give me a small one. If we both cover ourselves with some extra clothes, we won't be cold. We haven't shared a bed for a year; tonight is perfect for a good chat." Left with no alternative, Pincai could only indulge him. Oblivious to all propriety, Yuanmao smoked his opium and then demanded fruit; a moment later, he called for snacks, bossing Pincai's servant Si'er around and keeping him endlessly busy. Pincai lay sprawled to one side, completely ignoring him.`,`At the third watch, Si'er came to inform Pincai that Monk Tang wished to speak with him. Pincai went to the monk's room, where he saw a lamp lit on the kang and two candles burning in the room, casting a snow-bright glow. A copper brazier radiated a smoky heat. On a small square table nearby sat a few dishes of leftovers and a pot of spirits, yet the monk was nowhere to be seen. Pincai sat down to wait; after a while, Tang emerged, explaining, "Of all times to need the privy, I was suddenly struck by diarrhea." He had someone fetch a basin of water, washed his hands, and, taking a seat, said, "About the matter we discussed during the day—my brother came by just now, and I spoke with him. He said it can be done. The two vacancies opened on the same day, though Hubei technically takes precedence. By employing a bit of trickery, we can place Guizhou first without causing any hindrance. However, turning things upside down like this is still an act of corruption. My brother noted that he has no personal ties with Master Fu Third, so there's no reason to give away such a massive favor for nothing. Everyone knows that a full term in Guizhou cannot match a single year in Hubei. You will have to be the one to explain this to him." Pincai replied, "Naturally. But I wonder, is your brother truly certain he can secure it?" The monk assured him, "As certain as can be; this is no blind gamble. He will only take the money once the deed is done. Can you shoulder this responsibility?" Pincai said, "Why couldn't I? Master Fu Third is a wealthy man, and he conducts his affairs with great decisiveness. I only wonder how much of a fee your brother expects; give me a figure, and I will go speak with him." The monk said, "If anyone else were negotiating this, it would be exorbitant—three to five thousand taels would not be considered too much. But I told him he is my dearest friend, so he should consider this a favor to me as his elder brother. Therefore, he asks only for three thousand strings of cash. When you think about it, once he takes up this post, the customary informal income alone amounts to twenty thousand taels of silver. What is three thousand strings? Converted, it's only about one thousand two hundred taels. Tell him to write out a bank draft—either way, this is the amount. Once it succeeds, I won't expect anything more from him; I'll just sponge off him for a few extra meals."`,`Pincai calculated matters in his heart, then asked, "If, by chance, they feel the amount is too high, could it be reduced any further?" The monk replied, "This is already as low as it goes; aside from my brother, no one else can make such a decision. Go speak with him first thing tomorrow morning. This matter moves swiftly; an imperial audience could be arranged as early as the twenty-ninth. In affairs like these, one must be experienced and guard against subsequent backtracking. Tomorrow, you must have him deposit the money in an exchange house, with the stipulation that it will be withdrawn the following day. If the affair succeeds, haggling over the sum later on would be deeply unseemly." Pincai said, "I understand. I shall go first thing in the morning." He sat a while longer before returning to his own room, where he found Yuanmao asleep in his clothes, his snoring like thunder. Pincai woke him, provided him with a separate set of bedding, and finally lay down to rest. He pondered Fu Third's affair for a time, then calculated his own debts, and soon it was the fifth watch. After a brief doze, day broke. He called the servants to harness the carriage, washed his face, ate some morning snacks, and donned his clothes, all while Li Yuanmao continued to sleep soundly.`,`Pincai shook him awake, saying, "Get up, I must go into the city, and there is no one at home to attend to you." Yuanmao gave a groggy grunt, rolled over, pulled the quilt over his head, and fell asleep again. Exceedingly irritated and seeing that the man refused to rise, Pincai had no choice but to leave Si'er behind to watch the house, taking a young page with him to ride into town and find Fu Third.`,`As for Yuanmao, he slept until the late morning before finally getting up. Rubbing his eyes, he saw Si'er sweeping the floor and wiping the tables in the room. Yuanmao asked, "Where has your master gone?" Si'er replied, "He went to Master Fu Third's place." Yuanmao got off the kang, put on his clothes, and walked to the outer room. Si'er brought him wash water, brewed tea, and served some snacks. Yuanmao smoked several pipes of water tobacco, spitting phlegm all over the floor, which Si'er dutifully cleaned up. Yuanmao then asked, "Do you know when he will be back?" Si'er answered, "It's hard to say." Yuanmao mused, "There were a few important matters I didn't get to discuss last night before I fell asleep. If I leave now and come back later, I fear I might miss him again. I might as well wait here; I don't have anything pressing anyway." He then asked Si'er, "Have you all eaten?" Si'er replied, "We've already eaten. If you'd like a meal, Young Master Li, I will go tell the cook." Si'er went out. After about a quarter of an hour, he returned carrying a wooden tray holding several dishes, and asked Yuanmao, "Will you be drinking wine?" Yuanmao answered, "Two ounces of spirits will suffice."`,"Si'er first arranged the dishes, then took the wooden tray back out. Yuanmao inspected the food: one plate of smoked chicken, one of eggs, one of shredded meat, and one that looked like gluten. Unsure, he picked up a piece to taste—it was indeed gluten. Si'er fetched a small flagon of wine and a cup, poured him a serving, and stepped out once more. As Yuanmao drank, he observed the furnishings, which were rather exquisite. It was a two-room suite; preoccupied the night before, he hadn't paid much attention. During the day, they had been in the small three-room suite outside. Pincai's bedroom lay to the west of the courtyard, separated by a gate leading to another two rooms. Now sitting on the kang in the outer room, after three or four cups of wine, Yuanmao felt a slight buzz. Since the rice had not yet arrived, he took a closer look around. Above the kang hung four small, meticulously painted New Year scenes, and a striking clock sat on the kang table. To the east were three square nanmu chairs and two tea tables; on one table rested a pot of narcissus, and on the other, a vase of wintersweet. A pair of couplets hung on the eastern wall, and beneath them, by the window, was a small table displaying seven or eight dazzlingly bright copper spittoons. A door curtain with a glass pane hung in the center, and the windows on either side were also inset with glass. The cushions on the kang and chairs were all of sapphire-blue satin. Assorted flutes and stringed instruments—sanxians and pipas—adorned the walls. Losing interest in his wine, Yuanmao peered into the inner room, which featured a row of gauze windows and a blue crepe silk curtain hanging in the middle. Pulling it aside, he stepped in to find the room much more spacious. At the head sat a wooden bed adorned with an ice-crack patterned floor-to-ceiling partition, draped with pale-blue silk curtains. At one end of the bed lay four or five piled brocade quilts, while two bundles of clothes sat at the other. In the center was a rosewood kang table covered with crimson brocade cushions, and on the wall hung a horizontal painting of a sleeping beauty. The western side of the room held four large leather trunks topped with two small wooden chests, all resting upon two wooden cabinets. A large copper brazier, fitted with a copper wire cover, sat in the center. By the window was a writing desk holding two sets of small books. Glancing at the labels, Yuanmao saw they read *Jin Ping Mei*.",`There was also an organizer tray holding a set of brushes and an inkstone. The center of the window was inset with a large pane of glass. To the east sat a small bookshelf filled with miscellaneous items, and below it were two square stools, clad in black satin covers. Having taken all this in, Yuanmao thought to himself, "Does this look like someone with no money? Those four large leather trunks must contain no small amount of clothing, and those two wooden chests and two cabinets are undoubtedly meant for storing silver and cash. He's feigning poverty to deceive me! I absolutely will not let him off the hook today." He then walked back out. Si'er brought in two more dishes and a tin pot of rice: one dish was mutton, the other stir-fried liver. Later, the cook also sent in a small hotpot, all laid out together. Yuanmao devoured five bowls of rice, drank some soup, finished off more than half the bowl of mutton, rinsed his mouth, smoked a pipe, and asked Si'er for a piece of betel nut. He chewed on it for ages, sitting firmly and refusing to leave.`,`Meanwhile, Pincai arrived at Fu Third's residence and conveyed that the matter was all but guaranteed, much to Fu Third's delight. When asked how much it would cost, Pincai replied, "The sum required is not small. He said that the customary income upon taking up this post is thirty thousand taels, so asking for one-tenth of that is hardly excessive; he insists it cannot be done for less than three thousand taels of silver. The monk and I pleaded with him repeatedly, managing to negotiate a twenty percent discount, but he flatly refused to reduce it any further." Fu Third pondered for a moment and said, "Two thousand four hundred taels is not too much. When is it needed?" Pincai answered, "He said it will be required once the audience goes through on the twenty-ninth, but the bank drafts are needed today. Simply issue drafts dated for the thirtieth." Fu Third asked, "Who will hold the drafts?" Pincai replied, "The monk and I will act as guarantors; the two of us will keep them." Fu Third inquired, "And if the post is not secured?" Pincai assured him, "It is guaranteed, absolutely guaranteed. If it fails, the original drafts will be returned to you. You can simply go to the bank on the twenty-ninth and cancel them." Fu Third agreed, "Let it be so. However, these couple of days are the very end of the year, when cash is tightest. I don't know if the exchange houses can gather the full amount. Let's eat, and then we'll go together to discuss it." Thus, he dined with Pincai. Unwilling to delay, Pincai urged him to leave immediately. Fu Third said, "It's quite close by; I'll just ride in your carriage. If we can get everything sorted there, you can take the drafts right away. If one house can't cover it, we'll find another." The two men boarded the carriage and, after traveling less than half a li, arrived at an exchange house, where the manager ushered them inside. After serving tea, Fu Third announced, "I have a specific matter to discuss. I need you to issue me a bank draft for two thousand four hundred taels of silver, to be withdrawn on the morning of the thirtieth."`,`The manager replied, "If it had been two days earlier, it wouldn't have been difficult. But today is already the twenty-sixth, and these next couple of days are extremely busy; I'm afraid we won't be able to scrape it all together." Fu Third retorted, "If your establishment can't gather the funds, who else could?" The manager explained, "Third Master, surely you must know that cash is tight across all the exchange houses right now, and reserves are genuinely low, while outstanding debts from outside have yet to be collected. Let's see what we can take in over the next two days. If we can reach the full amount, that would be ideal; otherwise, we will just have to exchange whatever we have." Fu Third pressed, "How much should be written on the draft?" The manager suggested, "If it were up to me, you wouldn't even need a draft; just come on the thirtieth, Third Master, and we'll settle the exchange then." Fu Third shook his head, "No, no, I am using this to settle an account; it absolutely must be two thousand four hundred taels. If you truly cannot scrape it together, issuing a draft for two thousand, or even fifteen or sixteen hundred, is acceptable. I will figure out the rest elsewhere. If I end up not needing it, I will come on the twenty-ninth to cancel it."`,`Having no other choice, the manager agreed and issued a draft for one thousand four hundred taels. Pincai advised Fu Third, "Tell him to write it out in separate drafts: two for five hundred and one for four hundred. Just in case the recipient cannot use so much this year, they can keep two drafts to withdraw next year." Fu Third agreed, "That makes sense." The drafts were accordingly split. Fu Third pocketed them, bid the manager farewell, and they boarded the carriage. They sought out two more exchange houses, but both claimed they were unable to oblige. With no alternatives—having no established rapport with other unfamiliar houses—Fu Third was forced to return home and consult with his Third Madam. He gathered forty taels' worth of gold leaf, a pair of gold bracelets, and various small gold trinkets, totaling sixty taels in weight, and took them to an unfamiliar pawnshop, exchanging them for one thousand taels of silver and obtaining the corresponding bank drafts. Again, Pincai urged him to split the sum, resulting in one draft for five hundred, one for three hundred, and one for two hundred. Fu Third handed all the drafts over to Pincai. With the matter weighing heavily on his mind, Pincai refused to linger; he took his leave of Fu Third and boarded his carriage to head out of the city alone.`,`Upon returning to his lodgings, he first sought out Monk Tang and relayed the agreed-upon terms. He then produced the three drafts, counted out the sum of one thousand two hundred taels, and had the monk store them safely, reminding him that if the post was not secured by the twenty-ninth, the original drafts must be returned. Monk Tang chuckled and said, "There is no reason it shouldn't be secured. These remaining two hundred taels are rightfully ours to share; passing him one thousand will be more than enough." Pincai said, "I need to go in and change my clothes." He walked straight to his room, only to find Yuanmao still there, having lit the lamp and puffing away on opium. Seeing this, Pincai was furious and seized the opportunity to unleash his temper: "Why are you still here? How can you leave such things out in broad daylight? Furthermore, this is a temple; anyone could walk in and see. What if we get blackmailed? Do you want to be the death of me? How can you be so utterly foolish!" Yuanmao replied casually, "What's there to be afraid of? Who would come here? I've been sitting here all day and haven't seen a single soul walk in. Besides, Si'er is outside keeping watch." Pincai, too enraged to argue further and refusing to pay him any more attention, took off his fiery fox-leg fur coat, changed into his everyday fox fur robe, donned a casual cap, wiped his face, and drank some tea. Yuanmao then resumed his incessant whining to borrow money. When it became clear that Pincai would absolutely not yield, Yuanmao declared, "Since you have no money, surely there are clothes in those four large leather trunks of yours? I'm only asking to borrow a hundred or so strings of cash; it hardly seems like something that would drag you down." Plagued to near death by his badgering, Pincai could only open a small ornamental box, fish out a twisted gold bracelet weighing just over three taels, and hand it to Yuanmao, saying, "This is the only thing I have left. Take it and pawn it. Three taels and six mace worth of gold should fetch over a hundred strings. You must repay me as soon as your family's letter arrives." Only then was Yuanmao satisfied. He sprang to his feet, bid farewell, and departed.`,`On the twenty-ninth, Fu Third indeed secured the post in Hubei. Everyone was overjoyed, and Fu Third hurried to the temple to thank Pincai and the monk. The next day, the bank drafts were handed over to Tang's brother, who skimmed off another two hundred from the one thousand as a "brokerage fee" and kept it entirely for himself. As for the remaining two hundred taels of surplus, he split it, giving one hundred to Pincai. In the end, Pincai netted a solid one thousand three hundred taels. He went into the city, converted half of it into silver bullion, exchanged more for copper coins outside the city walls, and returned strutting with pride, exceedingly elated. Every single one of his debts was cleared, and it goes without saying that his New Year was a boisterous and extravagant affair. That evening, he even brought Yu Tianxian to the temple, invited Monk Tang over to observe the New Year's vigil, and locked the temple gates quite early. A night of fireworks and tube-flares crackled endlessly.`,"Monk Tang worried that incense-offering worshippers might come on New Year's Day; if they were to spot Yu Tianxian within the temple grounds, it would be rather inconvenient. Thus, just as dawn approached, he urged Pincai to send her back via carriage.","Pincai spent the first day making New Year calls and the second day listening to operas. On the third day, he threw a grand banquet at his lodgings, inviting a crowd of idle dandies—such as Feng Zipei, Yang Meichuang, and Wu Dasha—along with a flock of lowly young male companions. Every day was filled with raucous cheers and heavy drinking, accompanied by the clatter of gongs and pure melodies. It was a chaotic din of silk strings and bamboo flutes, a veritable lake of wine and forest of meat. This went on for five consecutive days before they finally took a brief pause, having burned through three hundred strings of cash. On the ninth day, someone was suddenly struck by the urge to gamble and urged Pincai to act as the banker. Pincai reasoned that his luck had been quite good lately, and perhaps he could win some money, so he opened the gambling tables on the evening of the ninth. Whether playing fantan or rolling dice, he again brought Yu Tianxian over to sit in the inner room and manage the banker's cut with him. The turnout was small on the first day, gradually increased on the second, and by the third day, the room was packed to the brim. Word spread from one to two, from two to three, eventually drawing in two major gamblers: Xi Eleven and Pan Third, both arriving with heavy purses.","That day, Pincai won over two hundred taels of silver, and together with Yu Tianxian, collected one hundred and eighty taels in the banker's cut.","The next day, Pan Third wanted to be the banker. He brought two baskets of Songjiang silver ingots—a full one thousand taels. After rolling the fantan fifty times, he had already lost the greater half. By the time accounts were settled, he had lost everything and owed an additional hundred-plus taels. Pincai also lost three hundred taels that day. Monk Tang won one hundred taels, and Feng Zipei won four hundred.",`Xi Eleven won big—over eight hundred and fifty taels. He distributed fifty-odd taels as tips among the young female impersonators and Pincai's servants, pocketing the remaining eight hundred for himself. Xi Eleven took a liking to the young novice monk and tipped him ten medium ingots. Yu Tianxian collected another two hundred and forty taels in banker's fees. Among the crowd was Clerk Tang, the monk's brother, who advised the monk, "I urge you all to stop gambling tomorrow. When I came in earlier, I saw two men whispering outside the door; they looked like ward constables. If trouble erupts, it won't be safe for anyone." Pincai, already jumpy as a startled bird from past troubles, felt a jolt of fear upon hearing this and said, "I'm quite exhausted myself; let's take a break for a couple of days before playing again." Monk Tang replied, "If it's because you're tired, then by all means. But as for fearing some trouble outside, you can all rest easy." Turning to Pincai, he elaborated, "Next to your quarters is a vegetable garden spanning two or three mou. It has five or six thatched huts where the gardener lives with his family, and it has its own separate entrance. Doesn't your courtyard have a double gate connecting to it? I found it unsecure, which is why I locked it. If anything were to happen outside, we simply open that double gate and slip out through the vegetable garden. It leads straight to completely open country—it's not as if they'd march an army in to lay siege!" Pincai said, "Even so, my pausing isn't due to losing money, nor is it from fear of trouble. The truth is that Master Fu Third is preparing to depart, and I wish to host a farewell banquet for him. The day after tomorrow is the fourteenth; I plan to invite him to stay over for a night." He then addressed Xi and Pan: "I hope you two gentlemen will deign to join us for a gathering. Will you do me the honor?" Xi and Pan both accepted. Feng Zipei chimed in, "Yet you don't invite me." Pincai retorted, "You're here every day; must I send you a formal invitation?" Zipei said, "I'll drag Meichuang along as well." Pincai agreed, "Very well." The gamblers settled their accounts and dispersed around the fifth watch, once again sending Yu Tianxian on her way.`,`Feng Zipei shared Pincai's bed that night. Pincai said, "It seems to me that lately there are many who love empty fame and care nothing for substance. Take Young Master Hua, Xu Duxiang, and that set: they scatter gold like dust—the temperament of grand lords indeed. But they hold those young actors in altogether too exalted a regard, treating them as their own equals.`,'"Then there are Tian Chunhang and that crowd of bookworms propping them up, so they get puffed higher and higher, until one cannot venture a single joke at their expense. It is laughable how those actors put on such phony refinement—neither oil nor vinegar—now of one heart with this man, now a bosom friend to that one. I simply do not believe it: could they keep up the same act with coarse, vulgar men? As I see it, they merely know how to beguile those bookworm patrons, and it is precisely those bookworms who have ruined their sense of station. When they see us, they offer not so much as a greeting, nor a single humble word in conversation, and they must go on about calligraphy, painting, the qin, and chess—truly detestable!" Feng Zipei said, "Exactly so. If it were always like that, who would still engage them? Surely all these actors cannot live off Xu Duxiang and his gentlemen alone? To pass a whole lifetime without knowing a single man of power or means—they are muddle-headed worms indeed." Pincai said, "The day after tomorrow I mean to call a few actors and hold a grand gathering of our own. As for those phony-refined ones, I want none of them. Think it over—whom should I call?" Zipei said:',`"The actors are all much of a muchness. But of late there are two who are really quite fine; engaging them is cheap, and what is more, you can send for them whenever you please. In looks they stand comparison with Yuan Baozhu and Su Huifang." Pincai asked, "What are their names?" Zipei said, "One is called Zhuo Tianxiang, the other Zhang Cuiguan." Pincai asked, "Which troupe are they in now?" Zipei said, "The Zhengrong troupe." Pincai said, "The Zhengrong troupe—that name is unfamiliar; I have never had the pleasure." Zipei said, "They are the little barbers of the soft-awning stalls." Pincai laughed, "Pah! How can you bring up such people?" Zipei said, "Don't look down on them—they are even more in vogue than the actors! What do you make of Deyue's head?" Pincai said, "Fine enough, but I don't fancy him—what charm is there in a shaven pate?" Zipei said, "Just so! And as for Tianxiang and Cuiguan, their looks are finer than Deyue's. If you don't believe me, call them over tomorrow first; have a look, and if they please you, engage them." Pincai said, "That will do."`,"The next day, Pincai sent out invitations for a gathering. His guests included Master Fu Third, Gui the Eldest, Xi Eleven, Pan Third, Zhang Zhongyu, and Yang Meichuang. Two sent their regrets—Gui was ill, and Zhang had other matters to attend to—so Feng Zipei and Monk Tang were brought in to fill their seats, making a total of seven host and guests. Pincai summoned Rongguan to accompany Fu Third, and sent someone to the stall to fetch Tianxiang and Cuiguan. Before long, the two barber boys arrived at the temple in a large mule cart, accompanied by a servant. The younger barber, recognizing Zipei, paid his respects to him first before greeting Pincai. Pincai studied him carefully; he was indeed handsome, with clear features, fair skin, and dressed in the same fashionable style as the young female impersonators. Though Tianxiang's complexion was fair, a closer look revealed his skin was slightly coarse. Cuiguan, however, was quick-witted and endearing, save for a few freckles, a mole by his eyebrow, and rough, darkened hands. They were both decked out in the latest tailored garments and hats, wearing gold bracelets and rings, with pocket watches and jade trinkets hanging from their waists. Pincai watched them for a while and found himself quite pleased. Zipei spoke to them, instructing them to return the next day to serve wine. The two boys were exceedingly attentive and eager to please—preparing the opium pipes, pouring tea, and offering all manner of fawning service, even massaging backs and kneading legs. Pincai was immensely delighted, finding them more appealing by the minute, and kept them for supper. Tianxiang and Cuiguan could both sing the local clapper opera; they played the erhu and moon-lute, creating a raucous, squeaking clamor that lasted until the third watch. Pincai tipped them each eight strings of cash and sent them off with thanks.",`Early the next morning, they arrived to serve before Pincai and Zipei had even risen. The two barbers asked Pincai to find his combs and began dressing his hair; once finished, they gave him a massage. The other barber did the same for Zipei. After breakfast, the opium lamps were lit, and everyone partook. Fu Third was the first guest to arrive, and upon seeing him, Monk Tang brought Deyue in. Tianxiang and Cuiguan paid their respects to Fu Third and the monk. Fu Third, not recognizing them, asked who they were and which troupe they belonged to; Pincai smoothly claimed they were from the Quanfu Troupe. Soon after, Xi Eleven and Pan Third arrived together. Xi Eleven had brought Ba Yingguan, while Pan Third was accompanied by a young apprentice whom he was treating as a servant. Greetings were exchanged all around. Upon seeing Tianxiang and Cuiguan, Pan Third laughed and asked, "How did you two end up here?" Xi Eleven quipped, "By the looks of it, Master Wei is preparing to open his own canopy stall and become a manager." Only then did Fu Third realize they were barbers, bursting into hearty laughter. "So it's them, not actors from a troupe! Well, that's perfectly fine too," he said. They all sat together, exchanging jokes for a while.`,`Suddenly, a voice called out from the courtyard, "I'm late! I'm late!" A man stepped through the door, wearing a fur robe and a massive fur hat that fell right to his eyebrows. He offered a bow to the room and explained, "Today, a friend from the Inner City dragged me around all day to inspect houses, and also asked me to evaluate some land for them—they're likely hoping for a provincial appointment." Pincai called Cuiguan and Tianxiang over to meet him, introducing him by saying, "This is Master Yang Eight, who is highly skilled in reading fengshui. Why don't you beg him to inspect your stall and tell you when you'll strike it rich?" Catching the joke, Fu Third chimed in, "You'd better be careful, Yang Eight! Make sure you don't end up like that Wu family affair, having people chase you down to your doorstep after your reading." The room erupted in laughter. Suddenly, Feng Zipei furrowed his brow, muttered that he felt unwell, and rushed out into the courtyard to vomit. Startled, everyone hurried out to check on him. After retching for a while, his face flushed red, he grew dizzy, and his whole body radiated feverish heat. Pincai hurriedly had him lie down on the kang. After resting a bit, his condition only worsened, and he asked to return home. Pincai ordered a carriage, and Zipei's servant escorted him back. As the time for lighting lamps approached, Pincai ordered the room illuminated. He had recently commissioned a glass lamp-screen painted with twenty-four erotic spring scenes, which now sat on the kang. Eight glass lamps hung in the room, with a colorful lantern suspended in the center; four floor lamps burned below. Two braziers were stoked on either side, and a round banquet table was set in the middle. Once seated, Xi Eleven studied the erotic scenes on the lamp-screen and joked to Pan Third, "Old Third, take a look at the one getting slapped—doesn't it look just like you?" Pan Third retorted, "And the one embracing someone looks just like you, only missing your bucket." Fu Third's gaze reached the final panel, and he couldn't help but roar with laughter: "Outrageous! Truly outrageous! Brother Wei, you really shouldn't have hung this here—it fits the scene perfectly! Come look, everyone: aren't these two monks engaged in sodomy?"`,`Everyone looked and erupted in laughter. Xi Eleven turned to Deyue and teased, "Does your master do this every day?" Deyue spat out a "Pah!", his face flushing crimson, and turned his head away, refusing to look. Monk Tang pressed his palms together and chanted, "Amitabha! Sinful, how sinful!" By this time, the seating was arranged. Fu Third took the seat of honor, with Pincai assigning Cuiguan to accompany him. Xi Eleven took the second seat; knowing Xi was a generous spender who had a fondness for Deyue, Monk Tang told Deyue to sit with him. Yang Eight took the third seat, and Pincai had Tianxiang sit next to him. Pan Third sat in the fourth seat, while Pincai and Monk Tang occupied the host's positions. Only Rongguan was absent. As the wine flowed, the conversation devolved into vulgar shouting and coarse jokes, becoming a chaotic, unsightly mess. Fu Third remained somewhat composed; he merely exchanged a few playful jests with Cuiguan without descending into utter crudeness. Xi Eleven, however, held a cup in one hand, aggressively forcing Deyue to drink, while his other hand groped Deyue's backside. Deyue twisted and writhed, his shoulders bobbing unevenly as he pushed against the cup, looking at Xi Eleven with tear-filled eyes. Xi Eleven threatened, "If you don't drink this cup, I'll force you to take a 'skin-cup'!" Left with no choice, Deyue choked it down. Yang Eight's behavior was even more revolting: he pulled Tianxiang onto his lap and bounced his leg up and down, jostling Tianxiang until his whole body shuddered. Yang Eight fed him wine mouth-to-mouth and asked, "I've heard people say your younger sister is very beautiful and knows many people." Zhuo Tianxiang's face reddened as he replied, "You shouldn't believe their one-sided rumors." Yang Eight shot back, "Last year I saw people inscribing fans for her—are those writings just one-sided rumors too?"`,`As he spoke, he leaned in and sniffed Tianxiang's face again. Only Pan Third and Pincai were left without someone to dally with. Pincai laughed and said, "It seems today we'll just have to take turns pestering this old monk." With that, they engaged in a lively game of finger-guessing with Monk Tang. After they had carried on for over two hours, Xi Eleven's craving for opium struck, and he ordered Ba Yingguan to bring out the smoking gear. The lamps were already lit; Xi Eleven lay down and commanded Deyue to join him in smoking. The two barbers, also being addicts, crowded around as well. Seeing this, Monk Tang let out two large yawns and stretched his back. Noting the severity of Xi Eleven's craving, Tang decided he couldn't wait and retreated to his own room to satisfy his habit.`,`Fu Third twisted his body around, pulled Cuiguan close, and asked, "Plying your trade in that shop doesn't seem to offer much of a future. Why don't you come with me to Hubei instead? Are you willing?" Upon hearing this, Cuiguan replied, "If you're willing to take me, you'll be my very own dear father!" With that, he leaned into Fu Third's embrace, pressing his cheek near Fu Third's mouth, and added, "I'm not like a young actor in a troupe, needing money to buy my release. When I started, we agreed my apprenticeship would only be three years. My time is up now; I can leave whenever I please. Dear father, will you truly take me with you?" Fu Third said, "If you're willing to follow me, I shall take you." Overhearing this, Yang Eight turned to Fu Third and said, "Old Third, you're fooling around again! Instead of spending money to take him with you, you'd do better to help me purchase a provincial posting. I implore you to add another pledge to that subscription list you saw the other day." Fu Third replied, "I'll pledge another thirty taels, then; there's no need for you to sit there stewing in jealousy." Rather than taking offense, Yang Eight offered a flurry of effusive thanks.`,`Cuiguan gave a smile and said, "Third Master, you certainly have good fortune! I had just called you my dear father, and you've already attracted another dependent for yourself." Pretending not to hear, Yang Eight sat to one side, quietly smoking his water pipe.`,`Pincai chimed in, "Going with Third Master is an excellent opportunity; what is there to hesitate about? Even though you don't need an actor's formal release fee, we should still tip your master a few strings of cash." Fu Third agreed, "Naturally." Cuiguan asked, "Is it truly settled?" Fu Third replied, "It is settled." Cuiguan practically climbed onto Fu Third, rubbing his head affectionately against Fu Third's shoulder and saying, "Then I shall kowtow in thanks! Good Third Master, good dear father!" Fu Third was overwhelmed with delight. Pan Third, observing Deyue lounging in Xi Eleven's arms and Tianxiang lying opposite them, decided he wanted a puff of opium as well. He moved to the edge of the kang, twisted his body, and leaned heavily against Tianxiang. Deyue finished preparing a pipe of opium, and Yang Eight took it. Parting his thick, unkempt beard, Yang Eight took a drag, his saliva dripping down in little drops, coating the opium pipe. He nonchalantly wiped his mouth with his fur sleeve and took another draw. When the pipe clogged, Tianxiang tried to reach over to clear it, but he was pinned down tightly under Yang Eight's weight. Yang Eight grabbed a cleaning pick and jabbed at the pipe wildly; waving his arm about, he accidentally brought his fur sleeve too close to the lamp, singeing a patch and sending the room into peals of laughter.`,`Yang Eight said, "This is my first time trying it as well." He forced himself to take a puff, but he burned the opium until it was charred and foul-smelling, finally setting the pipe down in defeat. Tianxiang said, "Stop pinning me down like this; I'll prepare it for you." Meanwhile, Deyue was resting his head on Xi Eleven's arm as Xi Eleven continued to grope his backside. Deyue tried to rise, but Xi Eleven pinned him down with one leg; with no other recourse, Deyue had to endure the fondling. Xi Eleven finished an entire box of opium and called for Ba Yingguan to bring more. Yingguan stood far off to the side, sulking in a fit of pique. Xi Eleven called out two or three times before Yingguan finally answered, "There's none left." Xi Eleven snapped, "What do you mean there's none left? I have a large box right in my pouch." After another long pause, Yingguan muttered, "It spilled." Xi Eleven demanded, "Spilled? Bring the box here and let me see it."`,"Fuming with anger, Ba Yingguan marched over and flung the large gold box; it flipped and rolled right up to the lamp.",`As Deyue scrambled to retrieve it, he carelessly knocked the lamp over with a loud clatter, shattering the glass chimney and splashing oil all over Xi Eleven's face. Deyue was deeply mortified, but Xi Eleven brushed it off, saying, "It's nothing." He quickly wiped his face with a towel, sat up, and asked for a basin of water to wash up properly. A few drops had also splashed onto his lynx-fur coat, which he hastily wiped clean. Pincai's servants hurried in to replace the lamp and wipe down the opium tray. When Deyue opened the box to check, he found it truly empty. Xi Eleven asked, "What do we do now? Let's go ask Master Tang for some." Pincai interjected, "I have some! I have some! The other day, I got my hands on a few taels of fine native opium." He then ordered Si'er to go to his room and fetch it.`,"Pincai's quarters were located on the western side of the courtyard; stepping through a gate revealed a small courtyard containing a two-room suite. Pincai had merely locked the courtyard gate; busy entertaining his guests, he had been unable to spare anyone to stand guard over the rooms. The moon that night shone as bright as day. Si'er walked over to the gate, unlocked the padlock, and pushed on the door, but to his surprise, it wouldn't open. The door was usually loose, yet it had suddenly grown tight; even with a bit of force, it refused to budge.",`Setting down his lamp cover, he planted both hands firmly and shoved with all his might, finally managing to crack it open. Peering inside, he saw a stone braced against the door. A deep suspicion arose in his heart as he thought to himself, "There's no one inside—who could have wedged this stone here?" Crouching down, he shoved the stone aside, picked up the lamp cover, and stepped into the outer room to take a look. Noticing that nothing seemed to be missing, Si'er breathed a slight sigh of relief. However, as he walked into the inner room and took a closer, more careful look under the light of the lamp, he jumped in fright. One of the large leather trunks was missing, and the two caskets and a clothing bundle that had been on the kang were gone as well! He bolted outside, shouting, "Master! Disaster—we've been robbed!" Pincai panicked and rushed over to see for himself. As for what he found in the room, if you wish to know, you must listen to the next chapter.`]},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations34:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),i={35:["When Pincai entered his room and saw the trunk and caskets gone, he panicked. He rushed to the vegetable-garden gate in the yard: it was still locked, but scattered items lay near the wall. He shouted for the monk. Tang arrived with everyone else. Pincai insisted the thieves must have climbed over from the garden side and demanded the gardener be questioned, especially since no watch-call had been heard after third watch. Others urged calm and said they should open the gate and inspect first. The monk fetched keys and opened it; luckily the moon was bright as day, so no torches were needed. He woke the gardener, who, terrified by the news, called for his helper repeatedly but received no answer. The gardener grew more frantic, searching everywhere without a trace. The garden gate remained securely shut. In the northwest corner they found a trunk. “Good, here’s the trunk,” the gardener said. They looked and found it empty except for a few padded garments and odds and ends. A foreign watch lay smashed on the ground. The monk concluded the thief had entered from outside the wall and escaped over it, proposing they check from beyond the garden. Pincai replied:","“The thief is long gone; what’s the use of looking?” Fu Third said, “Just go in and inventory the losses. Make a list so we can report it in the morning.” The monk, noting the gardener’s panic, grew suspicious. He intimidated the gardener, accusing him of colluding with the thief, threatening to hand him over to the ward officials tomorrow where a confession would surely be beaten out of him. He ordered everyone to search the gardener’s room. They rummaged through it but found absolutely nothing—only an old woman trembling on the earthen kang. The monk demanded, “Where is your helper? Why isn't he here?” The gardener, also shivering, stood stunned for a moment before answering, “I don't know where he went. He went to sleep before me, saying he would take a nap and then do the night watch. Now the gate hasn't even been opened, yet he's disappeared.” Pincai declared, “There’s no doubt about it then.” The monk added, “What more is there to say? If it wasn't you who colluded in the theft, who else could it be?” He ordered the temple workers and acolytes to tie up the gardener. The old woman cried out in grievance, wailing loudly, so the monk had her tied up as well. Fearing they might attempt suicide, he handed them over to the street soldiers for custody.","Amidst the commotion, Pincai returned inside with the others. He asked the monk to entertain the guests in the outer room while he went to take inventory. Inside the trunk had been seven pieces of fine fur clothing, fifteen taels of gold, and two hundred taels of silver. The casket had contained over thirty taels of broken silver, two taels of opium, and several small jade ornaments. The clothing bundle had held a few heavy fur garments. Fortunately, the silver he had earned from Fu Third, along with some bank notes, had been stored elsewhere and remained untouched. Calculating the losses, it amounted to over a thousand taels. Pincai hastily drafted a list and brought it out for everyone to see. Seeing that Pincai was preoccupied, the guests found it inconvenient to linger. Furthermore, the mao hour had already begun, so they all decided to take their leave. By now the city gates had opened, and Fu Third and Yang Eight were also preparing to return. While their carriages were being harnessed outside, they saw Rongguan arriving in a cart.","Fu Third's servant quipped, “The guests are about to disperse, and only now do you arrive.” Rongguan flicked his sleeves and hurried in. After greeting everyone and noting the imminent departure, he heard Fu Third tease, “What a fine famous gentleman! Invited for the fourteenth, yet you only show up on the fifteenth.” Seeing Tianxiang and Cuiguan, Rongguan sneered, “Since everyone is about to scatter and go back, I should too. I still need to call the barbers to shave my head.” With a slight bow, he turned and left on his own. The two barbers felt extremely awkward, and the others had nothing more to say as they each boarded their carriages and departed. The two barbers then came back inside to offer their consolations. Pincai rewarded each with four taels of silver, and they left in high spirits.","The next day, Pincai submitted his report of the stolen goods. The ward officials interrogated the gardener and found that he was genuinely unaware of the circumstances. It turned out the helper, surnamed Cai, was a recent hire from late last year whom the gardener had known for some time. Cai had originally done odd jobs at a cheap meat eatery, but when it closed down, he volunteered to help water the garden and keep the night watch. That night, after going to sleep, he simply vanished. It was indeed a case without any collusion or harboring on the gardener's part. The ward officials questioned him several times, but the story remained the same, so they had no choice but to forward the case to the Ministry. Notices were sent to all nine city districts to strictly apprehend the thief Cai. This thread is suspended for now.","Returning to Wang Xun, Yan Zhongqing, Liu Wenze, and Tian Chunhang—from the thirteenth to the fifteenth, they had all been at Yiyuan, admiring lanterns and drinking wine. Ziyu had also joined them for one day. Remembering how he had first met Qinyan on this very day last year, and feeling as if a whole lifetime had passed since then, he couldn't help but feel a pang of sorrow. During the New Year, the famous performers had frequently hosted one another, maintaining a lively atmosphere for over ten days. On the seventeenth, Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing sent a swift letter to Ziyu.","Upon reading it, Ziyu learned that the following day was Baozhu's birthday. Famous scholars and performers were invited to gather at Baozhu's residence, and Ziyun wanted everyone to assemble at his garden by the chen hour. Ziyu wrote a reply accepting the invitation.","When the next day arrived, Ziyu simply told Lady Yan he was invited for drinks at Yiyuan. Having obtained her permission, he went to Wang Xun's place, and together they proceeded to Yiyuan. That day, Cixian had arranged for everything to be hosted inside the Red Camellia Immortal Pavilion, taking charge of all preparations so Ziyun wouldn't have to worry. This pavilion had been newly constructed the previous year, situated in front of Plum Knoll and behind the Pear Courtyard and Crabapple Spring Enclosure. It had originally been an empty plot with only a single pavilion. Outside the pavilion stood two mystical Yingzhou stones, one over twelve feet high and the other over four feet. A large magnolia tree, its trunk thicker than a man's embrace, leaned against the massive stone. Beside the smaller stone grew a red camellia tree of the 'thousand-tiered rising tower' variety, known as the Baozhu Camellia. Already over six feet tall, it bloomed with a thousand red flowers of unparalleled beauty. Twenty-four rooms had been built right there, enclosing these two flowering trees in the center. Additional magnolias, camellias, and winter jasmines were planted, filling the courtyard with a rich fragrance.","Inside, twelve Flower Deities were carved into the walls. Because the meaning of Baozhu's name resonated so well with this setting, Cixian insisted on hosting the gathering here. Furthermore, dissatisfied with the inability to innovate ordinary banquet dishes, he had personally inspected the wine, food, and utensils three days in advance, determined to make this occasion uniquely distinct from usual gatherings. Very early that day, he had Ziyun's traveling kitchen moved into the side rooms of the Immortal Pavilion. Cixian prescribed a specific cooking method for every single dish, detailing exactly how it should be boiled or roasted, and specifying the precise measurements for oil, salt, soy sauce, and vinegar. He spent an entire day meticulously curating thirty-two distinct dishes.","Several of the famous performers could not attend that day, as they all had private theatrical engagements and couldn't be in two places at once. Besides Baozhu, the attendees were Huifang, Sulan, Yulin, and Shufang—four in total. On the side of the famous scholars, apart from the two hosts of Yiyuan, there were Liu Wenze, Yan Zhongqing, Wang Xun, Tian Chunhang, and Mei Ziyu—five in total. Together, they made twelve. Once all the guests had assembled, Baozhu first bowed in gratitude to everyone.","The weather that day was mild and sunny, with the wind turning to the southeast. Everyone had changed into mid-weight fur garments. The floral fragrance in the garden permeated the air. Ahead, in the Plum Knoll, hundreds of plum branches bloomed in unison, looking just like a snowy jade terrace from a fairy realm.","They all took a stroll through the garden. Ziyu noticed a newly inlaid stone inscription in the corridor of Plum Knoll, engraved with two and a half lines of characters, though the date below was not yet finished. Upon closer inspection, it was a five-character quatrain: 'Spring turns with the passing year; flowers revive like returning souls. I imagine the flower-loving guest, sitting in the moonlight until dusk.' Reading it, Ziyu thought to himself, 'Who wrote this poem? It's just been carved, and seems to express a longing for flowers one cannot see,' which made him feel quite envious. Ziyun and the others also came over to look at the poem. Ziyun asked, 'Yuxiang, what do you think of this poem? Is it good?' Ziyu replied, 'The sentiment is excellent, but why carve only this one poem? It must be a new composition.' Ziyun explained, 'This is Yunong's recent poem longing for Plum Knoll. Yaoqing copied it out for him. It's meant to quench his thirst by gazing at plums, so I had it carved. Truly, the flowers remain but the people have changed—do you still remember last year, brother?' These few words struck a chord in Ziyu's heart, bringing a sudden wave of bittersweet sorrow. He suppressed it, remained silent, and walked away. Zhongqing asked, 'Has Yunong recently learned to write poetry?' Baozhu answered, 'I searched his belongings and found over twenty poems, but he refuses to show them to anyone. This one was discovered by chance.' Everyone sighed collectively before heading back inside. Cixian remarked, 'With twelve of us today, one table is too crowded, but two tables would separate us.' Ziyun suggested, 'If you ask me, let's join two large square tables together; then we can all sit.' The seating was arranged: eight facing east and west, four facing north and south. Wenze, Zhongqing, Wang Xun, Chunhang, Ziyu, Cixian, and Ziyun took the east-west seats, while the upper and lower seats were occupied by Huifang, Sulan, Yulin, Shufang, and Baozhu. Baozhu took the very last seat.","Today, every wine vessel and dish was novel and extraordinary. At the four corners of the table sat four silver wine pots that required no one to pour; the pots would dispense wine automatically, provided one held a cup beneath the spout. Inside the pot was a mechanism featuring a silver counterweight and a silver chain, one end attached to the lid. When filled with wine, turning the lid to the left would activate the counterweight, causing the wine to flow from the spout; once the cup was full, turning the lid to the right would stop the flow. The guests eagerly tested the pots and praised them highly.","Ziyun said, 'Jingyi truly has a remarkable imagination. I don't know how he comes up with these things; he really has a creative mind.' Cixian chuckled, 'This is nothing extraordinary. Shortly, there will be two cups that can actually walk. They will go to whomever you call.' Everyone hurriedly asked, 'Why not bring them out to try?' Cixian replied, 'We'll use them later during the drinking game. There are only two of them. I had the silversmith alter them four or five times, and it took a whole month of effort to perfect them.' Huifang urged, 'Bring them out quickly for us to see. We can drink from them all the same; why must we wait for the game?' Cixian instructed a servant to bring a rosewood box from the room. Inside were two moderately sized gold-plated cups, exquisitely engraved with flowers on the outside, and resting on bases resembling clock dials, with four tiny wheels underneath. Cixian took them out and placed them on the table, but they didn't move. Wenze asked, 'Why aren't they moving?' He gave one a push; it moved slightly and then stopped again. The others couldn't understand the mechanism. Cixian smiled, 'Once you accept to drink a cup, it will walk.' Wenze declared, 'As long as it walks, I will drink a cup.' Cixian placed the cup in front of his self-pouring pot, filled it, and said, 'Please, precious one, turn around and offer Lord Liu a cup.' The cup's four wheels spun, and it rolled straight toward Wenze. Delighted beyond measure, Wenze gently picked it up and drained it in one gulp. He then filled a cup and said, 'Returning the favor, a cup for Lord Xiao.' But the cup suddenly went awry, stopping in front of Wang Xun. Wenze exclaimed, 'Why doesn't it work when I call it?' He brought it back in front of himself and repeated the command, but the cup rolled down to Baozhu and stopped. 'How bizarre,' Wenze muttered. Ziyu observed, 'There must be a hidden trick to this that you haven't grasped.' None of the others could guess the secret. They watched as Cixian retrieved the cup and said again, 'A cup for Lord Liu.' The cup returned right in front of Wenze. Astounded, Wenze said, 'I'll only be impressed if you can make it go to every single person; otherwise, it's just luck.' Cixian replied, 'It must visit everyone at the table.' He then toasted Zhongqing, Wang Xun, Chunhang, Ziyu, and the five performers. The cup rolled back and forth, smoothly and steadily, without spilling a single drop. Everyone was overjoyed and dancing with delight. Yet when others tried to command it, it failed to respond, leaving everyone marveling at the wonder.","Huifang examined the cup from all sides but found absolutely no markings. However, looking into the dial within the base, he noticed an incredibly tiny needle, much like a compass, but stationary. He thought to himself, 'Perhaps it's because of this needle.' He poured a cup of wine, secretly noting the direction the needle was pointing, aimed it at Cixian, and called out: 'A cup of wine for Lord Xiao!' True enough, the cup rolled toward Cixian. Huifang burst out laughing, and the others rejoiced, saying, 'He has seen through the trick.' Cixian laughed, 'What a clever thief, quite formidable indeed.' Wenze immediately asked Huifang for the explanation. Huifang smiled, 'Let me try it one more time before you can believe it.' So he inspected the cup again, noted the position, poured the wine, and announced: 'A cup of wine for Lord Xu!' The cup was delivered right in front of Ziyun. Ziyun chuckled, 'With twelve people here, how is it that only he can figure it out? I simply don't believe it.'","So Ziyun also inspected the underside of the base, poured some wine, and declared, 'A cup for Meixiang!' But the cup mistakenly rolled over to Ziyu, causing everyone to burst into laughter. Ziyun laughed and admitted, 'This really is a bit peculiar; I can't command it either.' Ziyu drank the wine, closely examined the dial mechanism, and, having understood most of it, smiled and said, 'Let me try too and see if it works.' He poured the wine and announced, 'This cup goes to Yaoqing!' The cup rolled directly toward Baozhu, stopping right in front of him, bumping against his chopsticks. Huifang clapped his hands and laughed, 'Now someone else has figured it out.' Ziyu was also very pleased. After Baozhu drank the wine, he said, 'I refuse to be defeated; I must figure this out.' Just as Ziyu picked the cup up to look closer, Baozhu snatched it from his hand and studied it from all angles. Zhongqing asked Ziyu, 'How did you figure it out?' Ziyu replied, 'Let me try it one more time.'","He poured the wine and aimed the cup's marker toward Ziyun. Just as he was about to release it, he suddenly thought, 'It's quite close; I might overshoot.' So he stood up, placed the cup a bit further away, and called out, 'A cup for Lord Xu!' The cup rolled straight to Ziyun. Ziyun marveled and drank the wine. Huifang said to Ziyu, 'You must have thought it would roll far, so you placed it further back. I noticed that Jingyi poured less for short distances and filled it for long distances. I suppose this cup needs to be heavier to travel far.' Ziyu nodded, 'Exactly.' Cixian complained, 'How utterly detestable, he's figured out the weight and distance too.' Wang Xun asked Ziyu, 'Where exactly did you spot the trick?' Ziyu explained, 'You all looked, but only at the outside of the dial, not the inside. Didn't you see the tiny needle inside the dial? Wherever it points is where the cup goes.' Everyone took a look and tried it out; it worked flawlessly every time. They all admired the quick-wittedness of Ziyu and Huifang.",`Cixian declared, 'Today’s elegant gathering must have a drinking game. Qianzhou, since you hold the first seat, propose a game for us all to play.' Wenze agreed, 'Very well. But my games aren't particularly novel, let me think for a moment.' After some thought, he said, 'There are twelve of us today. Let’s pass the cup by reciting a line of Tang poetry containing numbers. The first person recites a line with the word "one," and whoever the count lands on drinks. Then that person recites a line with "two," and so on, continuing in this manner up to twelve. Those who make a mistake must drink a penalty cup, how does that sound?' Everyone agreed, 'Good.' Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, and the others protested, 'This is hard on us; we’ll have to rack our brains, where will we find such convenient numbers to recite?' Wenze replied, 'You can do it; I fear you might know Tang poetry even better than we do. If you can't come up with the number, you simply drink penalty cups equal to that number.' Baozhu asked, 'For instance, if I have to recite twelve and can't, do I have to drink twelve penalty cups?' Wenze confirmed, 'Naturally.' Ziyun interjected, 'That's too much; let's deliberate on it when the time comes. Qianzhou, start the game and let’s see who the cup lands on.' Wenze instructed, 'Those of us sitting on the east side will count from bottom to top, and you on the west side must count from top to bottom, so it flows smoothly.' Cixian added, 'Quite right, please drink the starter cup first.' He poured a cup and walked over to Wenze. Wenze drank it and recited, 'Plum blossoms and willow catkins are newly bright together.' The word 'one' (in 'together' / 'all at once' - 一时) fell on the fifth position, landing on Shufang. Wenze poured a cup and presented it to Shufang. Shufang drank it and admitted, 'For this very first line, I don't even know whose it is.' Baozhu chimed in, 'I remember it’s from Zhao Yanzhao's *Court Poem on Snow on Human Day in the Garden*'s "Encountering Snow in the Garden on Renri, by Imperial Command."' Shufang said, 'Now I must recite a line with "two."' After thinking for a moment, he recited, 'Willows warm and flowers spring in the second month's sky.'`,`Counting two words, it again landed on the fifth person, Cixian. The cup stopped right in front of him. Cixian drank and recited, 'I wish to accompany the phoenix and crane back to the three mountains.' Counting to Zhongqing, he drank the wine, poured another cup, walked over to Chunhang, and recited, 'The silk bed-curtains droop low on all four sides behind the red candle.' Chunhang drank it and praised, 'What a fine line, "The silk bed-curtains droop low on all four sides behind the red candle," incomparably fragrant and alluring.' He drank the wine, immediately poured another, and recited, 'Embroidering five patterns requires adding delicate thread.' The count landed on Baozhu. Baozhu drank the wine and remarked, 'Lines with the word "six" are already rare, and of course it lands on me. I'm afraid I'll have to drink a penalty.' Ziyu encouraged, 'There are indeed lines with "six."'`,`Baozhu thought for a moment and said, 'I haven't calculated whose turn this line will land on.' He recited, 'On the bed, the emerald screen opens six panels.' The count landed on Yulin. Yulin quipped, 'I hope you didn't just make this line up.' Sulan teased, 'You claim to recite poetry every day, yet you don't even remember Lady Huarui's "Palace Lyrics."' Yulin laughed, 'Exactly. I fear he intentionally wanted me to drink.' He drank the cup and said, 'Now for a seven-character line.' After pondering for half a quarter of an hour, he passed it to Wang Xun: 'Before the gate, a carriage of seven fragrances has just arrived.' Wang Xun drank and countered with an eight-character line, 'Sorrow clings to the deep figure-eight brows' from Xue Feng's 'Night Banquet Bestowing a Courtesan.' The count reached Ziyun, who drank and noted, 'Lines with "nine" are likely scarcer, and even if there are some, they might not be good.' After thinking for a while, he recited, 'The jeweled fan welcomes her return to the nine-flowered canopy.' The count landed on Sulan. Sulan drank the wine and offered a ten-character line: 'The beautiful lady in the boudoir is just over ten years old.' The count fell to Shufang, who complained, 'It’s landed on me twice now.' He had no choice but to drink, saying, 'Fortunately, I remember the line, "In the eleventh month, the night of the winter solstice is long."' He then turned to Baozhu, 'You drink a cup!' Baozhu replied, 'You must drink a cup yourself; the number ten is still on you.'`,`Shufang had no choice but to drink a cup. Baozhu drank his, thought for a moment, and recited a line: 'On the southern path, the green towers stand twelve tiers high.' It landed on Ziyu. Ziyu drank the wine and asked, 'We're already at twelve, do we still need to continue?' Cixian replied, 'It hasn't landed on Meixiang yet. Once it reaches him, we can change the game. For now, we must proceed to thirteen.' Ziyu recited a line: 'Graceful and slender, she is just over thirteen.' It landed on Zhongqing. Zhongqing drank the wine, pondered for a moment, and said, 'With this count, whoever it lands on will have to drink. Let's wait for Meixiang to recite a line and conclude the game. Lines with numbers in the teens are quite rare.' He then recited, '"The flower-faced maid is thirteen or fourteen." Yaoqing and Meixiang, each drink a cup. Meixiang, recite a line to finish the game.' Huifang objected, 'Actually, it shouldn't land on me; it should be Duxiang's turn.' Ziyun urged, 'Just recite it.' Huifang thought for a moment and said, 'Fortunately, I remember this line. Jingyi and Gengxiang, both drink a cup.' He then recited, 'The fifteenth of the first month is the most romantic.' Cixian approved, 'Very good.' He and Ziyu drank their wine, concluding the game. They then enjoyed a variety of dishes and snacks.`,`After chatting a bit more, Cixian said, 'I have another drinking game that requires a bit more effort. But since everyone here today seems to enjoy playing, I suspect you won't find it too tedious. Let's play a round of this one.' Huifang warned, 'Don't bring out something from *Water Margin* to mock people again.' Cixian laughed, 'Do you still remember the "Teasing the Uncle in the Snow" trick? That day was certainly enough for you.' He then ordered a page boy to fetch the third tube of ivory lots from the bookshelf. Moments later, the boy brought it out. The guests saw an ivory cylinder filled to the brim with small lots, along with one large lot. Cixian pulled out the large lot to show everyone; it was a game based on the names of a hundred beauties. Carved into the large lot were the words 'A Hundred Beauties Offering the Cup' in clerical script.`,`Below the title were several lines of rules: 'This game uses the names of a hundred beauties, totaling one hundred lots, categorized by astronomy, geography, seasons, flora, and so forth. Each person draws a lot and notes the beauty's name and its category. First, they must compose two lines of Tang poetry. The first line must embed the first character of the beauty's name, and the second line must embed the second character. If the tonal patterns are discordant or the poetic rhythm mismatched, the player drinks three penalty cups and draws again; if the lines are excellent, everyone toasts them with a cup. After the Tang poetry, each player must match a "flying flower." They will compose two lines from the *Mao Shi* (*Mao Shi*). If the first character of the first line and the first character of the second line combine to form a flower name, it is a "paired-head flower," and the player drinks two cups while those seated beside them toast two cups.`,`Further rules read: 'If the last characters of both lines combine to form a flower name, it is a "paired-stem flower." The player drinks two cups, and the person sitting directly opposite toasts two cups. If the last character of the first line and the first character of the second line form a flower name, it is an "intertwined flower." The player drinks two cups, and the neighbors on either side each toast one cup. If the flower name's characters appear symmetrically in the middle of both lines, it is a "bud-contained flower." The player drinks half a cup, and the youngest person present toasts half a cup. If the characters forming the flower name are in mismatched positions, it is a "staggered flower." The player drinks one cup, and those sitting one seat away toast one cup. If a flower name is formed, but the imagery or category linkage is poorly executed, a penalty of three cups is imposed. Each beauty lot has specific allowable flowers inscribed upon it; substituting them is not permitted.' Upon hearing the rules, everyone declared it extremely difficult. Baozhu and Huifang said, 'We won't be able to play this. It's better if only you seven gentlemen play.' Zhongqing remarked, 'The hardest part is matching the flower names from the *Mao Shi*. Composing the two Tang lines shouldn't be much harder than our previous numbers game.'`,`Ziyu noted, 'There are indeed some flower names that can be found in the *Mao Shi*, although finding matches for the paired-head and paired-stem varieties will be quite challenging.' Wang Xun said, 'Racking our brains might at least help us digest our food. But forcing the performers to navigate the *Mao Shi* would indeed be too much to ask.' Ziyun proposed a compromise: 'How about we split up? The seven of us scholars will play this "bitter" and difficult game, while the five performers play a "sweet" and easier one. Listening to their lines might even inspire us scholars when we get stuck.' Everyone agreed and asked what game the five performers would play. Ziyun replied, 'I have one in mind,' and ordered a servant to bring two dice and a small dish.`,`Ziyun explained the dice symbols: 'One is the moon, two is a star, three is a goose, four is a person, five is a plum, and six is heaven. If someone rolls a one and a two, they must recite two lines of opera lyrics—one containing "moon" and the other "star"—ensuring the meaning and rhythm connect. If the two lines link naturally, everyone toasts a double cup; if they merely come from the same aria set, everyone toasts one cup. If the lines are poorly chosen, the penalty is one cup. Furthermore, the order matters: if one rolls "moon" first but recites a line with "star" first, there's a penalty. The other combinations follow the same logic. If doubles are rolled (like two persons or two heavens), the lines must include reduplicated characters (such as "人人" or "天天") for a bonus; failing to do so incurs a three-cup penalty.' Huifang and Baozhu repeated the rules, noting it wasn't easy, though they still had many lyrics committed to memory. Ziyun then instructed Cixian to have Xiangwan and Peixian switch seats so the performers could roll the dice in one designated area, and the seating was adjusted accordingly.`,`Cixian shuffled the lots and handed the tube to Wenze to draw first. Wang Xun asked, 'Why not have everyone draw their lots first and then proceed in order?' Cixian replied, 'That would be too easy. People would have too much time to overthink and avoid penalties.' Wenze’s lot was for the "Adornment" category, featuring the beauty "Yuhuan" (Jade Ring). It required two seven-character Tang lines, followed by a paired-head flower from the *Mao Shi*. Wenze paced back and forth, remarking, 'This feels less like a drinking game and more like a literary examination.' Cixian countered, 'The sooner you submit your lines, the better.' After the time it takes to drink a cup of tea, Wenze sat down and recited: 'Under the jeweled-hook curtains, shadows sink and sink; the ringed pendants return empty, a moonlit soul alone.' (Embedding "玉" for jade and "环" for ring).`,`Everyone praised the lines as excellent. Cixian added, 'Placing "yu" and "huan" at the very beginning of the lines perfectly matches the paired-head flower requirement. Now, what is your paired-head flower from the *Mao Shi*?' They first toasted him one cup.`,`Wenze drank the wine and said, 'The first character is "ji" (鸡 - rooster), and the next is "guan" (冠 - crest), making "jiguan" (cockscomb). My paired-head flower is from the *Mao Shi*.' Cixian asked for the source lines. Wenze thought for a moment. Because his wine cup featured a chicken pattern, he improvised from the *Mao Shi*: '"The rooster (ji) has crowed; the crest (guan) and tassel stand in pair"—thus "cockscomb" as the paired-head flower.' Since his seatmate was Jiantan, Jiantan was required to toast two cups. Zhongqing interjected, 'Wenze should drink his own two cups first.' Wenze gladly complied. As Zhongqing drew the next lot, Wenze reminded him of his toast. Zhongqing retorted, 'Your lines weren't smoothly linked and hardly deserve celebration.' Ziyu mediated, '"Cockscomb" technically fits, though the coherence is weak.' Wenze challenged the group to produce a better line for "guan," then suggested another himself. But Zhongqing argued it still failed the paired-head criteria. Wenze admitted his error. Cixian mediated, 'Let's just offer one courtesy toast.' Zhongqing drank and then read his own drawn lot: the category was astronomy, the beauty was "Chaoyun" (Morning Cloud), requiring two seven-character Tang lines and a paired-stem flower from the *Mao Shi*. After some thought, he combined a line from Wei Zhuang and one from Du Mu: '"Morning after morning, evening after evening below Yangtai; cloud-and-rain on the deserted terrace—is it but a dream-thought?"' He then provided a phrase from the *Mao Shi* for "wife and husband in concord," interpreting "night-blooming flower" (夜合) as his paired-stem flower.`,`Everyone praised Zhongqing's effort. Cixian noted, 'The flower matches your Tang lines perfectly. We should all toast one cup, and Meixiang, sitting opposite, should toast two.' Huifang had just rolled a two and a five and was still contemplating his lines when asked to drink the toasts. After drinking, he delivered his two lyric lines: '"Not even half a star of pity for jade or fragrance—fit only to keep to reed windows and thatched plum-curtains."' Ziyu clapped his hands and praised its tender elegance, remarking that the adapted pairing felt even better than the original context. Wenze pointed out that the lines came from *Visiting Su* in a specific aria sequence and deserved another toast. Back on the scholars’ side, Wang Xun had drawn the category of birds, with the beauty "Feiyan" (Flying Swallow), requiring a paired-stem flower. His composition was progressing slowly. Meanwhile, Sulan rolled a one and a four. After deep thought, he recited, '"The moon is bright, the clouds thin, the flowers heavy with dew; in which palace of Penglai does the beloved dwell?"' Chunhang praised it as even finer. Ziyu observed, 'The performers' lines are more fragrant and sensual than ours.' Sulan modestly replied, 'The scholars have harder constraints with the person's name and the specific flower type.' Shufang chimed in, 'I can hardly compose even by borrowing lines from what has just been spoken. I'm lucky not to be in the hard game.' Wang Xun still didn't have an answer, so Cixian identified Huifang’s lines as being from *Flirting with a Zither* and called for everyone to toast one more cup.`,`Yulin rolled a two and a four and recited two lines from *Hearing the Bells*: '"A lone goose in the vast sky deepens the grief; beneath Emei few travelers pass."' Everyone agreed it was good, though Ziyun noted the mood was rather bleak; nevertheless, they each toasted one cup. Over on the hard side, Wang Xun finally produced his lines, combining Pei Qianyu and what he thought was Wen Tingyun: '"The jade hairpin sways, twin phoenixes fly; where the swallow-pin falls, the silence grows soft."' Ziyun and Wenze praised the seamless linkage. Wang Xun then offered "paired-stem peony" from the *Mao Shi*. Before anyone could respond, Zhongqing joked, 'Half the dishes haven't even been served yet, and the roast pig has already appeared.' People looked around in confusion. Wang Xun asked, 'Where is there a roast pig?' Zhongqing laughed, 'Even if you want to eat roast pig, your phrase "presented at the hour of the boar, its color like rich cinnabar" sounds exactly like a description of roast pork color, doesn't it?' Everyone burst out laughing, including Wang Xun himself. Cixian corrected him: 'Yong'an, I think you got one character wrong in your second line, or perhaps it's an error in your edition. I remember it as "where the jade pin falls," not "swallow pin." Furthermore, the source is Li He’s *Song of a Beauty Combing Her Hair*, not Wen Tingyun. You should be penalized one cup.' Wang Xun conceded, 'I got the author's name wrong, but it seems I didn't misremember the "swallow" character.' Chunhang mediated, 'Perhaps other anthologies use the "swallow" character. In any case, the couplet itself is good.' Thus, they all toasted him a cup.`,`Just then, Baozhu rolled double twos and recited: '"Tonight, in this desolation, there are four stars."' Everyone enthusiastically praised it: 'This line is truly clever and effortless.' They each toasted a cup. Chunhang had drawn a lot from the colors category, featuring the beauty "Hongfu" (Red Whisk), with the required flower being an "intertwined flower." After a moment's thought, he recited his lines, using Wei Zhuang for the first and Du Fu for the second, combining to: '"A thousand, ten thousand branches in red spring brilliance; the fishing rod nearly brushes the coral boughs."' His flower name came from the lines, '"Long and far-reaching, the spring days are bright." Everlasting spring (长春) is the intertwined flower.' The group offered words of praise and toasted him a cup. Shufang then rolled a one and a four, immediately reciting: '"The moon shifts the flower-shadows—one suspects a jade beauty arrives."' Everyone exclaimed, 'This line is natural and absolutely excellent; it deserves a double toast.' And so they all drank.`,`Ziyu drew a lot from the geography category: the beauty "Luo Shen" (Goddess of the Luo River) and a paired-head flower. He thought of two lines but felt they weren't quite right. Just as he was about to think of new ones, he saw Huifang roll a one and a three. Huifang paused, then recited two lines from *Stealing the Poem*: '"Hating my sleeplessness by the waning moon west of the window; harder still to hear the lone goose cry."' Ziyu praised him, 'Truly a brocade heart and an embroidered mouth. You put us scholars to shame.' Ziyun added, 'Even if we were playing this game, we wouldn't come up with such fine lines.' Everyone offered a full toast. Ziyu, having finalized his lines, announced: 'I'll use one line from Leng Chaoyang's *Sending Red Thread* and one from Meng Haoran's *Ascending the Xiangyang City Tower*, combining them as: "Like the Goddess of Luo riding the mist away; gazing still as the river deity plays with pearls."' As soon as Ziyu finished, Cixian, Zhongqing, Chunhang, and the others lauded him: 'Of all the lines recited so far, this is the first and best. It's wonderfully balanced and graceful. Such tender and romantic charm is exactly Yuxiang’s style.' Ziyu then provided his paired-head flower: '"When the moon rises bright, the young lady longs in hunger." The moon-season rose (月季) is the paired-head flower.' Everyone cheered, 'This flower name is also excellent. We must toast three cups to properly reward these beautiful lines.' Ziyu modestly deflected the praise. Sulan was then seen rolling a one and a six. After a moment of thought, he pieced together two lines from *The Tavern*: '"Suddenly Chang'e appears in the moon palace, surpassing even Qiuchi's little heaven."' Everyone agreed it was wonderful, and again, they all toasted.`,`Cixian drew a lot from the seasons category: the beauty "Yelai" (Night-Coming) and a paired-stem flower. Ziyun suggested, 'Take your time to think; let's order some more dishes first.' They enjoyed a few more courses, and five or six new dishes were brought out. Once the lamps were lit, everyone relaxed in their seats. Cixian announced, 'I have my lines. One from Bai Juyi and one from Li Bai, combining to: "In the eighth and ninth months, the nights are long indeed; the lover says he will come, yet he does not."' The group praised it with sighs of admiration: 'Such seasoned maturity, just like "I wish to accompany the phoenix and crane back to the three mountains." It truly has your distinct voice.' Cixian cautioned, 'Don't be too quick to praise; I fear I might be penalized for the flower name. I'm using an alternative name, though it's not obscure; it's one everyone commonly uses.' He recited: '"Having seen the gentleman, on the auspicious gengwu day." The ziwu flower (子午花) is my paired-stem flower. Today happens to be a gengwu day, so my words are quite fitting.' His companions praised him endlessly, each toasting three cups.`,`Yulin rolled a four and a five. After contemplating for a while, he recited two lines from *The Pavilion of Gossamer*: '"For the one in my heart, the old heart-sickness stirs. The coy Lord of Spring, his spring-heart leans toward the small plum twigs."' Huifang smiled and commented, 'This excerpt from *The Pavilion of Gossamer* is much better than your earlier *Hearing the Bells*.' Thus, everyone toasted two cups. Ziyun announced, 'It's my turn to show my inadequacy.' He drew a lot from the flora category: the beauty "Lianxiang" (Lotus Fragrance) and an intertwined flower. Desiring to produce truly fine lines, he didn't speak hastily, polishing his thoughts while watching the others roll the dice. He saw Baozhu roll a two and a four, and after a moment's thought, Baozhu recited a lyric from *Spring Sleep*: '"Starry eyes rub with fatigue; a whole sheet of beauty-fragrance blends."' Ziyun praised, 'Excellent! That deserves a toast.' Everyone raised a cup. Shufang rolled a one and a two, paused to think, and recited, '"The moon climbs the eastern wall—most lovely when the stars are clear and the moon is bright."' Ziyun again praised, 'Good! That deserves a cup.' Everyone drank. Wenze teased Ziyun, 'It's time you submitted your own lines. You just keep watching others turn theirs in. Could it be that your mental draft isn't finished yet?' Ziyun chuckled, 'Almost done.' He then watched Huifang roll a one and a four. After half a quarter of an hour, Huifang recited two lines from *Stealing the Song*: '"The mountain enters the cold sky, the moon's shadow crosses; by the balustrade, a jade beauty leans at leisure."' Ziyun praised, 'Even better! That deserves a double cup. And now, I am ready to submit my lines. I will use two lines from Wen Tingyun's *Lotus-Picking Song*, combining them to: "Green duckweed, golden millet, the lotus stems are short; the dew is heavy, the flowers many, the fragrance does not fade."' Everyone voiced their approval. Cixian remarked, 'These two lines are quite beautiful. It's just a pity that the character "not" (不) and "stem" (茎) don't form a perfect poetic match.'`,`Baozhu cast a thoughtful glance at Ziyun. Cixian continued, 'Even without a perfect match on those two characters, his lines are just as worthy of a three-cup toast as Yuxiang's. But let's settle for Ziyun and the rest of us drinking two cups.' Ziyun then announced his flower name: '"In the south there are lofty trees; the hibiscus and shepherd's purse are as sweet as syrup." The wood-hibiscus (木槿) is my intertwined flower.' Everyone agreed, 'These two lines flow naturally and deserve a two-cup toast.' By this point in the day, everyone's minds were tired, and they were all eager to eat dinner. Ziyun said, 'It's still early. Let's watch them roll the dice a few more times. After all, their game requires less mental strain than ours.' Sulan rolled double fours and instantly recalled a line from *Peeking at the Bath*: '"Two people become one set of intestines and stomach."' Zhongqing slammed his hand on the table in sheer delight, exclaiming, 'This is heavenly inspiration! We must quickly toast three cups.' And so the entire table toasted another three cups. Yulin rolled double threes and recited a line from *The Small Banquet*: '"Across the vast sky, rows of new geese."' Cixian remarked, 'Their lines are getting better and better; truly, theirs are better than ours.' Wang Xun agreed, 'Fine words come from the mouths of beauties—it is indeed true.' Chunhang added, 'They are also remarkably quick-witted; we have no choice but to concede defeat.' Wenze pointed out, 'It's impressive how perfectly fitting every single one of their lines is. None of them have been penalized a single cup, yet they've earned dozens of toasts.' Ziyu said, 'I said from the beginning that we couldn't match them. If they played our game, I suspect they would still outdo us. But it's about time; we can conclude the game and eat.' Ziyun suggested, 'Let's wait until their turn finishes before stopping. They've also racked their brains to win these celebratory toasts.'`,"When it was Baozhu's turn, he rolled double twos and recited a line from *The Secret Vow*: 'Ask the twin stars—morning and evening, how can they compare to you and me?' Everyone praised it as wonderful and toasted him a cup. They all looked at Baozhu and smiled. Baozhu, without realizing it, blushed slightly, which only made everyone laugh even more. He had no choice but to lower his head with a faint smile. Soon it was Shufang's turn again. Since everyone had now rolled three times, it was time to conclude the game. He rolled double fours and recited a lyric from *Peeking at the Bath*: 'The one in the heart, the heart among people.' Everyone highly praised him: 'This conclusion wraps everyone present today right into the phrase; we are all the ones in the heart, and the hearts among people. We should each toast six cups according to the word count, and then have dinner.' Everyone happily drank their wine, finished their meal, rinsed their mouths, and changed their clothes. The clock already showed it was nearing the end of the hai hour, so everyone prepared to disperse. They bowed farewell to the host, and the host, along with the five performers, escorted them straight to the garden gate. The five performers then returned inside, chatted for a while longer, and finally went their separate ways.","Cixian said to Ziyun, 'Tomorrow I will have the rules of these two games engraved and circulated outside. It will encourage people to use their minds a bit, saving them from the endless, rowdy brawling of finger-guessing games.' Ziyun agreed, 'That would be good. Besides, there was nothing improper in our games today.' After talking a bit more, Ziyun also went inside. As for what happened next, you must listen to the following chapter to find out."]},m=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations35:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),o={36:["The last chapter told of Baozhu’s birthday and their joyful day at Yi Garden, showing how the joys and sorrows of human life vary. Meanwhile, at the Hua estate, on the day of the Lantern Festival, Young Master Hua had ordered Qinyan and Baling to perform a play. Qinyan, feeling unwell and grieving over the past, wore a desolate expression and lacked any spirit for the performance.","On stage that day, moved by the tender sentiments of the play, he could not help but weep real tears. Young Master Hua, assuming he was mourning for no reason, was deeply displeased; he called Qinyan down and sharply reprimanded him, not summoning him again for several days. Qinyan remained alone in his room without visitors. Finding the tempers of the servants and attendants incompatible, he felt entirely out of place.","Behind the wing of the Lingering Green Cottage, he occupied a small three-room suite with a single servant to wait on him. In the courtyard stood a few Taihu rocks, two green-calyx plum trees, and one red plum tree, still in full, brilliant bloom.","It was the twenty-seventh day of the first month. Facing the plum blossoms, Qinyan could not help but long for the Plum Blossom Ridge of Yi Garden. He recalled how Duxiang had treated him; compared to the present day, they were worlds apart. Even if the servants fell short in their duties, Duxiang not only refrained from showing anger but harbored no resentment, instead offering every comfort and consideration. On the other hand, when Young Master Hua was in a good mood, he seemed somewhat like Duxiang, but the moment things displeased him, he would grow irritated and fault-finding. On the Lantern Festival, he had even resorted to scolding, treating Qinyan like any other servant. The sycophantic attendants rejoiced in this, as if feeling that Qinyan had finally met his downfall and would sink into the cycle of reincarnation, never to rise again. Every time the master summoned someone, the others hated him; every time the master gave a reward, the others cursed him. Their mocking, sarcastic remarks were hard to bear. He deeply resented that scoundrel Xi Eleven for causing trouble at his door for no reason, which had led to his current plight. He also thought of Wei Pincai; though not a good man, he had at least occasionally spoken words that touched Qinyan's heart, yet now he was gone too. Then there was Lin Shanzhi, who acted like half a master; one had to flatter him carefully to please him, otherwise he would stir up trouble. “If only they would just cast me out entirely, I would be free,” Qinyan thought. “Then, naturally, I wouldn't have to return to my teacher's place either. If Heaven preserves me and lets me live a clean life, I would gladly endure hunger and cold for the rest of my days. Otherwise, people will say I was first an actor, then a servant—how would that sound? Would anyone still respect me?” The more he thought about it, the angrier he grew, and tears fell naturally. Sitting all alone under the plum tree, he grieved for a long time. Then he heard Lin Shanzhi’s voice calling, “Yunong!” as he walked in. Qinyan stood up. Seeing his sorrowful face, Shanzhi asked, “Do you know already?” Qinyan, confused, asked, “Know what?” Shanzhi replied, “Your teacher has died. Someone just came to deliver the news and informed the young master, who told you to go back for the encoffining.” Hearing this, Qinyan felt a wave of sorrow and wept continuously. “When did he die?” he asked. Shanzhi said, “The messenger said he wasn’t sick; he went to sleep last night and was found dead this morning.” Qinyan grieved a while longer, then asked, “How am I to go back?” Shanzhi told him, “Someone is waiting for you outside. The young master ordered you not to delay too long; return once the funeral matters are settled.” Qinyan thought for a moment and agreed. After Shanzhi left, Qinyan had the servant pack a bundle of clothes, roll up his bedding, and took a pouch of silver. Locking his door, he stepped out. Poor Qinyan did not even know the way; the servant pointed the direction. Passing the gatehouse, he was glad to see everyone already knew, so no one asked questions. Reaching the main gate, he saw a carriage waiting by the screen wall—the very carriage he always took. He also saw Wu Mazi, his teacher’s wife's cousin, who had come along. Qinyan went up to greet him, and the two boarded the carriage, talking all the way out of the city.","As they neared the door, a group of people was already setting up a mourning canopy. Qinyan entered and went straight in. Tianshou ran out, saw him, and ran back in. Inside, he could hear his teacher’s wife weeping bitterly. Approaching the bed, Qinyan saw his teacher already dressed in grave clothes, a cloth covering his face. Overcome with bitter grief, he knelt by the bed and wept profusely. His teacher’s wife pulled him up and urged him to stop crying. “What illness did he catch to die so suddenly?” Qinyan asked. She replied, “He wasn't ill at all. He was perfectly fine last night. He smoked opium until after midnight, talked a great deal before sleeping, but when I woke up and touched him, he was cold. If it was coal gas, why am I perfectly fine?” Qinyan asked about the funeral arrangements. She sighed, “Your teacher earned money his whole life, but heaven knows where he spent it. By the new year, things were already tight.” Sighing again, she added, “It’s far worse than when you were here. Your two junior brothers are idle eight days out of ten; I can’t think of a way forward. Your teacher died so suddenly he left no final words, and he never told me about his affairs outside. Now I know nothing of who owes us or whom we owe. Those two houses beyond the alley won’t fetch much rent. We’ve managed to put together the grave clothes, coffin, and canopy, but when it comes to buying a burial plot and arranging the funeral, things will likely get very tight.” Sighing, Qinyan walked to his old room, had the servant set things up, and opened the silver pouch he had brought. It contained fifteen ingots of various sizes, weighing perhaps fifty or sixty taels. He took it in and gave it to her, saying, “I don’t know exactly how much this is—it’s the New Year’s reward from the young master and his wife. It can help with the expenses.” She took it, weighed it in her hand, untied it to count, and said, “I heard you have a high-ranking position in the Hua estate. Have you managed to save much? I know you’re inexperienced; don’t let people cheat you. You must be careful and save a little. I have no sons or daughters; I’ll have to rely on you in the future.” Qinyan replied, “The young master mostly gives small trinkets. The silver rewards are few, and I have no use for them anyway. If I get more in the future, I will bring them to you.” She nodded, “That’s good. You’re a conscientious child.” She placed the silver in a drawer, and Qinyan went out.","Outside, amidst the bustling crowd, Wu Mazi brought in a bundle of mourning clothes. Then Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, and Lu Sulan arrived. Qinyan quickly welcomed them, and they sat down together.","They first asked about his teacher’s passing, then inquired about his life since the New Year. Qinyan briefly recounted recent events. Baozhu asked, “Since you’re back, how many days of leave did you get?” Qinyan replied, “Lin Shanzhi came to tell me this morning. I didn’t see the young master myself. He only said to return after the funeral, without specifying how many days.” Sulan said, “You must ask for at least a month’s leave and wait until after the burial to return; otherwise, you’ll be letting your teacher’s wife down.” Qinyan agreed. Huifang added, “You might as well ask for a long leave and just not go back. After all, you didn’t sell yourself to them.” Baozhu reasoned, “It has its advantages, though it’s a bit restrictive. And without any close confidants among your peers, it must be lonely.” Huifang scoffed, “Lin Shanzhi was nothing back in the day, but now he puts on such grand airs when he sees us. He’s forgotten how we used to sing in the same troupe. When he sang random tunes, he was so slick, coaxing those who didn’t know theater and making a little dirty money. Since entering the Hua estate, he acts like an official, looking down on his peers. If he occasionally speaks to us, it’s with the tone of an elder. In truth, we’re the same age—he’s no older than I am.” Qinyan sighed, “He was an apprentice here too, yet today he said something absurd to me: ‘Your teacher has died.’ Just because you’ve finished your apprenticeship, does that mean he’s no longer your teacher?” Baozhu laughed bitterly, “He now wants us to call him ‘Third Master.’ If we call him ‘Third Brother,’ he barely acknowledges us. He only dares to throw his weight around and act the veteran in front of Baling. Don’t you remember when Wang Jingfang almost beat him up at the Yanqiu Hall? Now, when he sees Jingfang, he completely ignores him, still holding a grudge.” Qinyan mused, “Young Master Hua’s temper isn’t entirely eccentric, but it’s unpredictable. Yet whenever Shanzhi is summoned, whatever he says is right; he never hits a snag. I suppose it’s just his luck. He’s truly adaptable; there’s never a question he can’t answer. You have to give him credit for that.” Sulan noted, “I heard that everyone in their household flatters him, even the servants whose families have served for three generations. Wei Pincai calls him ‘Third Brother,’ ‘Old Third,’ or ‘Third Grand Master.’”","Qinyan asked, “Wei Pincai has moved out. Do you know if he’s with Yuxiang?” Huifang scoffed, “Wei Pincai? He’s even more arrogant now. He lives at Hongji Temple and causes trouble every day with a bunch of scoundrels like Xi Eleven, Pan Third, and Yang Eight—barbers, monks big and small—gambling and visiting prostitutes. It’s a disgrace. Even Zhang Zhongyu no longer associates with him.” When Qinyan asked about Ziyu, Baozhu recounted, “The day before yesterday, we spent the whole day together at Yi Garden.” He described how they drank, played games, and used Cixian’s newly designed wine pots and cups, making Qinyan deeply envious. He also mentioned the poem Duxiang had engraved and described Yuxiang’s sorrowful expression upon seeing it, detailing every bit to Qinyan, which moved Qinyan to melancholy as well. Huifang suggested, “Since you’re back, we must find a few days to gather properly. Would tomorrow be alright?” Baozhu confirmed, “He has no obligations tomorrow either.” But Qinyan demurred, “My teacher has just died. It’s contrary to propriety; we should wait a few days.” Sulan countered, “If we wait a few days, you’ll have to return to the city. Besides, a quiet gathering for conversation and relaxation won’t hurt anything. You’re not a filial son in formal mourning—what are you afraid of?” Baozhu promised, “I’ll go ask Duxiang; tomorrow or the day after will both work.” The three sat for a long time, but when they tried to leave, Qinyan clung to them, and, reluctant to part, they sat back down. Later, Wang Guibao, Li Yulin, and Jin Shufang arrived. They all stayed until the coffin was sealed, paid their respects, and then departed. Since Qinyan was wearing mourning robes, it seemed improper to go out the next day, so they agreed to meet again in three days. He also sent Wu Mazi to the Hua estate to ask Shanzhi to request a one-month leave for him, waiting until after the burial to return to the city. Young Master Hua approved and sent back a chest of clothes, which finally put Qinyan’s mind at ease.","On the third day of mourning rites, many people came. Jin San and Ye Maolin were invited to help manage things. Aside from actors in the same troupe, members of other troupes, neighbors, and restaurant owners crowded the house, only dispersing after the paper offerings were burned. Qinyan, exhausted, returned to his room and slept.","The next morning, Baozhu sent a message: “We originally planned for today, but Duxiang has matters to attend to, so we’ve changed the gathering at Yi Garden to tomorrow morning.” Qinyan agreed. After washing up, he sat alone in thought: “Since I have nothing to do today, why not go see Yuxiang?” Remembering Lady Mei’s kind treatment last year, he figured there would be no harm in going. Mind made up, he changed into plain clothes, ordered the carriage to be readied, and told his teacher’s wife he was going to thank his fellow actors. When he reached the outer courtyard, however, he hesitated: “It’s been half a year. Before, Pincai took me, so I didn’t have to speak to the gatekeepers. If I go alone now, even if the lady treats me well and invites me in, I still have to beg the gatekeepers. What if they refuse me entry? Besides Yun’er, I don’t know anyone there.” He pondered but couldn’t decide, standing frozen. The servant came in and asked, “The carriage is ready. Where to?” Qinyan remained silent, thought a bit more, and said to himself, “I should go find Pincai and have him take me, saving a lot of explanation. Since he’s moved out, it’s only right I visit him, and he’ll appreciate the gesture.”","He told the servant, “I’ll go to Hongji Temple first to see Master Wei.” He went out and boarded the carriage; the servant climbed onto the shaft. After a few turns and less than a mile, they arrived. Qinyan saw a large, fine carriage with a sleeping driver resting at the temple gate. Assuming it was Pincai’s, he felt relieved to have arrived before he went out. The servant went in to ask and reported, “He’s home and invites you in.” Qinyan stepped down and entered the east gate. Following the servant’s directions, he passed two halls and entered a courtyard behind the eastern corridor. Keeping his head down, he paid no attention to his surroundings until he reached Pincai’s courtyard. Pincai’s servant, Si’er, came out, nodded to him, and opened the wind-door. When Qinyan looked up, he was startled—the room was full of people. His heart pounded, and his face flushed. He wanted to retreat, but Pincai was already coming out to welcome him. Gathering his wits, Qinyan stepped forward to greet him. Pincai exclaimed, “What brings you here today? I could never have dreamed it.” Blushing, Qinyan couldn’t find the words. Pincai turned to the room, saying, “This is the famous Master Du I talk about every day; he’s now called Master Du Qin.” To Qinyan, he added, “These are my closest friends: Master Xi, Third Master Pan, my landlord Monk Tang, and his little novice. Those two are from the troupe; you probably don’t know them, so let’s all get acquainted.” With no choice, Qinyan bowed to them. The monk, knowing he came from the Hua estate, pressed his hands together, bowed repeatedly, and smiled broadly, saying, “Too polite, too polite! Please sit, Master Qin.” Pan Third merely bowed shallowly; Qinyan, focused on the monk, didn’t notice. Pan Third, his lust already roused, took the chance to step forward and grabbed Qinyan’s hand. Qinyan tried to pull away but couldn’t. Pan Third, bearing his teeth, his eyes red and staring, smiled lasciviously and said, “You are Master Qin, my grand Master Qin! I’ve longed to meet you but couldn’t. Today is truly a fated encounter from a thousand miles away.” Ashamed and furious, Qinyan desperately yanked his hand free. Knowing Qinyan was shy and prone to tears when anxious, Pincai quickly pushed Pan Third away and pulled Qinyan down to sit. Before Pincai could ask anything, Xi Eleven spoke up: “Are you doing well in the Hua estate?” Qinyan had to reply, “Yes.” Xi Eleven asked, “Do you recognize me?” Qinyan looked up and saw a dark, imposing man; feeling somewhat intimidated, he said, “We are not acquainted.” Xi Eleven burst into laughter and stepped close to Qinyan. Qinyan tried to stand, but Xi Eleven pressed both hands heavily on his shoulders. Qinyan lowered his head, his heart racing. Xi Eleven continued, “You should thank me. Last summer I came looking for you. You were clearly home but wouldn’t come out to see me. Later, when I argued with your teacher, you ran out the back door, and from there you entered the Hua estate. Didn’t I make that happen for you? Now that we meet, you ought to thank me.” Realizing this was Xi Eleven, Qinyan grew even more panicked; he leaned away, stood up, and hastily retreated. Xi Eleven laughed loudly, “You’re not that young anymore. Why are you so bashful, acting like a little girl?” Fearing Xi Eleven would get rough, Pincai explained, “The rules in the Hua estate are very strict. He hasn’t seen a stranger in a year, so naturally, he’s restrained.” Meanwhile, Pan Third was scratching his ears and rubbing his cheeks, drooling with desire but not daring to act, while Monk Tang could only harbor wild fantasies. Pincai asked Qinyan, “How did you manage to come out today?” Qinyan explained his teacher had died and he was on a month’s leave. “I came to see you today, and I also want you to go with me to…” He was too embarrassed to finish, but Pincai understood and asked, “Where do you want me to go with you?” Qinyan finally said, “I want you to go with me to see Lady Mei and Yuxiang.” Pincai smiled, nodded, and said, “Of course, of course. We’ll go in a little while.” Seeing so many people present, Qinyan couldn’t urge him.","Xi Eleven was a coarse and vulgar man who cared only for raw desires, but he could easily distinguish a beautiful face. Seeing Qinyan now, he felt he had laid eyes on an unprecedented treasure, and his heart was deeply stirred.","But considering Qinyan had left the profession and was now a personal attendant in the Hua estate, Xi knew he couldn't just casually lay hands on him, nor would Qinyan ever agree to keep him company over wine. He had to think of a way to have his sport with him. As he watched and listened to them discussing Pincai accompanying Qinyan to the Mei residence, an idea formed.","After scheming for a moment, Xi Eleven stood up and announced, “I’m leaving.” Thrusting out his belly, he strode out the door in a few paces. Pincai and the monk hurried to see him off, while Pan Third remained seated, his eyes wide and fixed on Qinyan, watching him with an intensity that filled Qinyan with an unexpressible rage. Xi Eleven pulled Pincai into the monk’s room and bowed to him, saying, “Today I must beg you to do a good deed. I am truly enamored with that person we just saw. But if I asked him to drink with me, he would definitely refuse.” Before he could finish, Pincai shook his head rapidly, “He won’t, he won’t! Absolutely not.” Xi Eleven continued, “And since he’s left the profession, it’s hard to force him. Now I have a brilliant plan. Let’s go back in, and you keep him for a meal, saying you’ll take him to the Mei residence afterward. When you start eating, I’ll barge in and sit with him for a bit. Even if I can’t do much, it will satisfy my craving, and it shouldn’t count as disrespecting him. Then I’ll give him some gifts and see how he treats me. My dear brother, we are good friends; if you do this for me, I’ll be grateful to you for the rest of my life.” Pincai pondered for a moment. He knew well that Qinyan’s temperament could not be forced, but he couldn't refuse Xi Eleven’s plea. So he finally agreed, “Your plan is fine, but you must not act coarse or rough. He is not like the others; say one wrong word and he’ll burst into tears. I’ve hit that wall many times myself.” Xi Eleven reassured him, “Don’t worry, I absolutely won’t be rough. I just want to sit with him; how could I dare hope for anything more? I have some dishes being sent over. Get Pan Third out of there, send Tianxiang and Cuiguan away, and set the table. I’ll be right back.” With that, he hurriedly got into his carriage and left.","Pincai went in and told Pan Third, “The monk wants to speak with you.” Reluctantly, Pan Third dawdled out, looking back several times. Pincai then sent Tianxiang and Cuiguan away and feigned relief to Qinyan, “Ah, finally some peace and quiet. I was getting dizzy with all their noise, filling the room with such vulgar stench. Let’s have a nice, quiet chat. We’ll go after breakfast; a little delay won't matter.” Qinyan thought it over; eating at Pincai's place wasn't a problem, and since the others were gone, no one would bother them. He asked Pincai, “How many times have you seen Yuxiang this year?” Pincai casually replied, “Three times.” Qinyan asked further, “I’ve heard Xi Eleven is a bad man. Why are you friends with him?” Pincai deflected, “We aren’t that close. He just seems like a straightforward fellow.” Qinyan added, “And that man named Pan, I know of him too.” Pincai said, “He’s just an honest businessman, and even the monk is very practical.” Qinyan laughed inwardly but found it inconvenient to argue.","Meanwhile, Xi Eleven climbed into his carriage and ordered the driver to whip the mule fiercely, racing all the way back in one breath. Upon arriving at his residence, he went straight to his study and ordered his servant to ask his concubine for the four dishes and two pastries sent over the night before. He told the servant to take them to Master Wei’s place and instructed him on what to say. Without entering the inner rooms, Xi Eleven stayed in his study, lit a pipe on the kang, and had Ba Yingguan prepare the opium. He quickly took thirty large puffs, consuming three mace, enough to sustain him for half a day. He wondered, “What should I give him?” Looking at the steel-rimmed pocket watch at his waist, worth two hundred strings of cash, he considered giving that. Then he thought, “Just a watch isn't precious enough. Only that pair of jadeite bracelets my concubine has—you couldn't find their equal in the capital right now. Giving him those would be incredibly prestigious.” He went into Juhua's room and heard a splashing sound. Looking up, he saw Juhua using the chamber pot; she smiled at him. Xi Eleven remarked, “No wonder the fragrance is overpowering—I thought they were cleaning the drains outside.” Juhua spat, “Bite your tongue.” Xi Eleven opened a chest, rummaged in the corners, found a box, opened the lid, confirmed its contents, and tucked it into his robe. Leaving the chest open, he turned to leave. Juhua cried out, “Why are you taking my bracelets?” Xi Eleven replied, “I’m just comparing the color with someone else’s; I’ll bring them right back.” Returning to his study, he called for Ba Yingguan and hurried off with long strides, heading straight for Pincai’s place. He rejoiced inwardly, “If I can get my hands on him, I’ll be the greatest patron in the capital.” Back at the temple, the monk relayed Xi Eleven’s plan to Pan Third. Pan Third was ecstatic, praising the brilliant scheme, and waited in the monk’s room. He thought wildly, “This living treasure—just sitting with him and having a drink will be enough. Would he really want to play with me? Even if he only wanted to play with me, I’d be willing. And if he’s willing to play with me, surely he’d let me play with him.” Lost in these vulgar fantasies, drool leaked from his mouth; he gritted his teeth, pounded the back of his neck twice, and snorted. Monk Tang found it amusing and asked, “What are you doing, Third Master Pan? Does your neck hurt?” Pan Third laughed along. Just then, Xi Eleven’s servant arrived with the dishes and asked to see Pincai. Si’er brought him in. The servant announced, “My master says that he didn't know Master Qin was here earlier and deeply regrets his offense. He originally wanted to invite Master Qin over to sit, but feared he wouldn't do him the honor, so he had me bring these dishes. He asks that you, sir, present them on his behalf to help Master Qin cool his anger, as my master is occupied and cannot come keep you company.” Pincai laughed, “Why did your master go to such trouble? When did he ever offend Master Qin? Since he is so thoughtful, I will accept them and act as host in his stead. Please convey my many thanks.” He gave the servant five hundred cash and said to Qinyan, “This is Master Xi’s kindness, sent for you, and I am merely benefiting from it. You should also offer a word of thanks.” Not understanding the reason, Qinyan had no choice but to offer a polite word of thanks. Pincai told Si’er to have the kitchen prepare everything quickly; it was time to eat.","Not long after Si’er left, wine and dishes were set out on a square table. Pincai urged, “Though it's just a simple meal, let us share a cup of wine.” Qinyan declined, “There’s no need, I will just eat.” But Pincai wouldn't listen, pouring a cup and handing it over. Qinyan had no choice but to accept and poured one in return. Pincai was overjoyed; it was the proudest moment of his life to finally sit face-to-face like this. Pincai chatted casually to coax Qinyan into a good mood, mentioning that Yuxiang rarely went out to banquets or plays these days, and frequently asked him to pass on a message: “Tell him to relax, not to worry about me, and to slowly build a relationship with Young Master Hua; naturally, you'll be able to see each other often.” Pincai’s only goal was to keep Qinyan comfortably seated until Xi Eleven arrived. Qinyan, however, was anxious to leave; he neither drank nor ate, but sat stiffly, as if sitting on pins and needles.","Just as Pincai was about to urge him to eat, the sound of hurried footsteps echoed in the courtyard. The wind-door was pried open, and in walked Xi Eleven. Qinyan panicked and immediately stood up. Pincai smiled warmly, “Perfect timing—the host has arrived to keep the guest company.” Xi Eleven laughed, “I knew you hadn't finished yet. I’ve come in all sincerity to toast Qinyan.” He had Ba Yingguan pull up a stool and sat facing south. Holding the pot in one hand and a cup in the other, he poured a full measure and thrust it right to Qinyan’s lips. Trapped between accepting and refusing, Qinyan flushed a deep red. Pincai gently coaxed, “This is the host's gesture of respect for the guest. If you can’t finish it, just have a sip.” Qinyan reluctantly took it, took a single sip, and set the cup down. “I truly cannot drink anymore,” he told Pincai. “I am uncomfortably full. Please, drink a cup with him.” He tried to slip away, but Xi Eleven grabbed him firmly. “Well now,” Xi said, “I just arrive, and you won’t even sit? You’re clearly looking down on me. Go back and ask—your young master is my own sworn nephew, so I’m hardly an outsider. Since you are his favorite, you’re like a little brother to me. How can you leave the moment I arrive?” Still holding on, Xi effortlessly and lightly pushed Qinyan back down into his seat. As Xi spoke, his eyebrows twitched, presenting a terrifying sight. Remembering last year’s encounter, Qinyan was so frightened he turned pale and sat trembling. Highly pleased, Xi Eleven called for a teacup, downed a cup of wine, and picked up a piece of sea cucumber to offer Qinyan. Suppressing his anger, Qinyan stood up and said, “Please enjoy it yourself, I truly cannot eat any more.” Xi Eleven smirked, “You might not be able to eat other things, but this slides down smooth and easy, and won’t make your stomach swell.” Qinyan understood the obscene double meaning, and his brows furrowed in distaste. Pincai gave Xi Eleven a kick under the table, hinting he should back off. Xi Eleven then took Qinyan’s leftover wine, drank it himself, and smacked his lips, declaring, “Good wine.” Qinyan was boiling with anger, yet finding it inconvenient to lash out, he suppressed his fury and thought to himself, “This scoundrel means no good. I’m no longer an actor, what dare he do to me? If he acts out of line, I’ll make a scene right here.” Seeing Xi Eleven drain the wine, pour another half cup, and push it in front of him, demanding he drink, Qinyan pressed his hand over the cup and said to Pincai, “You know I never drink.” Xi Eleven continued to press him. Just then, quick, hurried footsteps were heard, and Pan Third entered with the monk. Pan Third shouted, “Perfect timing! I’ve crashed a fine feast.” The monk chimed in, “Master Wei hosts a banquet and doesn't even offer a polite invitation to me.” Pan Third hunched his back, hitched his shoulders, and hurried forward, saying, “Let me offer a toast.” He snatched Qinyan’s cup, saying, “This wine is cold, I’ll drink it for you.” He downed it in one gulp, wiped the rim of the cup around his own lips, poured another half cup, and offered it with both hands, shoving it right to Qinyan’s mouth. Qinyan twisted away, trying to leave, but with Pan Third on one side and the monk blocking the other, he was trapped. Forced, he took the cup. Pan Third refused to let go, trying to force it into his mouth. Qinyan flared up, “I truly cannot drink! Let go, and I will sip it slowly.” Pincai made Pan Third sit down, saying, “He really can't. Just let him drink it slowly.” Pan Third finally let go and sat. Pincai and Monk Tang pulled up two stools and sat lower down. Seeing that Pan Third had rubbed the cup on his mouth, Qinyan was immensely disgusted. He knew now they were in league to humiliate him, but feared that if he openly turned on them, he would suffer for it.","Forced to endure this bitter humiliation, he picked up the cup and, feigning clumsiness, let it slip. With a sharp crash, it shattered to pieces on the floor, splashing a few drops of wine onto his clothes. He wiped it with his handkerchief and said to Pincai, “How clumsy of me.” Pincai, perfectly understanding his intent, replied smoothly, “It matters not at all.”","When they called for a replacement cup, Qinyan flatly refused. “There is no need, no need at all. Even if you bring it, I will not drink.” Xi Eleven insisted, “That won't do. We won’t press you too hard—just three cups from each of us.” Pan Third had been eagerly anticipating that if Qinyan drank from that cup, it would be as if they had kissed; seeing it smashed utterly ruined his mood. He tried to press another toast but was blocked by Pincai. Monk Tang, completely lacking in tact, poured half a cup and declared, “Amitabha! The noble Hua estate is a great patron of our humble temple. The old lady adorned the Three-World Buddha with gold, the young mistress commissioned the Guanyin who brings sons, and they donated three years of lamp oil. Now that the gentlemen of the estate grace us with their presence, this monk has nothing to offer but borrowed flowers for the Buddha. Young Master Qin, please drink this cup.” Holding the cup, he bowed deeply and chanted, “Namo Greatly Compassionate and Merciful Guanyin Bodhisattva who saves from suffering and distress!” The room erupted in laughter. Qinyan, both annoyed and amused, found his expression softening slightly. “I truly cannot drink, please do not force me,” he said. Monk Tang smiled obsequiously, “My dear Grandpa Qin, I have just chanted a prayer over it; there is a Buddha inside this cup. If you drink it, the front door will welcome a hundred blessings, the back door will gather a thousand auspicious signs, and I pray you make a great fortune, taking in a bar of gold every day.” The others roared with laughter, but Qinyan remained resolute in his refusal. The monk then rubbed his own face, took off his felt hat, and said, “Little Master Qin, take a look at my monk’s face—doesn’t it look less like a human face and more like a dick-head?” Confronted with this bizarre display, Qinyan couldn't help but let out a laugh. The monk cheered, “Wonderful, wonderful! The heavens have opened their eyes. It seems my dick-head is better than a human head after all.” At these vulgar words, Qinyan’s face immediately darkened again.","The monk persisted, “My ancestral grandpa, if you don’t drink this cup, this monk will lose all face and will have to return to secular life tomorrow.” He balanced the wine cup on his bald head, dropped to both knees, rested his hands on Qinyan’s lap, and continuously chanted prayers, refusing to rise. The others laughed until their sides ached. Harassed beyond endurance, Qinyan finally conceded, “Please get up, please get up. I will take one sip, and never again.” He took the cup from the monk’s head and took a sip. Then, fearing someone else would drink his leftovers, he resolutely downed the rest. He stood up to leave, but Xi Eleven pushed him back down, while the monk threw his arms around Qinyan’s legs and kowtowed against his knees. Forced to sit back down and genuinely angered, Qinyan spoke with stern severity: “May I ask what exactly you gentlemen intend today?” Pincai hurriedly intervened, “No more drinking, let’s just chat,” and hauled the monk to his feet. Qinyan insisted, “I have matters to attend to; I can stay no longer.” As he tried to leave again, Xi Eleven blocked his path, saying, “If you won’t drink, fine. Just sit for a while. What's the rush?” Pincai could only urge, “Serve the food quickly, we have business to attend to.” Qinyan was forced to sit once more, overflowing with rage yet violently suppressing it.","Xi Eleven thought to himself, “This boy is truly strange; we just can't fit our joints together. If it were anyone else, I would have cursed them out and forced myself on them by now. But out of pity for him, he acts this stubborn. It’s absolutely hateful.” He reconsidered: “They always said he was proud, and it’s clearly true. If I use force, it will offend the Hua estate. Besides, he’s no longer an actor, so it’s inappropriate to make him serve wine.","I'll give him the gift; perhaps once he receives the reward, he'll change his mind.” Pan Third thought, “This boy is even tougher than Su Huifang. If only I had brought some bank notes to reward him, perhaps he would fawn over me if he got some money.” Xi Eleven spoke up, “I have something to give you, and I hope you won't find it too meager.” He pulled a brocade box from his robe, opened the lid, and revealed a pair of translucent, flawlessly green jadeite bracelets, their brilliance dazzling the eyes.","Pan Third clicked his tongue and said, “A treasure like this, only you would have. Where could anyone else find one? You couldn't find a pair of bracelets like this in the whole city for a thousand strings of cash.” He continued clicking his tongue in awe. Pincai and the monk stared wide-eyed. Pincai thought, “What an opening move—offering such a fine thing right away. Let's see if he takes it.” Qinyan didn't even look, keeping his head bowed. Xi Eleven urged, “Try them on, I guarantee the size is perfect,” and told Qinyan to put them on. Qinyan stood up and said sternly, “I absolutely dare not accept this. Furthermore, I never wear bracelets.” Without thinking, Qinyan held out his hand to show them—meaning to demonstrate that he wore no bracelets. Xi Eleven misinterpreted this as an invitation to put them on him. He grabbed Qinyan's arm and yanked him forward; using too much force, the delicate and frightened Qinyan lost his balance and tumbled directly into Xi Eleven’s chest. Xi Eleven simply wrapped his arms around him, unable to hold back any longer. He buried his face in Qinyan’s cheek, deeply inhaling his scent, then grasped his hand and forcefully slid a bracelet onto his wrist. As Xi Eleven reached for the second, his grip loosened for a moment. Qinyan tore himself free, tears streaming down his face. Sobbing uncontrollably, disregarding all consequences, he cried out, “I don't even know you! I've left the profession, yet you still treat me like a gigolo and humiliate me. I will go back and tell my master, and then we will speak again!” He bolted out the door. Reaching the courtyard, he frantically tore the bracelet from his wrist and hurled it to the ground with all his might. With a sharp crack, it shattered into three pieces. He ran for his life. Xi Eleven erupted in fury, cursing, “You ungrateful little bastard!” He lunged forward to chase and grab him. Pincai desperately held him back. Xi Eleven refused to yield, stomping and raging, screaming curses. With his great height and strength, Pincai could hardly restrain him, finally pressing his own head against Xi Eleven’s chest, pleading repeatedly, “It's all my fault! If you must hit someone, hit me! Hit me!” Pan Third and Monk Tang stood by, adding fuel to the fire and aiding the tyrant. Blocked by Pincai, and realizing Qinyan had likely run out the temple gates, boarded his carriage, and gone too far to catch, Xi Eleven finally gave up. His eyes bulging with anger, his belly heaving, he sat down, panting heavily.","Ba Yingguan stood by, suppressing a smile. He went into the courtyard, gathered the shattered pieces of the bracelet—three in total—pieced them together in his palm, and remarked, “If I spend three mace of silver to mount these three sections, I can show off a fashionable three-section inlaid jadeite bracelet. I wonder if anyone would be willing to reward me with it?”","He brought them over and placed them in front of Xi Eleven, adding, “A bracelet worth a thousand strings is now worth three thousand.” Seeing this, Xi Eleven cursed even more viciously. Pan Third and Monk Tang kept repeating what a pity it was. When Xi Eleven returned with only one bracelet left, Juhua would surely raise a massive uproar. It was precisely the case of a toad longing for swan meat, completely unaware of its own meager lot in life.","Meanwhile, Qinyan boarded his carriage, lowered the curtain, and wept into his hands all the way back. Upon arriving home, he stripped off his outer coat, collapsed onto his bed, and the more he thought about it, the more bitterly he hated himself for foolishly seeking out Pincai and bringing this disaster upon himself. He pulled the quilt over his head and cried for half the day, nearly driven to thoughts of suicide. For what happened next, listen to the following chapter."]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations36:o},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),s={37:["To return to our story: Qinyan had returned to his lodgings in a fury. He wept for half the day before lying down on his bed fully clothed, pulling the quilt over himself. He cursed himself a thousand times over for having gone to see Pincai, realizing too late that Pincai was in league with the rest of them and had deliberately set out to humiliate him. His wretchedness was so profound that he wished he could die. He went on seething and weeping through half the night. Morning came and he was still asleep, so his young manservant had to come in and shake him awake. 'The master from the Garden of Ease, Mr Xu, has sent for you,' the boy said. 'He says you should go quickly, as Young Master Mei has already arrived.' Qinyan got up, and the boy folded away the bedding while Qinyan washed his face and drank a bowl of tea. After spending the previous day in a rage and half the night in tears, following two days of exhaustion, he now felt dizzy and light-headed. His mouth was dry and he felt utterly wretched. He forced himself to collect his strength, changed his clothes, and glanced at himself in the mirror, noticing that he looked a little thinner and paler than usual. He sat for a while longer in listless reluctance. By the time mid-morning approached, he made himself get into a carriage and set off for the Garden of Ease.","It was the first day of the second month. In the garden the plum blossoms had not yet fully opened, though the camellia and magnolia were in bloom.","For today's gathering, Liu Wenze, Yan Zhongqing, and Tian Chunhang did not attend. Chunhang was attending a New Year gathering of his examination year-mates. Since Wenze and Wang Xun were sons of the examiners, they had all invited him. Chunhang had also invited Zhongqing, but Zhongqing had recently caught a chill, so he declined both invitations. Wang Xun also declined his invitation, having arranged early in the morning to accompany Ziyu to the Garden of Ease. Cixian and Ziyun welcomed them into the Plum Blossom Retreat and they took their seats. This retreat was laid out in the shape of a plum blossom, comprising five sections of five rooms each. Winding corridors and curved balustrades connected them, surrounded by a sea of green calyxes and crimson fragrance. Looking from the outside, the buildings were indistinguishable; one perceived only an expanse of fragrant snow. Whenever Ziyu visited the garden, he always took time to admire several such spots. Ziyun remarked, 'We are rather short of company today, as there are many theatricals and banquets this month. I imagine that since Yunong has more than twenty days before he must return, these plum blossoms will last another ten. I intend to host a ten-day gathering where, regardless of numbers, whoever is free may come. Even if I happen to be occupied outside the garden, Jingyi will always be here to act as host. What do Yongan and Yuxiang think of this?' Wang Xun replied, 'That sounds ideal. I shall certainly come whenever I have the time.' Ziyu said, 'If I may say so, our host need not trouble himself every single day. Whoever feels the inclination might simply bring his wine and join the company, or perhaps we might find some other exceptional way to pass the time.' Cixian added, 'There are ways enough to pass the time, it is merely that our particular circle lacks any single devotion, making us seem rather more austere than most. For instance, some people gather to play cards or roll dice, or even gamble on Baobao and Yaotan. Others might beat gongs and drums, watch juggling, or listen to blind storytellers; these are all common amusements. Failing that, they might hire a theatrical troupe to perform, filling the stage with spears and swords and tumbling acrobats, before binding their own heads, painting their faces, and strutting about the stage themselves in high triumph. That too is a way to pass the time. And then there are the green bowers and winding alleys, where one might embrace powdered faces and oiled hair, flirting and jesting in a great tumult. Not only am I incapable of such things, but I expect none of you gentlemen care for them either.' Ziyun and the others murmured their agreement, noting that by this measure they could hardly be called lovers of excitement. Yet they agreed that nothing in the world brought greater joy than drinking wine and admiring flowers. Wang Xun turned to Ziyun and said, 'I have a question for you to judge.' Ziyun invited him to speak. Wang Xun asked, 'Between the flower among men and the flower among flowers, which is the more beautiful?' Ziyun smiled and replied, 'Each has its own beauty.' Wang Xun pressed, 'If one cannot have both, does one choose the person or the flower?' Ziyun laughed, 'What nonsense you speak! Naturally the person is precious and the flower is cheap, why even ask such a thing?' Cixian interjected, 'He surely has some hidden meaning in this; he is not speaking idly.'","Ziyun smiled faintly. Wang Xun laughed, 'I see your garden is filled with blossoms, yet in all the time we have been talking not a single flower among men has appeared. Does this not show you prefer flowers to people?' Ziyun replied with a chuckle, 'You are just talking for the sake of it. We made perfectly good arrangements the other day, so why have they not arrived yet?' A short while later, Baozhu and Guibao arrived and exchanged greetings. Ziyun asked, 'How is it that only the two of you have come at this hour?' Baozhu answered, 'I fear some may be unable to attend today. Has Yunong not arrived either?' Guibao explained, 'Today Lianjin has five private engagements and Lianzhu has four. Since they are almost exclusively performing Kunqu operas, the roles cannot be easily distributed. We have both already performed our share.' Wang Xun inquired why there were so many engagements today, to which Guibao replied that they would be busy for another half month before things quieted down. Baozhu added, 'I saw Xiangfan and Qianzhou there; why did Jiantan not come?' Wang Xun explained that he was unwell. Guibao turned to Ziyu and observed, 'This is the first time we have met this year.' Ziyu agreed, noting that although he had been out several times, he had never run into him. Baozhu remarked, 'Xiangwan and Jingfang are having a hard time of it today, as they are scheduled to perform everywhere and simply cannot get away.' Ziyun sighed, 'I thought I could count on six or seven people, but who could have known they would all be unavailable?' As midday approached, Guibao looked out and announced, 'Yunong is here!' Everyone turned to watch him enter. Ziyu saw that he had grown considerably taller since last year. Dressed in elegantly plain clothes as he walked through the plum grove, he seemed to blend with the blossoms in a dazzling display of radiant beauty. The company rose to welcome him with smiles. Qinyan first greeted Cixian, Ziyun, and Wang Xun, then turned to Ziyu to exchange pleasantries about the weather. Ziyun laughed and said, 'He has grown taller now and his learning has advanced. Look at him exchanging pleasantries with Yuxiang; he would hardly have been able to do that last year.' Hearing this, Qinyan felt somewhat embarrassed and replied, 'I have not seen him for half a year.' Ziyun teased, 'And when did we ever see each other often?' Qinyan smiled, 'Did we not see each other when you and Jingyi came to pay New Year calls?' Cixian laughed, 'Ah, yes, seeing each other once is apparently enough to absolve one of further conversation.' This made Qinyan laugh. Wang Xun added, 'I am the one who sees Yunong the least.' Qinyan nodded in agreement, then sat down alongside Baozhu and Guibao. Baozhu urged him to take the upper seat, which he eventually did.","Ziyun gave orders for the table to be laid, but did not yet circulate the wine. He said to Wang Xun, 'By seniority you are older than Yuxiang, but today's gathering has been arranged especially in honour of Yunong. Therefore, I want Yunong to sit in the place of honour, with Yuxiang beside him.' Qinyan protested, 'That cannot be, I shall not sit there.' Ziyu suggested that Yongan should have the top seat. Ziyun replied, 'On other occasions, yes, but today we are reversing the order.' He took Qinyan by the arm and settled him firmly in the seat of honour, placing Ziyu beside him. Guibao took the second seat, with Wang Xun alongside. No further demurral was to be permitted, and anyone who deferred would be penalized ten cups of wine. Ziyun then called on Baozhu to sit in the upper position. Just as Baozhu was about to decline, Huifang arrived. Ziyun welcomed him warmly, 'Splendid, do sit down, Cixian will be beside you.' Huifang was reluctant to sit above Cixian, but Cixian reassured him, 'Today's arrangement puts all of you above us. Have you not seen that two have already been settled?' Huifang had to comply, and Baozhu below likewise had no choice but to take the seat above Ziyun. Once everyone was seated, Wang Xun laughed and remarked, 'If a restaurant were to seat its guests this way, it would soon be paying them to eat rather than the other way round.' Everyone smiled, toasted one another a few rounds, and leisurely enjoyed a few dishes.","Baozhu noticed that Qinyan's eyes looked swollen from weeping and guessed it must be on account of his teacher. Ziyun had also noticed this and sighed quietly. 'Yunong is truly a tender-hearted soul,' he observed. 'Changqing did not treat him particularly well, and yet he has wept like this for him; that is rare indeed.' Everyone present sighed in sympathy. When Qinyan heard this, it stirred up yesterday's resentment, casting an expression of anger across his face. Then, catching sight of Ziyu beside him, who was the very cause of all that had happened, a surge of bitter feeling welled up within him and tears spilled down his cheeks. Everyone at the table hastened to offer words of comfort, not realizing that Qinyan's grief had quite a different source and had nothing whatsoever to do with Changqing. Since they did not know the truth and he could hardly explain it to them, he kept it all locked inside himself. The more he thought about it, the angrier he became, until he could no longer hold back. Pressing his handkerchief to his face, he thought to himself: 'That creature Wei Pincai is sure to spread rumors that I was drinking and entertaining at his rooms, that Qi Shiyi gave me a bracelet, and so forth. It would be better to speak up myself and let people know the truth. Besides, surely no one here would laugh at me.' He waited another moment, then asked Ziyu, 'When did you last see Pincai?' Ziyu replied, 'Not since sometime in the tenth month of last year. He is living now at Hongji Temple outside the city wall and never comes to my house.'","Qinyan said, 'I saw him yesterday and he told me he had met you three times this year.' Ziyu replied, 'That is quite untrue. The most absurd instance was at dawn on New Year's Day when he came banging on the gate. The doorman had only just gotten out of bed and dressed. Pincai said a word or two, left a calling card, and to this day we have not seen each other. Where did you see him?' Qinyan uttered a curse under his breath, 'That Wei Pincai has never been anything but trouble.' Huifang agreed, 'He has always been trouble, you need hardly tell us that.' Ziyu pressed Qinyan to continue. With tears in his eyes, Qinyan confessed, 'The fault was mine. I went to his lodgings and asked him to come with me to see you.' At these words a sharp pang went through Ziyu, and a flush of red appeared at his eyelids. Everyone listened intently as Qinyan continued. Wang Xun asked, 'And how did he treat you?' Qinyan explained, 'Pincai himself was alright at first, but there is a set of bad characters who have got their claws into him and are leading him astray.' Ziyun inquired about their identities. Qinyan revealed, 'One is Qi Shiyi, another is Pan Qiguan, and then there is a monk who is the landlord of the place where Pincai is staying.'","Huifang frowned and asked, 'And what happened to you?' Qinyan's anger had by now reached such a pitch that he went ahead and recounted the whole story in detail: how Qi Shiyi had made a point of leaving early, how Pincai had then sent Pan San away, how Qi Shiyi had unexpectedly turned up again with a gift of food, and how, one after another, Qi Shiyi, Pan San, and the monk had all barged in. He detailed every manner of insult and mockery they had subjected him to at the table, and how they had ultimately smashed his bracelet. Listening to this, Ziyu was furious. 'This is all Pincai's doing,' he declared. 'He must have arranged the whole thing on purpose, deliberately setting those men on to ill-use you.' Qinyan agreed, 'Of course he was in it with them. Fortunately, I no longer perform on stage, so they dared not go too far. Otherwise it would have been far worse, and I doubt you would be seeing me here today.' Ziyun remarked, 'What an ill fortune to encounter all three of those fiends at once. That must have been a dreadful ordeal.' Cixian and Wang Xun both laughed sympathetically. Guibao added, 'That Qi Shiyi, I have never had dealings with him myself, but Peixian and Yuyan came badly unstuck with him.' Qinyan noted, 'For me it has been twice now.' Wang Xun asked Guibao what he would do if he ran into Qi Shiyi. Guibao replied, 'If I ran into him, I would make him look into a bucket and have a cart-driver play a trick on him.' Everyone burst out laughing. Huifang wondered aloud, 'What scheme could we devise to deal with him?' Cixian laughed and suggested, 'If you really want to deal with him, you would have to use the stratagem of the flesh, but I doubt you would be willing to.' Huifang spat in contempt, prompting Cixian to laugh again. Ziyun asked what he found so amusing. Cixian explained, 'I was thinking that Qi Shiyi is only so mischievous because of a certain attribute of his. Why not simply have it cut off? That would calm him down well enough.' Wang Xun laughed, 'That would be no easy matter; it could only be done if Meixiang were willing to employ the flesh stratagem.' Huifang retorted, 'Why do you not do it yourself?' Wang Xun replied, 'I have no quarrel with the man, so what reason would I have? Never mind cutting Qi Shiyi, cut Pan San first and you would spare yourself a great deal of terror.' Huifang spat several times in disgust, then suddenly poured a cup of wine and offered it to Cixian, saying, 'It is entirely your fault; who told you to bring up those people?' Cixian accepted the cup without demur, smiled, and drank it down.","Just then, Ziyu and Qinyan caught each other's gaze, and each raised his cup to drink half. Ziyu could not help breaking into a gentle smile. Ziyun turned to ask him, 'How many times have you and Yunong sat at the same table now?' Ziyu replied, 'This is the second time, and it has been nearly a year since the first.' Ziyun lamented, 'Only twice! How sad! How much more parted than together.' Qinyan smiled and gently corrected him, 'This is in fact the third time.' Cixian observed, 'Yuxiang is being a little greedy, counting fewer than there have been. Last year you two slipped away to stroll along the canal without telling anyone; does that not count?' Ziyu admitted, 'I had momentarily forgotten.' Ziyun then proposed, 'I should like to invite my younger brother and Yunong to enjoy ten days of happiness together. I trust you will not find it tiresome?' Ziyu graciously responded, 'To spend time often in a fine garden among good friends is more than I could wish for; how could it possibly be tiresome? I only fear I may lack the felicity for it, and that my thin fortune might invite misfortune.'","Ziyun laughed heartily. Cixian quipped, 'He already lacks the felicity for ten days of gatherings; if he had the fortune of the Star of the North, now that would be a truly sea-vast happiness.' Everyone laughed at this remark. At that moment, both Qinyan and Ziyu felt a deep and expansive contentment.",`Wang Guibao turned to Ziyun with a smile and proposed, 'I have a word-game where one character is transformed into three. I will tell it to you, and anyone who cannot produce a match must drink a penalty cup.' Ziyun invited him to proceed. Guibao explained, 'Take the character for "great" (大) and add a dot to make it "extreme" (太). Move the dot to the top and it becomes "dog" (犬). Now, everyone must offer a sequence just like this.' Ziyun laughed, 'This is a dog's game; who has the patience for it?' Guibao grinned at Huifang and prompted, 'You give one.' Huifang thought for a moment and offered, 'Take the character for "king" (王) and add a dot to make it "jade" (玉). Move the dot to the top and it becomes "master" (主). Is that not better than your dog?' Guibao nodded in agreement, 'Very good.' Then he suddenly laughed again, 'You shouldn't have done that. Duxiang just scolded me, and now you have scolded Duxiang.' Huifang was puzzled, 'When did I scold him?' The others were equally confused, so Guibao explained, 'He is the host, and you spoke the word "master" right after the word "dog". Does that not amount to scolding him?' Huifang smiled, while Ziyun chided Guibao, 'You little fox-spirit, you have been quite mischievous lately, full of this glib and oily talk.' Baozhu chimed in, 'I have one: take the character for "wood" (木), add a stroke to make it "root" (本), and move it to the top to make it "not yet" (未).' Ziyun laughed, 'I have a method of reverse incarnation: take "not yet" (未), remove a stroke to make "wood" (木), and move it to the bottom to make "root" (本).' Everyone burst into laughter.`,`Qinyan offered, 'I have the character for "water" (水), add a dot to make it "ice" (冰), and move it to the top to make it "eternal" (永).' Cixian critiqued, 'That "eternal" is lacking a little something; it can only be considered a thin "ice". Still, there are not many of these readily available.' Wang Xun noted, 'I fear there are only a few, and they have all been taken.' Guibao proposed another, 'I have the character for "ten" (十), add a stroke to make it "scholar" (士), and move it to the top to make it "shield" (干).' Everyone approved. Huifang added, 'I have the character for "dark" (杳), add a stroke to make it "investigate" (查), and move it to the top to make it "fragrant" (香).' The company praised this one even more highly. Baozhu suggested, 'I have the character for "man" (丁), add a stroke to make it "in" (于), and move it to the top to make it "step" (亍).' Ziyun observed, 'That character is rather obscure.' Ziyu countered, 'It is acceptable enough.' Baozhu defended it, 'The characters 彳 and 亍 are not so very obscure.' Qinyan offered another, 'I have the character for "divine" (卜), add a stroke to make it "above" (上), and move it to the top to make it "below" (下).' Cixian praised this one warmly. Guibao said, 'I have the character for "white" (白), add a stroke to make it "self" (自), and move it to the top to make it "hundred" (百).' Huifang noted it was slightly lacking. Wang Xun then offered, 'I have the character for "speak" (曰), add a stroke to make it "field" (田), and move it to the top...' He paused mid-sentence. Guibao prompted, 'Move it to the top and it becomes what character?' Wang Xun burst out laughing, and Ziyu explained, 'If you push it straight through to the top, it becomes "reason" (由).' Ziyun added, 'I would pull it down to make it "armour" (甲).' Cixian laughed, 'One of you wants it to go up, the other wants it to go down, and you will end up quarrelling. I say let it go up at one end and down at the other, making the character "explain" (申). How is that?' Everyone burst into laughter.`,`They ate a few pastries and drank several more cups of wine. Wang Xun asked Huifang, 'Have you seen Xiangfan and Qianzhou?' Huifang replied, 'I was in fact with them for quite a while, which is why I was delayed. My part in the performance was moved up before I could get away. I saw a remarkable old gentleman of letters there today, said to be Qianzhou's former teacher. He had a refined and antique bearing and appeared to be well past sixty.' Ziyun inquired about his family name. Huifang answered, 'It is an unusual name. Let me think, something like Qu, I believe. He was dressed in the insignia of the sixth rank, and his conversation was so brilliant and wide-ranging that everyone present held him in admiration.' Ziyun thought for a moment and corrected him, 'It would be Qu, not Qu.' Huifang agreed, 'Yes, Qu, I had it wrong.' Cixian pondered, 'Could it be Qu Daosheng?' Ziyun confirmed, 'Almost certainly, I had heard he was in the capital.' Ziyu asked, 'Would his given name be Benli?' Ziyun affirmed, 'Exactly! Do you know him?' Ziyu explained, 'I do not know him personally, but I have read several of his letters to my father, discussing obscure points of history. In them, he advanced wholly original views that illuminated the ages. My father had them mounted as a booklet and I have often looked at them.' Cixian shared, 'Master Daosheng is sixty years old this year. He and my late elder brother were both recommended through the "filial and upright" examination. He served as a county magistrate in Jiangxi; what brings him to the capital?' Ziyun replied, 'He was promoted to assistant prefect last year and has presumably come to be presented at court. One of these days I shall invite him here and we can all meet him. He may be a man of principle, but he is also very fond of flowers and wine.' Ziyu hesitated, 'He is a friend of my father's generation, so I fear it may not be quite appropriate for me to keep him company.' Ziyun reassured him, 'What harm could there be?' Cixian added, 'Daosheng may be rather traditional in his ways, but in his writing and painting he is very playful. Besides his major works, he has produced various miscellaneous pieces, including one collection called *Forgetting Death* and another called *The Awakening from Sleep Collection*, all of it written in a spirit of amusement.' Qinyan noted, 'Those two titles alone are remarkable.' Wang Xun asked, 'What is in them?'`,"Cixian explained, 'I once flipped through them briefly on someone's desk, but did not read them closely. I recall that the *The Awakening from Sleep Collection* collection contained pieces composed by collaging lines from *ci* poems to make new *ci*, and from *qu* lyrics to make new *qu*, as well as couplets assembled from the classics and poetic anthologies. It was quite amusing. I believe towards the end there was a section of couplets matching opera titles, most of them two-character titles, but I regret I did not examine it carefully.' Ziyun asked, 'How would you compare Daosheng's poetry and prose with Hou Shiweng's?' Cixian offered his judgment, 'In my estimation, Daosheng is the superior of the two. Shiweng had great talent, but his standards were not as high, making his work rather mixed and impure. Daosheng possesses no less talent, yet his standards are pure and consistent, ensuring his body of work will last. Even Shiweng himself greatly admired him.' Wang Xun inquired, 'Do you mean our Nanjing man, Hou Shiweng? He considers himself the foremost genius under heaven, but from what I have seen, I am not sure that is warranted.' Ziyun defended him mildly, 'The talent is great and the learning is broad. To achieve what he has achieved is no small thing.' Then he proposed a new game, 'I think opera titles are quite amenable to being paired into couplets. For instance, *Watching a Painting* could be paired with *Stealing the Poem*, and *Stealing the Poem* could also be paired with *Picking Up a Painting*. It is a rather amusing game. Let the eight of us divide into four pairs, with each person pairing their partner's title. Anyone who produces a pairing not quite right in one character is penalized one cup; two characters off, two cups; half a character off or a partial answer, half a cup. A particularly clever or perfect pairing earns a toast of congratulations.'","Cixian agreed enthusiastically, 'Excellent. Let Yuxiang and Yunong start.' Ziyu suggested, 'It would be better if you and Meixiang go first, then Duxiang and Yaoqing, then Yongan and Ruixiang, and we shall go last.' Ziyun agreed to this arrangement, 'So we are placed in the middle, making it easy enough to look well in that position.' Cixian sighed, 'The first mover has the hard task, and I cannot think of anything brilliant on the spot. The day before yesterday I saw Shou Xiang perform *Inscribing the Song* and he sang it beautifully, so I shall take that as my opening.' Huifang immediately responded, '*Inscribing the Song* could certainly be paired with *Stealing the Poem*.' Baozhu interjected, 'Someone else just paired those; you are borrowing a ready-made answer. The pairing is muddled, so the penalty is one cup.' Huifang protested, 'If it does not count, it simply does not count. Why should there be a penalty?' Ziyun insisted, 'There must be a penalty, otherwise people would just pair haphazardly and refuse to drink.' Thus, Huifang was made to drink a cup as forfeit. After a moment of thought, Huifang offered, 'Will *Teaching Song* do as a pairing?' Cixian approved, 'Good.' Everyone agreed it was a fitting match. Huifang then declared, 'Since it is agreed to be good, there ought to be a toast.' Ziyun concurred, 'Indeed there should.' A toast was drunk by all at the table. Huifang then put forward *Burying the Jade*, and Cixian matched it with *Finding Gold*. Wang Xun praised it, 'That is perfectly matched, let us toast again.' And so each person toasted again.",`It was now Ziyun's turn to put forward a title, and he offered the first half of a couplet: *Treading on the Moon*. Baozhu thought for a moment and paired it with *Sweeping Flowers*. Guibao applauded, 'Excellent.' Ziyun offered a mild critique, 'The pairing is good in terms of meaning, but when two-character opera titles are matched, the tones should also correspond. The word "sweep" is also an oblique tone, so there is a slight imperfection here.' Cixian concurred, 'You are right to point that out. However, when I think about it, although there are many opera titles, those that pair well are actually quite few. The second character ought to observe tonal correspondence strictly, while the first may be forgiven. This is unlike a general couplet matching historical references, where one may range freely in search of material; here the choices are finite. It is better to have a pairing where the characters match well but the tones are slightly off than one where the tones are perfect but the characters are weak. As for the second character, no error can be excused.' Ziyun reflected on this, agreed that the pickings were indeed slim, and accepted the answer. Baozhu then put forward *The Monastery Gate*, and after some deliberation Ziyun matched it with *The Stone Grotto*. This was deemed acceptably well-matched, earning a toast. Next came the turn of Wang Xun and Guibao. Wang Xun offered *Singing the Ballad*, and Guibao matched it with *Composing the Score*. Cixian remarked, 'I feel the opening title ought to be something fresh and unexpected; too conventional and it lacks novelty.' Guibao turned to Wang Xun and said, 'Let me give you a fresh one: *Stealing the Chicken*.' Wang Xun replied, 'I shall match it with *Subduing the Tiger*.' Everyone praised this as a fine match and toasted. Cixian observed, 'It is indeed a good pairing, but *Stealing the Chicken* is small and petty, while *Subduing the Tiger* is bold and grand. What do you all think?' Wang Xun acknowledged the point, 'That is a precise observation. I shall change mine to *Visiting the Rat*.' Cixian declared, 'Now that deserves a toast.' Everyone drank. When it came to Ziyu's turn, he offered *Looking at the Stocking*, and Qinyan gracefully paired it with *Borrowing the Boot*. The company exclaimed, 'Beautifully paired! That deserves two toasts.' Huifang interjected, 'One is enough. The pairing came quickly, it is true, but if we go on toasting two cups at a time we shall drink everyone senseless and nobody will be able to pair anything.' Cixian agreed, 'Quite right. Henceforth, we shall have a full toast for the very best pairings, half a cup for good ones, and nothing for ordinary ones.' And so each person drank a cup.`,`Qinyan then put forward *The Drunken Consort*. Ziyu, whose ear had been caught by Wang Xun's *Subduing the Tiger* a moment earlier, brilliantly paired it with *Sobering the Courtesan*. Everyone exclaimed, 'That pairing is delightful! The wit of setting "drunk" against "sober" is admirable. A toast all round!' Qinyan added, 'The charm is in the one being drunk and the other sober; it is a lucky find.' When it came to Cixian's turn, he offered, 'I shall propose *Flinging the Dipper*.' Huifang mused, 'What a *Flinging the Dipper*.' After a moment's thought, he replied, 'I shall match it with *Searching the Cup*.' Cixian praised him, 'And what a *Searching the Cup*! The pairing is precise and tight inside. A toast, a full cup!' Everyone drank joyfully.`,"After a pause, Cixian pressed Huifang to produce the next title. Huifang hesitated, 'I have one in mind, but I fear it may have no possible answer.' Cixian encouraged him, 'If it truly has none, we shall simply drink a cup and move on. Say it out, and let me think.' Huifang explained, '*Female Thief* has another name: *Hinny Bandit*. Those two characters are quite original. If you can produce a pairing, I will willingly drink three cups.' Cixian challenged, 'Is that a promise?' Everyone mentally tried and failed to come up with a match. Ziyun admitted, 'I cannot think of a pairing.' Suddenly, Cixian burst out laughing and said to Huifang, 'You drink your three cups first, then I shall give you the pairing.' Huifang countered, 'You pair it first, then I drink.'","Cixian said, 'You will drink. Now: *Power and Profit* also goes by the name *Powerful Monk*. Is that not a perfect pairing?' Huifang said, 'How can *power* pair with *female*?' Ziyu thought for a moment, then broke into a loud clap of the hands and burst out laughing. 'Marvellous, utterly marvellous! Only power could possibly pair with female. A truly perfect match.' Qinyan and Baozhu had not yet understood it. Ziyun and Wang Xun, having worked it out, also began laughing and said, 'A brilliant conceit, to treat those two characters as those two things: a truly inspired stroke of imagination.' The four performers had not yet grasped it; Huifang was still sitting blankly thinking. Wang Xun said, 'You have not worked it out, do you not know that for a man, yang is power?' At this Huifang and the others suddenly understood and began to laugh as well, all agreeing it was splendid. Huifang drank his promised three cups; everyone else toasted one.",`Ziyun then offered *Attacking the Inn*, and Baozhu cleverly matched it with *Fleeing the Pass*. Baozhu proposed *Seizing the Beauty*, which Ziyun neatly countered with *Killing Xishi*. Both were deemed robust and steady pairings, and each earned a cup. Wang Xun brought forth *Grass Bridge*, and Guibao paired it with *Hemp Ground*, then suddenly paused. 'The word for "field" here is slightly mismatched,' he mused. 'Let me change it to *The Pavilion of Gossamer*.' Wang Xun praised the revision: 'This borrowed pairing of *The Pavilion of Gossamer* is excellent. It deserves a half cup.' Guibao next proposed *Flower Lady*. Wang Xun thought for a moment and offered *Fire Judge*. The group was already applauding and calling for a toast when Wang Xun held up a hand. 'Wait, I have a better idea,' he announced, and changed his match to *Grass Chancellor*. The others exclaimed, 'Even better! That is remarkably fresh and inventive.' They all toasted in agreement. Ziyu offered *Sealing the Chamber*, and Qinyan smoothly matched it with *Declining the Pavilion*. This too was judged a solid pairing and rewarded with a half cup. Qinyan then proposed *Removing Armour*. Ziyu reflected but could think of nothing entirely novel. Suddenly, he remembered a scene from *The Peach Blossom Fan* called *Duping the Ding Clan* and offered it as a borrowed match. Everyone was full of admiration, and each drank a full cup in toast. Cixian offered *The Rice-Shop*, and Huifang matched it with *The Tea-House*. Huifang offered *Plucking the Eyebrow*, and Ziyun said, 'This is even harder to match.' Cixian matched it with *Piercing the Eye* and *Opening the Eye*. Huifang said, 'Now that is truly clever and precise.' Cixian said, 'I also considered *Piercing the Eye*, which might be even better, except that the character for "piercing" carries an oblique tone.' Ziyu said, 'Both are excellent. They feel as if heaven and earth made them for each other, and nothing could surpass them.'`,`It came round to Ziyun again, and he offered *Tumbling in the Snow*. Baozhu confidently stated, 'That is a broad one, making it easy for me.' He quickly added, 'Though the word "tumble" is not so easy either.' After a moment's thought, he matched it with *Falling on the Ice*. Everyone praised this in unison, 'What a fine pair! *Tumbling in the Snow* and *Falling on the Ice*: one idea rendered beautifully in two layers.' Huifang urged Baozhu, 'Give him a hard one.' Baozhu nodded, but Ziyun objected playfully, 'Why are you asking him to make it difficult for me? You just want me to be penalized wine.' Huifang laughed, 'Exactly.' Ziyun challenged Baozhu, 'Do bring on the hardest you have.' Baozhu thought for a while before offering *Supporting the Head*. Ziyun chuckled, 'That is truly not easy.' Then he suddenly slapped the table in triumph, 'I have it! I shall match it with *Cutting the Foot*.' Ziyu exclaimed, 'Superb! Superb! This and *Plucking the Eyebrow* matched with *Piercing the Eye* and *Opening the Eye* can both be called perfect pairings.' Cixian added, 'It is even better than *Plucking the Eyebrow* and *Piercing the Eye* and *Opening the Eye*. In this pair, "head" and "foot" are both used in their secondary meanings, their internal linguistic meaning is identical, and the tones correspond perfectly. Since it was Meixiang who provoked it, we should toast double cups.' And so everyone drank and happily resumed eating.`,"It was Wang Xun's turn again, and he offered *Flower Drum*. Guibao thought and thought, but could not come up with a match, his face growing red with the effort.",`Wang Xun pressed him gently. Guibao sighed, 'I did not expect this one to be impossible. The only thing I can think of is *Hearing the Bells* and *Bell in the Rain*, but that is a prelude and not an opera title. *Grass Bridge* is not really a perfect match either, as the "bridge" does not quite fit. I cannot think of anything else, so I shall simply drink a cup.' Guibao drank half a cup, then offered *Kneeling at the Pool*. Wang Xun promptly matched it with *Leaping into the Well*. Everyone approved, and half a cup was toasted. When it came to Ziyu's turn, he offered *Plucking a Willow Branch*. Ziyun laughed, 'Yuxiang has had a tender eye on Yunong and has produced a perfectly easy title.' Ziyu confessed, 'I could not think of anything original in the moment. I find producing titles harder than matching them.' Qinyan gracefully matched it with *Sweeping the Pine*. Ziyu smiled, 'My match has made even my own title look better.' Everyone toasted half a cup. Qinyan then proposed, 'I shall take the word "sweep" for my next title: *Sweeping Away Qin*.' The company murmured, 'Now that is a hard one.' Ziyu agreed, 'Very difficult indeed. "Qin" is both a surname and the name of a dynasty, which makes it particularly tricky.' Suddenly, he thought of a match and looked quite pleased with himself. 'There happens to be one just right here. I shall match it with *Checking Han*.' Everyone exclaimed in delight, 'Perfect! It is entirely natural: the Qin and Han dynasties pair beautifully, and the verbs "sweep" and "check" are matched just as well. A double toast!' By this point, they had completed three full rounds. It had been quite a long session, and the lamps had now been lit. They paused for a short rest and exchanged some light conversation.`,`Cixian announced, 'It is my turn again. I shall follow Yuxiang's example and make it easy for someone.' He offered *The Tavern*, and Huifang easily matched it with *Book Shop*. Huifang then declared, 'Following Yunong's trick of the linked chain, I shall take the word "book" and offer *Revising the Letter*.' Cixian grumbled playfully, 'You are putting me in difficulty. Well then, I shall make myself produce a good one.' After thinking for a moment, he matched it with *Chasing the Messenger*.`,`Wang Xun nodded approvingly, '"Book" and "letter" form a fine pair of characters.' Cixian added, 'I also thought of *Releasing the Easy*, since "easy" is much like "letter" in meaning.' Everyone cried out together in praise, 'That one is even better! It deserves double cups.' And so they toasted. Ziyun casually remarked, '*Seeing the Ghost*.' No one paid attention. After a pause, Baozhu pressed him to produce a title. Ziyun laughed, 'You are not matching it, yet you are pressing me. What is there for you to match when I have not yet given a title?' Baozhu countered, 'You have not given one, so what am I supposed to match?' Ziyun clarified, 'I said it just now: *Seeing the Ghost* is my title.' Baozhu reflected, recalled that such an opera title indeed existed, and seamlessly matched it with *Departing the Soul*. Ziyun nodded approvingly, 'A good match.' Half a cup was toasted. Baozhu then offered *Eating Chaff*, which Ziyun quickly matched with *Splashing Gruel*.`,`It was Wang Xun's turn, and he offered *The Infernal Judge*. Cixian warned, 'This is not easy. That word for "judge" is half concrete and half abstract; Ruixiang may well be penalized.' Guibao thought for a while and announced, 'I have a good match. It is slightly unusual and not an established opera, but in *Empty Valley Fragrance* there is a scene called *The Buddhist Healer*. I shall match it with *The Buddhist Healer*.' Cixian praised him, 'Excellent indeed. You not only escape a penalty, you deserve a toast.' Guibao then proposed, 'Now I have thought of a difficult one: I offer *Frightening the Clown*.' Wang Xun pondered for a while and replied, 'I have a good match. Of the four pairings so far, these two have precisely the same dark coloring, so you should toast double cups. I match it with *Terrifying the Simpleton*.' Everyone laughed and agreed, 'Indeed, it is dark and gloomy, exactly the same color. Double cups are in order.' And so everyone toasted.`,"Ziyu suggested, 'We can end the game here, as the night is not young. Besides, I left the house early this morning, and if I stay too late my mother will worry. Let us close with this pairing.' Wang Xun agreed, 'It is time. After the pairing, let us eat.' Ziyun protested, 'Wait, it is actually still quite early.' Ziyu countered, 'Since we plan to meet for several more days, we ought to save our energy for tomorrow. It is better to break up early today.' In truth, Ziyu had noticed that Qinyan was beginning to look tired, and that was his real reason for wishing to end the game. Ziyun had to agree. Ziyu proposed, 'Let me offer a three-character title for the close.' He offered *The Flying Bear Dream*. Everyone murmured, 'Three characters will be harder, as fewer good matches exist.' Qinyan thought for a while and elegantly matched it with *The Crouching Tiger Scroll*.","Everyone praised this warmly and drank a final toast. Qinyan smiled and said, 'With that one pair we are done. Now, let me offer a four-character title.' The company sighed, 'Four characters will be even harder.' Qinyan reassured them, 'The penalty for a failed match is still only one cup. If everyone were trying to match four-character titles at once it would, of course, be far harder, but for one or two rounds there may still be possibilities.' He offered *Selling One's Son to Cast Himself into the Abyss*. Ziyu pondered for a while, then offered *Longing for One's Parents, Abandoning the Feast*. Everyone struck the table in admiration. Ziyun observed, 'Heart expressed through words! Yuxiang said just now he was afraid of being home late and his mother worrying, and here is *Longing for One's Parents, Abandoning the Feast*. A pairing straight from life; let us each toast three cups and then eat.' In this round, each person had taken four turns, producing a total of thirty-two paired couplets and sixty-four opera titles in all. It had been no small effort of the mind, and a good many cups of wine had been drunk. Everyone was beginning to feel the warmth of the wine and was no longer in a condition to eat a proper dinner. After the three cups of toast they had their meal, sat for a while, and then Ziyu and Wang Xun took their leave. Ziyun made arrangements for the following day. The next day, Wenze and Chunhang joined the party, as did several more of the famous performers, and they spent another day together at the Garden of Ease. Lu Sulan gave a small private gathering for Ziyu and Qinyan alone, a day of quiet conversation and light company that was, in its own way, even more delightful. Over three days in a row they met and feasted, and Ziyu felt perfectly content, though by now he was quite worn out. Xu Ziyun had been planning to invite Qu Daosheng, and just at this point Shi Nanxiang arrived in the capital, providing the occasion for a grand literary gathering of wine and verse. Ziyu could not decline, and had no choice but to attend. As to what happened next, listen to the following chapter for the explanation."]},y=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations37:s},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={38:["To return to our story: Xu Ziyun had invited Master Qu to attend, with Nanxiang, Zhongqing, Wenze, Chunhang, Wang Xun, and Ziyu as companions, again in the Plum-Blossom Hermitage. Wang Xun on this particular day had been called away by Sun Lianggong on an important matter: the wedding date had been fixed for Li Yuanmao, who was to be adopted into the Sun household as a son-in-law. Since Sun's two sons were not yet capable of managing such affairs, everything had been entrusted to Wang Xun, and so Wang Xun was unable to come.","Knowing that Qu Daosheng was a man of refined tastes who preferred quieter company, Ziyun had invited only four performers: Baozhu, Shufang, Huifang, and Sulan. Shufang was indisposed and unable to attend; as an added guest, Qinyan was also notified. That day Master Qu arrived first and spent a good while in conversation with Ziyun and Cixian, catching up on old times.","Let us briefly describe Master Qu's background. Master Qu was a native of Wuchang Prefecture in Hubei, a descendant of the Grand Officer of the Three Lü Clans [i.e., the poet Qu Yuan]. His learning spanned heaven and earth, and he had mastery of all six classical arts; yet his entire life had been beset by ill fortune and difficult circumstances, and outside of his books he had nothing at all.","His father had been appointed a compiler of the Hanlin Academy through the examination for broad learning in belles-lettres, but had died before reaching the age of thirty.",'At that time Daosheng was only four years old. His paternal grandparents were still alive, and his grandmother bore many years of widowhood in fidelity, single-handedly both rearing and educating him. Daosheng was admitted to the prefectural school at the age of sixteen, only to observe mourning for his grandfather. When the three years of mourning were complete and he was about to sit for the higher examinations, his grandmother died, and he entered mourning once more for another three years. After completing that mourning period, his mother too passed away in quick succession. Daosheng had observed nine consecutive years of mourning and by the time it was over he was twenty-five. He then married a woman of the Min family, a paragon of virtue and wisdom. Making his living by his brush, Daosheng roamed as a private secretary and advisor to various officials, travelling through Yan, Zhao, Wu, Yue, and the provinces of Yunnan and Guizhou — tens of thousands of li in all, wandering for twenty years. He acquired a name in his time, and those who admired his character and talent compared him to Du Shaoling [Du Fu] and Meng Dongye [Meng Jiao]. Yet by nature he was high-minded and impractical, with no gift for managing household affairs, and he was constantly burdened by poverty. He later came to the capital to sit for the examinations but failed twice, took a post as a tutor in the household of Secretary Liu, where he taught Wenze for two years. He was then engaged by the young master of the Hua family to teach, remaining there another three years before finally returning to his native place. The local magistrates, admiring his virtuous reputation, recommended him through the "filial and upright" examination channel. He was selected as a county magistrate in Jiangxi — a hard and poorly rewarded post — and upon completing his term was promoted by memorial to assistant prefect of Nanchang. The year before, his wife had died, leaving him quite alone in the world, without a single close relation.',"He had a few servants, all of them engaged through outside recommendations. Only one, his personal attendant Liu Xi, had been with him for five or six years and was a man of some conscience; the rest were of no particular consequence. Though Master Qu travelled light in the way of baggage, a lifetime of collecting had filled several trunks with bronze and stone antiques, precious books, and old paintings. When he arrived in the capital, Secretary Liu, mindful of their old connection, saw that Qu's official purse was thin and presented him with two hundred taels of silver.","Young Master Hua, learning of his arrival, came out of the city to pay a formal call and gave him three hundred taels. With five hundred taels in hand, Master Qu went round the antique shops and bought a good many books and famous rubbings. He also distributed a fair amount among impoverished friends of former times, so that only a little remained by now.","In former times, when Grand Secretary Xu had been in the capital, he too had been on friendly terms with Master Qu, had consulted him on various matters, and had engaged him from time to time to compose poems and prose pieces on his behalf — which is why Ziyun treated him with the deference due to an elder. Shi Nanxiang was a younger native of the same region, and it goes without saying that he was already acquainted with him.",`Tian Chunhang had met him the day before, but Zhongqing and Ziyu were making his acquaintance for the first time. Seeing that otherworldly, Taoist-sage bearing, and mindful moreover that he was a friend of his father's generation, Ziyu naturally showed the deepest respect. Master Qu saw in Zhongqing a refined and spiritually luminous quality and knew him immediately for a man of exceptional character. Then he looked at Ziyu — warm and composed as a piece of jade, bright as the glow of a pearl, fine without and wise within, serene of spirit and grave of manner — and recognised that he was no mere person of outward graces alone. His heart was greatly pleased, and he thought to himself: "Old Mei Tie'an may indeed be said to have a worthy son." He spoke to Ziyu about matters in Jiangxi, saying, "Your honoured father strictly refuses to be swayed by personal connections and absolutely bars the door to bribes and gifts — the entire province compares him to Sima Guang and Wen Yanbo. The gratitude of the scholars and people goes without saying." He also asked about Ziyu's experience at the provincial examinations the previous year, and Ziyu answered each question in full. Qu saw that his speech was clear and gentle, his manner unassuming, and that he was evidently already a man of deep learning; he thought to himself that he would like to put him to the test, and would do so gradually over the course of the meal.`,`Just then the four performers arrived together. Huifang he already knew; all four came forward to pay their respects. Qu clasped his hands in return, bade them be seated, and looked them over carefully, asking the names of three of them. He said to Ziyun, 'The young performers in the capital today are even finer than in former times.' Ziyun said, 'Strictly speaking I ought not to have invited them to wait upon us today; but they are not entirely without refinement and can handle an ink-stone and unfurl paper. Besides, when they heard beforehand that you were coming, they wished to have a glimpse of a great man of letters. A few words of praise from your lips would be worth more to them than a handful of gold, and I trust you will not think me frivolous for the invitation.' Qu smiled and said, 'You speak as though I were the sort of stiff old "filial and upright" man who would object. Drinking wine and admiring beautiful people — what harm is there to one's character? Not to flatter you, but I think these four look nothing like theatrical entertainers. You of course know them intimately; I am meeting them for the first time. Let me venture a brief description of each, and you shall tell me whether I am right or wrong.' Everyone settled in to listen carefully to what he would say. Cixian said, 'I know you have a gift for physiognomy, Elder Brother. But spare us the predictions — tell us about the past instead. Talk of great careers and fine outcomes is something any of us can manage. If you can describe each person's actual temperament and nature, then I shall believe in your art.' The performers all smiled at this. Ziyun said, 'That may be beyond even his ability.' Qu said, 'It is not so difficult — let me tell you.' At this point the table had been laid. Ziyun offered wine, and the company was divided into two tables, east and west. Master Qu, needless to say, took the head of the east table.`,"The head of the west table was to be taken by Nanxiang, who said, 'This is a senior from my own region — how could I presume to take such a position?' And so Zhongqing was given that seat instead. Second on the east table was Nanxiang; second on the west was Chunhang. Third on the east was Ziyu; third on the west was Wenze. Ziyun presided at the east table while Cixian acted as second host at the west. Baozhu and Qinyan sat on the east side; Huifang and Sulan on the west. Everyone took their places. The host circulated wine and the guests each drank a few cups. Qu said, 'I shall begin with Su Meixiang, whom I met the other day.' Everyone at the west table listened attentively. Qu said, 'In my physiognomy I do not rely on complexion or the formal positions of features — those are secondary matters. What I observe is the spirit and bone-quality of the whole person, the bearing and movement, the manner of sitting and standing, and the voice and manner of speech, through which I distinguish refinement from coarseness and discern the heart, in order to judge a person's fortune. But my very first observation may be somewhat surprising: in my view Meixiang comes from a good family background — he is not an ordinary household's son. You will all be aware of this, no doubt — am I right?' Everyone felt a quiet surprise at this, suspecting that he had already inquired into Huifang's origins, and that this was why he had started with him, to display his skill. Cixian said, 'Let us not imagine you have been making inquiries about his background beforehand.'","Qu said, 'There is no need for inquiries. I can see that he is exceptionally perceptive, courageous, and spirited — a person who dares to act. Yet despite his good background, he clearly suffered greatly in his early years; what one might call an escape from the jaws of death. From what I can observe, within the next year or two he will undergo a significant change and leave his current profession. How he will fare in the end is still far off, and I shall not venture to say. If I did, Jingyi would dispute me again.' He then looked at Sulan and Baozhu — their general qualities were much the same — and at Huifang as well, finding no great difference; for these three he made no comment about their origins. He said, 'Emerging from the mud unsullied — that is the general impression these three give.' When his gaze reached Qinyan, Qu said, 'This one is rather different. Are you still in the troupe at present?' Cixian said, 'Still in the troupe — and a fifth-month pomegranate blazing before the eye at that: admired by sophisticated and common alike, at the very height of his fame.' Qinyan smiled at this. Qu said, 'Admired by the sophisticated, perhaps — but by the common, I rather doubt it. I see in him a proud and unyielding spirit that absolutely cannot bend itself to the times; and moreover a heart full of concerns for which nothing in his present life is truly suited. Were this person to have studied, he would turn out very like me — absolutely incapable of passing any examination.'  Everyone laughed, hearing how apt this was.","He then asked to see Qinyan's hand and examined it. 'A pity,' he said. 'The lines show talent — change your profession as soon as you can. You will not be wealthy, but yours is the path of the pure and lofty. Your heart is made of different stuff from other people's. What is not your own will is something you cannot endure even a moment's constraint of; what is your own will, you would embrace unto death without complaint. In matters of loyalty, fidelity, and duty, you would be capable of doing what others cannot. But one word of caution: if you direct your heart toward magnanimity, you can shape your own destiny; the only danger is that your allotted span of years may be insufficient. Yet through self-cultivation and attentiveness you can turn even that around.' Everyone who heard him speak felt the precision of what he had said and knew that his art was genuine and not mere talk. The words he had spoken struck Qinyan to the very core of his being, and he felt a great warmth towards the old man. Seeing too that Master Qu had about him an air of one who might at any moment ascend to the immortal realm, Qinyan felt an intimacy and affection toward him that grew by the moment.",`Qu turned to Nanxiang, who was seated beside him, and asked, 'Has your honoured father made any notable achievements since taking up his post? Tell me of any good governance he has carried out.' Nanxiang said, 'My father has only recently taken a provincial post, and it has been just three months — he has not yet accomplished much. He did discover one powerful local magnate and two corrupt runners, which has greatly pleased the local people, and the Governor-General wrote to commend him with a few words of praise. Beyond that there is not much to report.' Qu said, 'I know your father is an upright and resolute man who will certainly achieve things. Powerful magnates and corrupt underlings — where are there none? In Jiangxi, when I first took my post, the local magnates and corrupt runners were particularly severe, and the people who had suffered at their hands were beyond counting. Seven successive county magistrates had all turned a deaf ear and blind eye, not daring to act against them — which only made the culprits bolder still. There was a local saying: "In the east village there is a tiger; in the west village there is a wolf. The tiger eats the people's flesh; the wolf eats the people's entrails. When wolf and tiger have devoured all but the last blood, the greedy serpents and starving scorpions are still not sated. The courthouse gates stand wide open, and serpents and scorpions come streaming in. The county magistrate sits on his dais like a clay idol, the scorpion climbing his back and the serpent coiled about his head." The wolf and tiger were the local magnates; the serpents and scorpions were the corrupt runners. The magnate of the east village had purchased a title as a garrison commander, and the one to the west was his own brother — one a successful military candidate, one a military student, with a band of thieves and ruffians at their command. They served as the ring-leaders of the criminal world, conspiring with the east-village tiger, manipulating lawsuits, and controlling the courts, with the serpents and scorpions acting as their go-betweens. By the time I arrived in my post I found that within the past three years seven county magistrates had been replaced, with more than two hundred cases of banditry and homicide outstanding. After half a year of painstaking effort I had dealt with these five individuals, after which the district became peaceful — not a single case of homicide or banditry has occurred since.' Ziyun said, 'That was no small achievement — the people who owe you gratitude extend far beyond a single county.' Qu said, 'I dare not claim credit. Whatever the district requires to be dealt with, I deal with; I put in the effort regardless of personal risk, and see what comes of it.' He then went on to discuss many other matters with the assembled scholars.`,`Ziyun noticed that one of the house servants who was bringing dishes had a louse crawling on the shoulder of his new jacket. He waited until the man had set down the dishes, then called him over and told him to pick it off. Qu immediately turned to Ziyu and asked, 'Since the young gentleman has read widely in the classics and histories, I wonder — in which text does this creature make its earliest and most venerable appearance? I need not say anything of it in poetry and miscellaneous writing.' Taken off guard by this question, Ziyu paused a moment in thought. "The character is indeed unusual," he reflected. "He asks which text has the earliest occurrence — so passing references like flicking lice or threading lice on a string need not be considered." He answered with graceful modesty: 'My learning is shallow and my reading limited, I fear. Of what I have encountered in histories and records, the earliest instance is probably the passage in Ruan Ji's "Essay on the Great Man" — "How does the gentleman dwelling within the four seas differ from a louse dwelling within a [trouser-seam]?"' Nanxiang said, 'There is also the line from the Records of the Grand Historian: "The horsefly attacking an ox cannot pierce a nit."' Qu said, 'Both of these come after a passage in the Book of Lord Shang. In antiquity there was an office called the "lice official," which is recorded in the Book of Lord Shang. Bibliographic Treatise of the Hanshu records that the Book of Lord Shang was transmitted in twenty-nine chapters, three of which were later lost, leaving only twenty-six in circulation. Among them, benevolence, righteousness, ritual, and music are referred to as the offices of lice. Du Mu of the Tang dynasty inscribed a version of this passage on the reverse of the stone stele at the Confucian temple in Chuzhou, writing: "That Shang Yang was able to cultivate and fight, was able to enforce his laws, and thereby laid the foundation of Qin's greatness, this he called the office of the benevolence-and-righteousness lice." The idea is that benevolence and righteousness arise naturally from the human heart, just as lice arise naturally from human grime. The meaning of the character for louse seems to connote something easily bred and densely proliferating — though I am not certain this is correct.' Nanxiang and Ziyu bowed in admiration. Cixian said, 'Master Qu is opening his trunk of books today — fortunately the guests at this table are all capable of receiving instruction. If it were just me alone, I would not give you permission to lecture.' Qu laughed and said, 'You are all accomplished men of letters, and there is no book you have not read. I am an old fogey — how would I dare "play the craftsman before the master carpenter"? Besides, in my younger days my learning was all from hearsay, and whatever I heard I immediately forgot — by now none of it remains.' Ziyun said, 'The other day Cixian mentioned having seen a collection in your collected works called the The Awakening from Sleep Collection collection — do you have it with you?'`,"Qu said, 'The woodblocks for that were long ago split up for firewood. It is a worthless thing I wrote in my youth, without the least sense or reason.' Ziyun said, 'I also heard there was a section of couplets matching opera titles.' Qu said, 'There were several dozen — I cannot remember them now.' Cixian said, 'We ourselves produced a good many the other day,' and, gesturing toward Qinyan, Huifang, and Baozhu, he added, 'These three, along with one Wang Guibao, matched a great number of pairs — and their results were somewhat better than ours.' He sent a servant to his study to fetch a sheet of paper on which the results of that session had been written out, with the participants' names noted against each entry — including also the word-game from the earlier occasion. Qu read it through and praised it repeatedly. 'I would not have expected these four to be capable of this — they are truly of our own circle. I am genuinely fortunate to meet them today. In the theatrical world I have encountered many over the years — but they generally amount to dashing off a few orchid-and-bamboo sketches, smearing out a few seven-character quatrains, and even those half-baked at best. To find something like this — I have never seen it in my life. No wonder you gentlemen hold them in such affection. What a pity this old man was born forty years too early — otherwise I too might have joined the company of admirers.' All present, seeing his pleasure and appreciation, were thoroughly delighted.","On the other side of the table, Zhongqing asked, 'Your collection of bronze and stone inscriptions is extensive, and you are known for the precision of your assessments. Of seal script, clerical script, and carved steles, which examples do you consider the finest?' Qu said, 'Seal script is not much studied in recent times. Take the Mount Heng Stele, traditionally said to contain seventy-seven characters, located on the Miyun Peak of Mount Heng. As late as the Song, in the Jiading reign, a certain He Zhi visited the Southern Peak, made a rubbing of the text, and had it re-engraved at Yuelu; Yang Yongxiu had it re-engraved again in Yunnan; Yang Shiqiao re-engraved it yet again at Qixia — a chain of re-engravings about which I shall not say more. I once attempted a rendering of the text as follows:",`"The Thearch spoke: Alas — with loyal support you serve my ministers." "Islets and shoals do you mount — the gateway of birds and beasts." "Your person entered the great flood, and in brightness Yu arose." "Long wandering, forgetting home, you lodged at the Yuelu courtyard." "Your wisdom shaped and your form yielded; your heart left nothing unattended." "You went forth to seek stability — Hua Yue and Tai Heng." "Ancestral succession the tasks assembled; your toil and your spirit [rested]." "Dense the blockages, confused the migrations — southward the floods abated and came to fruition." "An enduring provision of food was established; the ten thousand kingdoms found their peace; the wanderers danced and fled forever." [These are the seventy-seven characters in total.] Wang Yuanmei remarked: 'The inscription's phrasing does not accord with the holy classics — it resembles the language of Zhou dynasty bronze inscriptions or the Travels of King Mu.' This is a perceptive observation.`,"Next in order comes King Wu of Zhou's Bronze Plate Inscription, which reads:",`"To the left a forest, to the right a spring; behind a ridge, before a road." "For the peace of ten thousand generations, this place shall be treasured." [Can this truly be the language of the Three Dynasties? That it is a forgery admits of no doubt.] As for the Stone Drum inscriptions: Zheng Qiao's contention that they date from after the time of Duke Hui of Qin, and the three grounds for doubt advanced by Ouyang, are both insufficient. Wei Yingwu's attribution to the time of King Wen, with the poems engraved at King Xuan's command, and Ma Ziqing's claim that they were made during the Northern Zhou of the Yuwen clan, are even more baseless. Only the evidence cited by Dong and Cheng, which draws on the account in Zuo Tradition of King Cheng's royal hunt at Qiyang, is solidly documented and reliable. After these, the Mount Yi Inscription of Qin was re-engraved in the Chunhua reign of Song by Zheng Wenbao — it still preserves something of the ancient seal style. Among Han clerical script, the finest is the Ritual Vessels Stele of the Confucian Temple, which stands first; next is the Han stele to Cao Jingwan — one being miraculous and wholesome in its primitiveness, the other rich and lofty in magnificence. As for the Memorials of Accession and Received Abdication stelae of the Wei, and the stele honouring Confucius, together with the Zhang Jun Panegyric by the Northern Wei Prefect of Luye, Li Zhongxuan's Stele for the Restoration of the Confucian Temple, and others of that kind — they show varying degrees of excellence and inferiority. Once Han clerical script had been lost, how much more so everything that came after.' Zhongqing expressed his admiration.`,"Chunhang said, 'The Preface to the Orchid Pavilion has been debated endlessly, and even the Dingwu edition is said to exist in two versions. Now that the question of authentic and spurious has been settled, how does one tell them apart?' Qu said, 'The Orchid Pavilion text was engraved in the Zhenguan reign of Tang Taizong. Before Taizong became emperor, while still the Prince of Qin, he obtained it from the monk Biancai. In the tenth year of Zhenguan he commissioned Tang Pu, Feng Chengsu, Zhuge Zhen, and Zhao Mo each to make traced copies to be presented to favoured ministers. At that time both Chu Suiliang and Ouyang Xun made their own hand-copies, which were universally admired. What is called the Dingwu edition is Ouyang's version. The Tang silk version is Chu's copy. In those days Ouyang's stone-engraving was kept in the imperial archives, but during the upheaval caused by Later Jin and the Khitan incursion, the stone was carried off and discarded at Shahukou. It was subsequently recovered by Prefect Li Jingwen of Dingwu, who placed it in his treasury. During the Xining period, when Xue Shizheng went out as governor, he had a second version cut to satisfy demand. This is why Dingwu has both a genuine and a spurious engraving. Xue Shizheng's son Xue Daozhu then made yet another copy on a different stone, secretly substituting this for the ancient original, and further chiselled away the characters 湍, 流, 带, 左, 右 in the ancient stele to create distinguishing marks. In the Daguan period an imperial order was issued to take it from his descendant Xiangneng for installation in the Xuanhe Hall; after the Jingkang catastrophe it was lost. Then in the Hongzhi reign of the Ming dynasty it was recovered from a Heavenly Master's hermitage and placed in the Imperial Academy, whereupon Ouyang's version came back into view. Chu's silk copy was in those early times widely distributed to prefectural academies throughout the land — the Yingshang stone and the Changzhi County stone both derived from it. Later, in the Ming period, a well in Yingshang gave off light at night like a rainbow, and the county magistrate, a man named Xun, thought it strange. He had the ground excavated and found the Orchid Pavilion, together with a bronze lock and several śarīra relics, which Magistrate Xun then took home. Where it has ended up since, nobody knows. As for the various hand-copied versions by different masters — they are beyond counting, and you gentlemen have your own discerning eyes; there is no need for me to say anything further.' ","Chunhang also said, 'People say that Han stelae and Song model calligraphy books each stand supreme for their era. The four great model books — Chunhua, Daguan, Jiang tie, and Tan tie — how do they compare?' Qu said, 'In my view, Chunhua ranks first, Daguan second, Jiang tie third, and Tan tie fourth. Yet Song connoisseurs often held Tan tie to be superior to the palace model books, and also maintained that Chunhua, being the first of its kind, was limited by the inadequate skill of Wang Zhu as the copyist and could not match the refinement of Daguan. Nevertheless, Chunhua has a quality of simple and unaffected vigour, while Daguan is brilliant but somewhat showy — compared in terms of poetry, Chunhua is the High Tang and Daguan the gradual decline toward the Late Tang.'","Everyone present expressed their admiration and assent.",`Ziyu asked, 'A moment ago you drew the distinction between the High and Late Tang in poetry. In my own view, Li Bai, Du Fu, and Han Yu are the natural summit of Tang poetry. Yet Wang Anshi, in ranking poets, placed Du above Li, and in his selection of four poets — Du, Li [Bo], Ouyang [Xiu], and Li [Bai] — put Li Bai fourth. Yuan Zhen also held Du Fu superior to Li Bai, as his views make clear in the Epitaph of the Works Department. Even given Li Bai's towering genius, it seems there are those who find him wanting in certain respects. Han Changli, on the other hand, wrote: "The writings of Li and Du endure, a blaze ten thousand zhang long. How can those foolish children know to what use their slanderous words are put? Trying to shake a great tree with the strength of an ant, a laughable lack of self-knowledge." This was clearly an expression of genuine and heartfelt admiration. How then should one arrive at a balanced judgement?'`,`Qu said, 'Poetry speaks to us through the natural affinity of our own spirits. Those whose hearts resonate with Li Bai will love Li Bai; those who share a kinship with Du Fu will love Du Fu; those fortunate enough to encompass both will naturally admire both. Yuan Zhen, with his careless, unpolished prose and rather drooping, slack-spirited verses, was hardly a kindred spirit to Li Bai; it was only natural for him to elevate Du Fu while diminishing Li. Wang Anshi argued that Li Bai possessed only a single style, while Du was capable of encompassing all styles. This criticism misses the fundamental truth that Li Bai was equally capable of encompassing all forms; he simply disdained to trouble himself with trivial stylistic constraints, which led people to suppose he could not manage them. When most people discuss Li Bai's poetry, they invariably praise his unbridled, headlong genius and the startling power of lines that seem to rend the heavens, much like his celebrated pieces "The Road to Shu" and the "Mount Tianmu" poems, which have been widely imitated. For my part, I hold a special fondness for quieter masterpieces, such as "Raven-Roost Song" and "Crows Crying at Night." The "Raven-Roost Song" goes:`,`"When crows settle at dusk on the Gusu Terrace,
The King of Wu's palace holds the drunken Xi Shi."

"The songs of Wu and dances of Chu, the revelry not yet done,
And the western hills already clutching at half the setting sun."

"Silver arrow, golden vessel, the water-clock drips on and on;
Rising, I watch the autumn moon sink into the river's waves.
The east grows slowly bright: what can be done with so much joy?"

And the "Crows Crying at Night" goes:`,`"Yellow clouds by the city wall, the crow about to roost,
Flying home with harsh cries, perching to call on the branch."

"The woman of Qin, weaving brocade at her loom,
Speaks through the gauze blue as smoke that veils her window."

"She drops the shuttle, lost in longing for her man so far away,
Sleeping alone in an empty room, tears falling like rain."

Both of these poems demonstrate a lofty talent and a transcendent spirit that are in perfect harmony with Chen Zi'ang [Chen Zhuyi]. Moreover, Li Bai's own remarks on poetry are revealing: "Since the Liang and Chen dynasties, the poetic manner has grown effete and thin to the extreme; Shen Yue further imposed his tone-pattern rules. If the ancient way is to be restored, who but myself?" Consequently, he wrote very little regulated verse, and he often maintained that when it comes to conveying profound and subtle feelings, the five-character line is inferior to the four-character line, and the seven-character line the most dissipated of all. In my estimation, Li Bai's poetry reaches back to carry forward the ancient traditions, while Du Fu's poetry opens up the pathways for all that was to come. This is a subtle distinction, but it places both masters far beyond the grasp of those who merely tinker with tonal patterns and mechanical metrics. As for Han Changli's ancient-style verse, it connects directly to the timeless Odes and Hymns, echoing the enduring style of the Western Capital [Han dynasty]. His five- and seven-character ancient verse, in particular, delights in straining after the unusual, producing a hundred variations of wild eccentricity, transmitting what Li Bai and Du Fu never transmitted. Reading works like his "South Mountain" reveals a poetic force that challenges the supremacy of classical rhapsodies like the "Two Capitals" and the "Three Capitals". Those readers who lack both his vast breadth of learning and his sheer creative power see only pages of dense and jagged difficulty. For this reason, his poetry is sometimes dismissed merely as "literary prose," with some critics going so far as to claim that his rhymed writings are practically unreadable. Such a view is no different from hearing the divine music of heaven and complaining that the notes are poorly arranged. It must be noted, however, that his five- and seven-character quatrains and his regulated verse were not his primary concern; they represent just one facet of poetry's vast possibilities, and he never intended for future generations to learn the art of poetry solely from those shorter, simpler pieces.' This discourse was so cogent that everyone present nodded in agreement, and even Baozhu, Huifang, and the others were quite capable of following it.`,"Ziyu said, 'I have now received your instruction on the art of poetry. On the question of rhyme and the distinctions between standard, transferred, and merged rhymes — this system began to take shape in the Wei and Jin period. Thus Li Deng compiled his Poetic Rhymes, Lü Jing his Collected Rhymes, and in the Qi and Zhou dynasties a work on the Four Tones and切韵 was compiled; Shen Yue of the Liang composed his Four Tones in one chapter, and so the rhyme tables were established. In the Sui, Lu Fayan, Liu Zhen, and others, drawing on Shen Yue's principles, compiled a further Expanded Rhymes; in the Tang, Guo Zhixuan produced his Rhyme-Cuts; Sun [Mian] compiled the Tang Rhymes; Ding Du and Song Qi compiled the Collected Rhymes. After the Jingyun era, a further Ministry of Rites Rhymes was produced; there were also Wang Zongdao's Rhyme-Cuts, Wu Yu's Supplemented Rhymes, and in the Yuan, Yin Shifu's Forest of Rhymes in Groups. With all these systems for combining or separating rhyme categories, which arrangement of rhymes is ultimately to be considered correct?'","Qu said, 'The debates in rhyme scholarship have each school drawing on its own authorities for the merged and transferred categories. Shen Yue established a standard for the eight regional dialects using the Yue dialect — how could that possibly cover all of them satisfactorily? And the same character may be pronounced one way by a dental and palatal speaker, yet another way when palatal and dental are reversed. Even the native dialects of the five regions are difficult to reconcile precisely — which is why the zhi and yuan rhyme groups are the most chaotic, and one genuinely does not know what regional speaker could pronounce them all as a single rhyme. In former times the rhymes were divided into two hundred and six groups; it was only from the Chunyou era onward that Liu Yuan of Pingshui consolidated them into one hundred and seven groups. The Expanded Rhymes counts twenty-six thousand one hundred and ninety-four characters; the Collected Rhymes counts fifty-three thousand five hundred and twenty-five characters; the Ministry of Rites Rhymes includes only nine thousand five hundred and ninety characters; Mao Huang's Rhyme Supplement added two thousand six hundred and fifty-five characters to the Ministry of Rites table; Liu Pingshui's Abridged Ministry of Rites Rhymes added a further four hundred and sixty-three characters — and so the ancient books were thoroughly transformed. Critics have said that the failure of rhyme scholarship lay not in the division into two hundred and six groups, but in their consolidation into one hundred and seven groups. Yin Shifu, in turn, comparing his work against the Ministry of Rites table, Mao Huang, and Liu Pingshui, deleted more than three thousand one hundred characters, including some that were ancient and elegant but had fallen into corrupt popular usage. Huang Gongshao's Rhyme Treasury Jade Collection divided and combined according to Mao and Liu's systems and provided quite rich annotations and notes, adding twelve thousand six hundred and fifty-two characters — a not negligible contribution. But its arrangement, being bound to the system of seven sounds and thirty-six initial consonant groups, has in turn attracted criticism from later scholars. The rhymes in use today are essentially those of Shen Yue; but ancient rhyme transferences seem more reliable than modern ones. Zhang Fu's Collected Essentials of Rhyme Learning calibrated and fixed the four tones, and from this the transferences and mergers of ancient rhyme can be extrapolated by category. If one examines the ancient songs and poems of the Odes, Hymns, and Li Sao in the light of this, the differences between ancient and modern transferences become readily apparent.' Ziyu rose from his seat and bowed in thanks.","Nanxiang said, 'The ancients who discoursed on the Book of Changes spoke of principle and not of number; scholars today speak of number and not of principle. Can number really fully encompass principle? Moreover, since Shao Kangjie [Shao Yong] no true transmission of the system has survived. As for what is now called the Great Yi numerology, it is used to verify the fate of dynasties, the incidence of calamity and fortune, of war, flood, and fire, and to determine a person's rank and station. The calculation of the Yangjiu and Baili cycles yields uncannily accurate correspondences to historical events — might I be permitted to hear something of this teaching?'","Qu said, 'After the Southern Song, a certain Wang Di composed the Three Scrolls of the Taichi Elbow-Backup Formulary, covering the yin and yang excursions and drawing one hundred and forty-four diagrams. The work uses the Great Yi system to illuminate the virtuous and wicked acts of sovereigns, with the specific calculation of the Yangjiu and Baili cycles proceeding as follows: four hundred and fifty-six years for one Yangjiu cycle, two hundred and eighty-eight years for one Baili cycle. Yangjiu is an odd number — the exhaustion of yang; Baili is an even number — the exhaustion of yin. According to Wang Di: the chaos of Hou Yi and Han Zhuo corresponds to the seventh Yangjiu count; the decline and weakness of King Nan corresponds to the eighth; the feebleness of Emperors Huan and Ling to the ninth; the destruction of Emperor Yang of Sui to the tenth. Verified against the historical chronology, each is precisely on the mark. He further states: the reign of King Li and then the five-time fallen King You of Zhou corresponds to the twelfth Baili count; during King Jing's time, when Wu and Yue fought each other and the realm was in turmoil, this corresponds to the thirteenth; the destruction of the Six States by Qin corresponds to the fourteenth; the migration of Eastern Jin and the splitting of the realm into sixteen kingdoms corresponds to the extreme of the Baili count, and then its return to unity; the chaos of the Five Dynasties corresponds to the third Baili count. These Baili correspondences have verifiable evidence. Yet there are also cases that do not correspond: the perfect governance of Shun and Yu, the model for ten thousand generations, falls at the seventh Baili count; the period of Kings Cheng and Kang, during which punishments went unused for forty-odd years, falls at the eleventh; the reigns of Xiao Jia and Yong Yi fall at the fifth Yangjiu and ninth Baili counts; the reigns of Geng Ding and Wu Yi fall at the sixth Yangjiu; Bujian's reign of fifty-nine years falls at the eighth Baili count; the period of Pan Geng and Xiao Xin falls at the tenth Baili count; Emperors Ming and Zhang of Han, following Guangwu in bringing about great peace, fall at the fifteenth Baili count; the twenty-third year of the Tang Zhenguan period falls at the second Baili count. All of these fail to correspond — why is that? And more remarkably: the exile of Jie of Xia to Nanchao, the fall of Zhou of Shang at Muye, Wang Mang's usurpation of Han, and An Lushan's rebellion against Tang — none of these coincide with any Yangjiu or Baili count either. What then explains that? This is why I say number cannot overcome principle. Principle arises from nature itself; number seems to be predetermined. For this reason the sages spoke of principle and not of number — number is but one thread within the whole of principle.'",`Nanxiang said, 'A truly bold and penetrating argument — it dissolves doubts that have accumulated through the ages.' Cixian said, 'Let us set aside the great risings and fallings of the world, and attend to what stands before us in this cup. I have an extremely trivial and vulgar point I wish to put to you. I have read in the Yuejue shu [Book of Yue Annals] the saying that "the sagely spring forth from the sage-stock, the mad from the mad-stock; the osmanthus fruit produces osmanthus, the tung fruit produces tung." And yet I have repeatedly observed dull-witted and stupid parents producing children of the most brilliant intelligence and striking beauty. Looking at the fathers and mothers, there seems to be no such cause — no prior condition, whether innate or acquired — that could account for such a marvellous result. How does this come to be?'`,"Qu laughed and said, 'That is a difficult principle to explain fully. Yet the Qimin Yaoshu [Essential Techniques for the Common People] contains the following on the method of growing pear trees: one pear yields ten seeds, but only two of them produce pear trees, while the rest become wild pears. Duan Chengshi wrote: a goshawk produces three chicks, one of which becomes a kite. The Classic of Birds states: the stork produces three chicks, one of which becomes a crane. The Huashu [Book of Transformations] says: the summer sparrow produces quail chicks; the Chu dove produces a certain bird; the Nanhai ji [Record of the South Sea] states: the crocodile produces hundreds of offspring, of which only twelve become crocodiles — the rest become soft-shelled turtles or giant turtles, each transforming according to its pneumatic endowment. Extending this principle further: sages do not necessarily produce sages; worthy men do not necessarily produce worthy men. Earlier scholars said that Yang Xiong ought to have descendants but Zhang Tang ought not — yet can human shortsighted wisdom really determine the patterns of heaven? Principle has its constants, but also its variations. Is it not possible that some are moved by surrounding pneumata to transform their fundamental constitution? Or perhaps a foolish husband and a stupid wife, though dull of outward appearance, have some aspect of their seven emotions and six desires that is not quite so dull, and from this one spark of brightness a child is formed — who knows?' Everyone burst out laughing.","Ziyun said, 'Among the beautiful people of antiquity, their physical loveliness has been preserved only in the words and brushes of those who wrote about them. Among all the poems, prose-poems, and literary descriptions, those who described beautiful women are beyond counting on one's fingers — but which piece of writing has rendered the subject most perfectly? Will you indicate one or two for us?'",`Qu said, 'All the writers of antiquity are admirable — how can one single any out? But the piece of writing that depicts a beautiful woman most fittingly, and that every reader would unanimously acclaim as superb, is none other than the description of Lady Zhuang Jiang of Wei in the ode "The Stately Lady" [Shuo ren, Ode 57 of the Classic of Poetry]. It opens: "Stately and tall is the lady, robed in embroidered silk under plain gauze" — two lines that already make radiance shoot from the page. Then from "Her neck is like the larva of the tree-borer" to "her beautiful eyes glisten" — every character conveys perfect beauty, without a single filler phrase and not a single borrowed image. As the saying goes, to write of the moon is to write the full moon; to write of a flower is to write the flower in bloom — all misty clouds and propping mountains have been swept away. That is the most difficult achievement of all. If one describes the richness of dress and adornment, or the elegance of bearing, one has still not shown how the eyebrows, eyes, nose, and mouth are arranged in relation to each other. Song Yu's "Rhapsody on the Goddess" makes its attempt, saying: "When she first appeared, she blazed forth like the white sun newly risen, illuminating a roof-beam; as she drew a little nearer, she shone pure and bright like the full moon releasing its light" — all this is doing is saying how brilliantly she shone. "Pure and bright" may still pass, but comparing a person to "the white sun" and "a roof-beam" is incongruous. Then Cao Zijian's "Rhapsody on the Goddess of the Luo" borrows the same idea: "seen from afar, she was bright as the sun rising through morning clouds." And "The Divine Woman" also writes: "Suddenly she changed expression, graceful as a wandering dragon riding the clouds" — and then the Luo River rhapsody again borrows the line verbatim: "Like a startled wild goose in flight, graceful as a wandering dragon." That is truly a failure to comprehend the original: to compare a beautiful woman to a wandering dragon — I do not know what the author could have been thinking. Then there is Song Yu's "Rhapsody on Lust," which says: "Add a tenth of an inch and she is too tall; take away a tenth of an inch and she is too short." As a general statement, all well and good — but if one must examine the matter precisely, there is a logical flaw. If adding a tenth makes her too tall, she is truly tall, and subtracting a tenth should not make her too short. If subtracting a tenth makes her too short, she is truly short, and adding a tenth should not make her too tall. That is a case of literary exaggeration. In prose fiction there are some where the delineation is so thorough, saying what no one else has dared to say, that readers' eyes swim and their minds are transported, with the character vivid on the page — and none surpasses the description of the young woman Ying's body in the Miscellaneous Secret Affairs [Miscellaneous Secret Affairs]. Consider this passage — let me read it to you carefully: "The female official came with an imperial warrant to spend time with Ying in her sleeping quarters. Dismissing the attendants and barring the inner door, at a moment when the sundial showed the hours passing, sunlight came through the frosted-glass window and shone upon Ying's face, his radiance shining like morning clouds mixed with snow so brilliant one could not look straight at her. Her eyes shone clear and fresh; her brows were delicate and curved; her mouth was red and her teeth white; her ears were long and her nose well-proportioned; cheeks, dimples, chin, and jaw were all perfectly arranged. The female official then removed Ying's hairpin and unloosed her hair, combing it out length by length — black as lacquer-ware, lustrous enough to see one's reflection in, wound in the hand eight coils, and when let down, extending a half-handful beyond the floor. Then she asked that Ying relax her undergarments. Ying's face went crimson and she tried to block the request. The female official told Ying: "The imperial household is observing the proprieties; this wretched person is merely here to look — once these garments are relaxed, they will be replaced by official robes and adornments." Ying wept several tears, closed her eyes, and turned her face inward. The female official then gently loosened the garments with her hands, supporting them as the sunlight came through; a fragrant warmth rose up, and the skin was fine and smooth, the hand gliding over it without catching. Rounded in front and square behind, she was as though hewn from fat and carved from jade. The breasts swelled like budding beans; the navel could hold half an inch of a pearl. The private area was raised and full; drawing apart both thighs, the inner channel was moist and crimson, as if a fire-gem were about to emerge. Truly a woman who had strictly observed decorum, a virgin in the fullest sense. To estimate Ying's body overall: her blood was sufficient to nourish her skin; her skin was sufficient to adorn her flesh; her flesh was sufficient to clothe her bones. Proportion between long and short was perfectly maintained. From crown to heel she measured seven feet one inch in height; her shoulders were one foot six inches wide; her hips were three inches narrower than her shoulders; from each shoulder to the tips of the fingers, two feet seven inches; the fingers four inches from the palm — resembling ten bamboo shoots newly peeled. From the thigh to the foot, two feet two inches; the foot eight inches long; calves and instep shapely and fine, the sole flat and the toes neatly gathered. About them thin silk was wrapped close as a band of gauze, binding them minutely as if they were confined within the palace; and for a long while there was no sound. The female official told her to thank the Emperor for ten thousand years; Ying then slowly bowed and said: "Ten thousand years to the Emperor" — like a gentle wind stirring a flute, a soft and pleasing murmur to the ear." Though the passage is improper in content, for precision of delineation nothing surpasses it.'`,`Everyone said, 'Quite so — from ancient times to the present, no one has ever taken someone's measurements in such detail.' Ziyu said, 'The binding of feet is said to have originated with Consort Pan of the Later Chen dynasty. Yet the passage in the Mixin — "thin silk wrapped close as a band of gauze, binding them minutely as if they were confined within the palace" — is that not an even earlier origin of foot-binding?'`,`Qu said, 'That is merely a loose binding to prevent them from spreading freely. If one reads the words "calves and instep shapely and fine, the sole flat and the toes neatly gathered," it seems not at all like the tight and severe binding of today that forces feet into the pointed shape of a lotus petal.' Huifang said, 'And how do those measurements work out? Body height seven feet one inch; shoulders one foot six inches wide — how is this calculated? By today's measurements, no one could be as tall as that.' Qu said, 'These are Han dynasty measurements. Against the Board of Works construction inch of the present day, each Han inch is worth only seven and a half fen. And the Board of Works construction inch is only nine point three fen against the common tailor's inch used in the marketplace. Converted to the construction inch: seven times seven makes four feet nine, five times seven makes three point five inches, adding seven and a half fen gives five feet three inches and two and a half fen in height. If reckoned further in today's tailor's inch: five times nine makes four feet five, three times nine makes two point seven, add two point two fen, for a total of roughly four feet eight inches. That is quite a tall body, about the same as you, perhaps a little taller. Shoulders one foot six inches: converted to construction inch gives one foot one and a half inches; in tailor's inch, one foot one inch and a fraction. Hips three inches narrower than shoulders: in today's tailor's inch, the lower body is only eight inches and a fraction wide — indicating a slender and fine build. Arm length from shoulder to fingertip, two feet seven inches: in construction inch, about two feet and two and a half fen; in tailor's inch, about one foot eight inches. Thigh to foot, three feet two inches: in construction inch two feet four inches; in tailor's inch two feet one inch and six fen, showing perfectly balanced proportions between the upper and lower body. Foot length eight inches: in construction inch six inches; in tailor's inch five inches four fen. This is still different from a bound foot, and not excessively small. Taken all together, the figure is somewhat on the tall side. Then again, ancient beauties were generally described as tall and stately.' Cixian said, 'You have made a remarkably detailed calculation. It is possible that when the female official was measuring, she made a small error and recorded an inch too many — one never knows.' This made everyone laugh.`,"Master Qu then said, 'There is a supremely cultivated and erudite person currently in the capital — young in years but an old acquaintance of mine, and also from a great Nanjing family. In terms of family standing, his lineage is on a par with Ziyun's and Xingbei's, and I am sure you all know him well.' Ziyun said, 'Who might that be?' Master Qu said, 'His family name is Jin, given name Su, courtesy name Jifu — are you acquainted with him?' Everyone said together, 'We have long heard his name but have never yet had the honour of meeting him.' Master Qu said, 'In scholarship, learning, and personal character he is among the very first or second in the world today. He also has a prose work — a historical novel about the suppression of the Japanese pirates. I have forgotten the title. I read through it once, and the prose style is magnificently forceful. The two rebel leaders — King of Settled Rivers and Chancellor of Stilled Seas — are excoriated with such biting precision that it is truly the brush of a man of genius.' Cixian said, 'Such traitors and rebels have inflicted misery on countless lives; every person's blood boils to speak of them. Such a denunciation is a cause for rejoicing; I must find a copy to read one day.' Master Qu said, 'Yet his luck in life has been very poor, and his great talents have never been fully employed. It is a true shame.' Baozhu quickly said, 'How fortunate this gentleman is — to find, today, a true friend who understands him.' Master Qu said, 'Are you on close terms with this gentleman, Yaoqing?' Sulan, who was beside Baozhu, said, 'He taught Yaoqing painting, calligraphy, and the qin. Only last month they spent two days together at Cuiwei Mountain. His regard for this gentleman is no less than Meixiang's regard for Xiangfan.' Baozhu smiled and said, 'Surely not quite to that degree.' Ziyu said, 'I once saw at Yaoqing's some of his brushwork — supremely elegant and refined, with much of the quality of Tang Liuru [Tang Yin].' Master Qu said, 'Not only is his brushwork like Tang Liuru's — his horoscope is also like Tang Liuru's, which is extraordinary. How can the ancient and modern coincide so exactly? This is another case where principle alone is insufficient and number must be invoked. I am still planning to call on him tomorrow.' Ziyun said quickly, 'Will you not introduce me first? To acquire such a fine friend would itself be a great delight.' Master Qu said, 'An excellent idea — excellent!'","Baozhu said, 'This gentleman is rather reclusive and not fond of society.' Master Qu said, 'With these gentlemen I think he would find himself perfectly at ease.' That day Master Qu had talked at length from beginning to end — everyone was weary. After finishing the meal they sat about in a relaxed manner for a while; it was already about the second watch. Liu Wenze, being a former student of his, did not venture to ask difficult questions. Baozhu asked Ziyun for a folding fan so that he could request a poem from Master Qu; Ziyun went further and had four fans brought out, one for each of the four performers. Thereupon Baozhu wiped the small table clean, Huifang shifted the ink-stone into position, Sulan ground the ink, and Qinyan moistened the brush — all four together asked Master Qu to write a tribute. Master Qu was greatly pleased; he captured the general character of each person and composed a regulated poem of seven-character lines for each, written in a semi-formal semi-cursive hand modelled on Yu Shinan, with a double inscription. The four performers gave thanks, they chatted a while longer, and then the company dispersed. As to what happened next, listen to the following chapter for the explanation."]},w=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations38:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),r={39:[`To return to our story: Wang Xun had been unable to keep his appointment at the Yi Garden the other day, for Sun Lianggong had engaged him to help arrange the wedding, and he had spent several days running about on the matter. The ceremony was set for the tenth day of the second month, which was not many days off. At the New Year, Li Xingquan had sent several hundred taels of silver to Yuanmao, along with a letter to Wang Wenhui asking him to arrange his son's marriage. Wang Wenhui had no patience for matchmaking and delegated everything to Wang Xun. Li Yuanmao then sought out Wei Pincai and begged him to take charge of all the arrangements. Wei Pincai had been carousing for over a month, spending, losing at cards, and frittering away a good deal of money. As the theft case had still not been resolved, so his spirits were rather low and he was at a loss for what to do. When Li Yuanmao came to him, Pincai reflected that he himself had originally served as co-matchmaker with Wang Wenhui, and that this would give him a convenient pretext for visiting Wenhui again. So he agreed, then added, "It is time you returned that bracelet you borrowed from me last year. Every day you delay, the interest mounts." Yuanmao said, "Father only sent three hundred taels, and I'm afraid that's barely enough to cover the wedding. I've nowhere else to borrow, so if you press me on that debt, you'll be the death of me." Pincai said, "What a strange thing to say. How does asking for what's mine put you to death? What did you tell me last year? That you'd repay me the moment you heard from home. Now I find it hard even to ask." Yuanmao said, "Don't worry. Once I've moved into the house, I'll redeem it for you." Pincai said, "Once you've moved in, you'll have even less money." Yuanmao said, "I may not have money, but she ought to." Pincai said, "She? Who?" Yuanmao laughed. "My wife, of course. Not just this one debt, but I have all sorts of plans that depend on her." Pincai laughed. "Your wife is going to be a source of funds?" Yuanmao said, "What an absurd idea!" Pincai said, "But you just said so yourself that you were going to draw on her." Yuanmao said, "I only mean that she'll have a dowry, and once she's married to me it'll all be mine anyway. Would I really be unable to get a single bracelet back?" Pincai said, "A man who lives off his wife's purse is no man at all." Yuanmao said, "What's the harm? It's not like living off someone's shame." The two men joked and laughed for a while, and then Yuanmao left.`,`The following day Pincai called on Wang Wenhui, but Wenhui had gone into the yamen; Wang Xun received him instead. They then went together to pay their respects to Lianggong, exchanged the usual pleasantries, mentioning that they were currently sojourning away from home with no one to look after things, and begged for his forbearance. Lianggong said, "We are joining hands because we love the same family; all this formality can be dispensed with. I don't want to cost the other side a single cash. Leave everything to me." He then kept Pincai to dine.`,"Three days before the wedding came the exchange of betrothal gifts, and Pincai had no choice but to go and find Yuanmao, which meant calling on Madam Yan as well. Having stayed away for several months and having caused that unpleasant scene the previous year, he was quite ill at ease. Madam Yan, for her part, asked nothing about past events and exchanged only a few bland words with him. When Pincai went to see Ziyu, the latter still remembered what Qinyan had said some days earlier and bore him a quiet grudge and was noticeably less warm toward him than before.","All the arrangements for Yuanmao's affairs were handled by Mei Jin, who procured hairpins and bracelets, embroidered silks and gowns, and borrowed from Madam Yan a pearl crown, jade sash, official rank-badge robes with court beads, embroidered dragon skirt, and other finery, arranging everything in sixteen lacquered boxes and erecting a ceremonial bower to make a respectable display. The two matchmakers escorted the gifts over. The Sun household accepted them and sent back the return-boxes with gifts of commensurate value, nothing particularly rare or precious.",`On the auspicious day the household of the Mei family saw to all the preparations, laying on two tables of wine, one for Madam Yan and one for the matchmakers, while inviting Ziyu and Yan Zhongqing to keep them company. Zhongqing said, "Brother Yuan, tonight you truly arrive at the summit of the Jade Mountain." Wang Xun said, "A life of glory and splendor all the way to white-haired age." Ziyu said, "'Still they say the lamplight shows two shadows face to face, the more you rub your eyes, the blurrier they grow.' That is a poem about a nearsighted man in the bridal chamber; it would do nicely for Brother Yuan this evening." Yuanmao said, "I've always thought nearsightedness had its advantages; even if the bride is a bit plain, you can't see her clearly." Zhongqing said, "But if she should turn out to be beautiful, wouldn't that be a waste?" Yuanmao laughed. "I rather doubt my bride will be a beauty. I've heard a few things, so I'll just hope she doesn't resemble the two brothers in looks." When the auspicious hour arrived, they all escorted Yuanmao to the Sun residence, where he was welcomed with drums and music. The young lady in question was the daughter of Lianggong's first wife; the current Madam Sun did not dote on her especially, and so the whole affair was conducted simply. Among the female guests, only the sister-in-law of Madam Lu, the wife of Lu Zongyuan, had come, bringing her young daughter. The groom's family had offered their congratulations in the morning. It was Ji Liangxuan who was making a show of festivity; he was privately hoping to rowdy up the bridal chamber, and had the two useless brothers to receive him.`,`Yuanmao and his new bride performed the wedding rites before the bridal candles, were escorted to the bridal chamber, sat upon the bed while wedding verses were scattered over the curtains, drank from the crossed cups, and then the groom was called back out to sit at the great banquet table. Sihui and Siyuan kept them company for a while, until Wang Xun and Zhongqing proposed moving the party into the bridal chamber to drink more freely. Everyone filed into the bridal chamber. Zhongqing said, "Tonight we get to look at the new bride," and made as if to approach the bed. By the bed stood two elderly escort-women and two small maidservants. Siyuan, afraid that Zhongqing might lay eyes on his sister, ran to the bed and seized hold of the curtain, managing to push out a few "look" sounds and then, with a great effort, the two words "not permitted," which made the whole company burst out laughing. Wang Xun pulled Zhongqing over and made him sit down, but Siyuan still wasn't reassured and kept his grip on the curtain door, so that everyone continued to laugh under their breath. Sihui said, "The marriage chamber is the foundation of the greatest human bond; how should outsiders intrude? We may count ourselves fortunate that a brother guards the bedside against all insult. Otherwise, that which is white as snow would be viewed by ten pairs of eyes." Ziyu heard this and broke into laughter. Wang Xun said to Zhongqing, "It is as the old text says: 'Arouse not my temper, lest even the dragon bark.'" Zhongqing smiled faintly. Li Yuanmao drank his wine with an air of supreme self-satisfaction.`,`Ji Liangxuan had met Wang Xun and Zhongqing on several previous occasions, but this was his first meeting with Ziyu, whom he eyed with private admiration: "This young master Mei is a fine-looking man. Compared to that old Sihui of the Sun family, it's like comparing the heroic generals Cen Peng and Ma Wu to some stage ruffian." Pincai asked Ji Liangxuan, "I haven't seen your master come out in days, what's he doing at home?" Liangxuan said, "My master has been indisposed these past couple of days and hasn't been able to get about." Pincai said, "What sort of indisposition?" Liangxuan said, "I hear it's a boil on his leg; that's why he hasn't gone out." The table that evening was divided into three separate clusters: Ziyu, Zhongqing, and Wang Xun formed one group; the Sun brothers Sihui and Siyuan another; and and Pincai and Liangxuan yet a third, so the gathering never warmed into real conviviality. Wang Xun, always amiable by nature, saw how the company was failing to mix and made a determined effort to draw everyone together. By this time the seating in the bridal chamber had been taken without any ceremony of deference. Pincai and Liangxuan sat on one side; Zhongqing and Ziyu on another; Yuanmao sat alone at the head; and Wang Xun sat below with Sihui.`,`Someone called Siyuan to come over, but he would not leave; he pulled up a stool and planted himself in front of the bed-curtain. Ji Liangxuan turned to Ziyu with a grinning smile and said, "Elder Master Mei rarely goes out in company, and today is the first time I have had the honor of your acquaintance. If you're inclined, why not come visit my master one of these days? He is a most agreeable person." Ziyu, not knowing whose household was being spoken of, gave a vague reply, then quietly asked Wang Xun, who answered that it was Xi Shiyi. A sudden surge of indignation filled Ziyu and he said nothing more. Liangxuan then turned to Yuanmao: "Your future wife, my cousin's cousin on the other side, is a paragon of virtue, Brother Li is truly fortunate to have arranged such a fine match. Our mutual kinsman will be posted to a province before long, either Kuizhou in Sichuan or Chenzhou in Hunan. As the son-in-law of the family, you'll travel together to his posting. It won't just be as a secretary, for I should think the entire household, inside and out, will have to rely on you." Zhongqing found this amusing and could not help asking, "And how exactly are you related to the Sun household?" Liangxuan said, "The legitimate sister-in-law of Elder Sun's mother-in-law is the elder cousin on my mother's sister's side, through her legitimate niece on the paternal line, which is the recent connection. Going back to the older tie, the maternal grandfather of the late mistress of the house was the father-in-law of my father-in-law." Zhongqing started laughing. Pincai said, "That's a connection so light you'd call it eggshell blue." Liangxuan said, "Though slender, it still beats having no kin at all. I've heard a couplet that goes: 'What writings could astonish all within the seas, when not a single friend or kinsman stands at court.'"`,`He continued, "But that's a careless thing to say. All of you gentlemen here have the court full of close friends and worthy associates. Take the two members of our host's family, who speak for themselves. Brother Li, the new son-in-law, is the son of the prefect; Elder Master Mei is the son of the School Inspector; Elder Master Wang is the son of the Vice-Minister; and Elder Master Yan is the Vice-Minister's own son-in-law. Even Master Wei was once an honored guest in the household of the Lord Hua, practically a deputy to the young master. You are all men of distinction. Only this humble nobody here is a mere secretary's secretary, and I shall have to beg your generous patronage and assistance in future, your support and kind mention." This speech fairly turned everyone's stomach. Zhongqing could not contain himself and said, "The way Master Ji works out these connections, we all become kinsmen, as it merely requires a few more turns in the pedigree." Liangxuan agreed heartily. Ziyu smiled. Yuanmao said, "Not only can I not follow the connections, I couldn't even hear them properly. It's like a pumpkin vine tangled up with a bean vine." Pincai laughed and said, "And in the middle of it all you run into a man with his legs all wrapped in creepers." Sihui said, "Kin love is the way of kin, whosoever has blood and breath cannot but honor his kin. To love one's kin is humanity; humanity is humanity itself." Siyuan, hearing his elder brother open his mouth, immediately set about refuting him. "Th-th-that s-s-saying," he stuttered, "is n-n-not right. You s-say whoever has b-b-blood and breath honors their k-k-kin, meaning everyone is your kin. I th-think only the c-c-crab has no blood, while the turtle still has blood, so I suppose the t-t-turtle is your kinsman too. I haven't g-g-got so many relations myself." He burst out in a loud laugh, and the whole room laughed with him. Sihui said, "What a fool, not worth speaking to." Siyuan said, "I'm not a f-f-fool, for you're the one who's d-d-dead. 'The dead man prizes nothing, but the humane man prizes his kin.'" The company laughed all the louder at this.`,`Zhongqing said, "I have a ready-made joke for the occasion. One day the Dragon King of the Sea was releasing the various monsters to be reborn into the world, and had already freed a great many. At last a night-patrolling Yaksha dug two creatures out of the mud and begged the Dragon King to let them go. The Dragon King looked: one was a soft-shelled turtle, the other a toad. The Dragon King said, 'I'm not entirely easy about letting these two go, so have them bring a guarantor.' The turtle, hearing this, pointed to the Minister Tortoise standing nearby and said, 'He is my kinsman,' and then pointed to General Snake and said, 'And he is my relation.' The Dragon King said, 'If the Minister is your kinsman, that's quite sufficient, why bring in a General as a relation as well?' The turtle replied, 'Not merely a relation, but something of a true kinsman as well. We turtles cannot father our own young; we must invite a snake to do it for us. Though the lineage is tortoise, the seed is serpent, so the snake counts as both a relation and a kinsman.' The Dragon King laughed and said, 'Since you have such distinguished kinsmen and well-connected relations, I'll let you go.' He then called the toad forward and asked, 'And do you have kinsmen and relations?' The toad said, 'Every man is my kinsman; every person is my relation.' The Dragon King said angrily, 'How can that possibly be?' The toad said, 'Our kind is born from the spare essence that men leave in the privy, so we resemble men in our form. If that doesn't make every man my kinsman and every person my relation, what does?' The Dragon King cried out in alarm: 'Release him at once! Should he linger, he will soon claim kinship with me, and I have no desire for a toad as a cousin.'" At this, Pincai, Wang Xun, and Ziyu nearly fell off their seats laughing. Sihui and Liangxuan, knowing themselves to be the butt of the joke and having no reply, had to swallow their indignation. Siyuan, however, seeing those two mocked, laughed all the louder. "A f-f-fine turtle kinsman!" he cried. "A f-f-fine toad r-r-relation!" Wang Xun said, "I have a joke of my own. There was a blind prostitute, and when men came to her she couldn't see them, but she could somehow tell them apart by rank. One day she received three clients, and her procuress afterward asked her: 'Miss, can you guess what manner of men your three visitors today were?' The blind girl said, 'The first was a scholar, the second was a criminal-law secretary, and the third was a nearsighted simpleton.' The procuress said, 'How could you tell them apart?' The blind girl said, 'The first came up very properly and carefully separated my two parts, then brought them together again, made me face one way and then the other, and I heard him murmur: softer here, firmer there; one deep, one shallow. He was composing an eight-legged essay. So I knew he was a scholar. The second came in, poked about for a while, then examined me closely. I heard him say: a scar on the left temple, a fingernail mark on the right breast, an inch and two-tenths long at a slant. Presently he said: both legs stretched straight, both arms spread wide. Wasn't that the style of a coroner examining a body-in-evidence? So I knew he was a criminal-law secretary. The third was the strangest of all. The moment he came in he peered at my private parts, and his eyebrows were like a brush, and they tickled me dreadfully. He peered, then sniffed; sniffed, then peered again. From that I knew he was a nearsighted simpleton.'" The whole company burst into laughter, even the old escort-women and the little maids laughed. And from behind the bed-curtain came a barely perceptible tremor of suppressed merriment: the bride herself was laughing, covering her mouth tightly. Siyuan said, "Th-th-that n-nearsighted one sounds just like Brother Li, and that c-c-coroner one is just like B-Brother Ji." Liangxuan laughed and said, "Not at all, not at all. I am quite certain it wasn't the coroner, it must have been the one who examines the body after the inquest." Li Yuanmao said, "I don't believe eyebrows can actually tickle." Ziyu laughed. "Your own eyebrows are by no means light." Sihui said, "Of the three, I am studying the one who composes eight-legged essays." Pincai said, "I have a joke too. Two brothers, both nearsighted, yet neither would admit it. The elder always said the younger's eyes were bad; the younger always laughed at the elder's poor sight. Next door there was a local-deity shrine that had just hung a new plaque. The two brothers agreed to go test their eyes, to see which of them could read the plaque most clearly. The two of them were unfortunately both quite short. The elder first made the younger crouch down, then climbed onto his shoulders and had him stand up, so they could get close to the plaque. The elder squinted carefully at it, then came down and said to the younger: 'Let me lift you up to look.' The younger went up in the same fashion, looked, and immediately asked his brother: 'What characters did you read?'`,"The elder said, 'I read it as a pawnshop sign, so there must be a pawnshop inside. I clearly made out the characters tu ye dang, meaning even earth can be pawned. Let's go home and carry over a couple of loads of earth to pawn.' The younger laughed: 'Brother, you've misread it. I read it as shang ta dang, meaning you've been taken in. If we haul over the earth and they won't accept it, haven't we been made fools of?' The elder thought his brother had a point, so they both went home. Another day the two brothers decided to have another contest of eyesight. The younger said, 'Brother, you shouldn't compete with me. Say you describe my appearance and I describe yours, but since we can't see ourselves, we wouldn't believe each other. Now, my sister-in-law's face I can clearly recall, and you must know your younger sister-in-law's face well enough. Let's each describe our wife's appearance and see whose eyes prove sharper.' The elder thought this was sound reasoning and nodded, but when he tried to recall his wife's face it was a blur and he couldn't bring it to mind. The younger thought it over at length and couldn't picture her either; so each hurried off indoors. The elder went home and couldn't find his wife anywhere; after some searching he found her in the mill-room, just sifting flour, her whole face powdered white as snow. He leaned in close and peered at her face very carefully, then walked out and sat down to wait for his brother. The younger ran to his own room, found the door shut, pushed it open. His wife had just taken off her trousers to step into the basin for a bath; seeing her husband enter, she was embarrassed and grabbed the nearest thing to cover her lower parts. There was a fly-whisk within reach, so she held it up over herself. The younger, seeing those trailing silky threads, was quite astonished, and stooped down to examine them closely before going out.",`He found his elder brother sitting there grinning, and asked, 'What's so funny?' The elder said, 'Brother, I confess my eyes are worse than yours. I've been married five years and only today have I seen her clearly. Who would have thought that your sister-in-law is an albino with a whole head of white hair.' The younger also sighed and said, 'Brother, sister-in-law's white hair is nothing remarkable. I just now got a good look at your younger sister-in-law, and all her intimate hair is white as well.'" The company let out a roar of laughter. Then suddenly from behind the curtain the bride's voice rang out in fury: "Who is that shameless wretch over there talking his filth! Your mother's an albino! Your grandmother's an albino!" Before she had finished, something flew out from behind the curtain and smashed two dishes of food. Everyone was so startled they looked at one another in mute bewilderment. Siyuan, seeing his sister had started cursing, leapt to his feet and joined in. The party broke up at once in embarrassment; everyone scrambled to their feet and hurried out, going their separate ways as fast as they could. Yuanmao and Sihui were left with no way to rein things in and could only see the departing guests to their carriages before going back inside.`,`As it happened, Pincai's joke, though he had aimed it at Li Yuanmao's nearsightedness, had unwittingly landed on a tender nerve where the bride was concerned. As had been mentioned before, she was an albino: her hair, eyebrows, and body hair were all white. In the north such a person is called a "Heaven's Elder"; in the south, a "white lamb." On top of this, her temper was fierce and imperious. She was already thirty years old, famed throughout the surrounding area, and no one had been willing to take her, so she had been given to Yuanmao for want of a better match. Yuanmao had had no idea. He came back into the room in high spirits, thinking to himself: "Just now Pincai's joke was aimed at my nearsightedness, and she went and cursed him for it, and threw something besides. We haven't even been properly intimate yet, and already she's defending me like that. Whoever heard of a wife as devoted as this?"`,"He hurried the maids and escort-women out, took off his outer robe, bolted the door, trimmed the candle to a good bright flame, lifted the curtain, drew aside the red veil, and held the lamp close. Yuanmao felt his bones go soft and his sinews melt with delight. There before him was a face like a silver basin, peach-blossom fair; a head of black hair like dark clouds; two curving, vivid black brows; a small cherry mouth, red as crimson; a fragrance of powder and oil, a breath of orchid and musk. Yuanmao was beyond himself with joy. He helped her out of her outer garments, and the new bride slipped first into the bedclothes. Yuanmao hastily undressed and slid in after her, and what followed between them was a matter that need not be described; suffice to say the bride yielded by degrees and the first union was duly accomplished.",'The new bride handed him a handkerchief. Yuanmao remembered something about the practice of the "original red" and used it to wipe himself, then tucked it under the pillow to examine the next morning. He lay there thinking that this was truly a very satisfying experience, and tried to think of something to say, but nothing came to mind. He slept for a while, then rose again for a second union. The whole bed of quilts was newly padded cotton; moreover it was the tenth of the second month and the air had already turned warm. The exertion left Yuanmao steaming like a pot, sweat pouring down his head without stopping. He came to rest at last, and in the cooling-down happened to feel the handkerchief; forgetting he had already used it, he took it up and wiped his face and head with it. Then he pulled back half the quilt to let the heat escape and drifted off to sleep.',`Very early in the morning the new bride rose first and went to comb her hair in another room. Yuanmao got up, washed his face, and dressed; then, quietly slipping the handkerchief into his pocket, he tried to go and watch his bride at her morning toilette but was caught by an escort-woman who dragged him out to pay his respects to his father-in-law, and then there were various senior relations to greet, and the delay stretched on. By the time the bride had finished her dressing and sat in the room in her finest attire with her head bowed, Yuanmao sidled up and managed to squeeze out a few words; the bride could only smile without replying, but she stole a glance at him, a broad-featured face with good proportions, which, though except for the eyes and brows nothing really remarkable, was far better-looking than her two brothers, and she felt not displeased. Then she looked again and noticed a dusky, mottled darkness on his face, patches and streaks of it, deep and light in different places, patchy and uneven, like a man who had rubbed coal-black onto his face and not wiped it off clean. She thought to herself: "He must wash without soap. Tomorrow I'll tell him to scrub harder." Yuanmao stared at her with extreme satisfaction, thinking: "Life is good now. No more wandering about outside." He also thought of the handkerchief and stepped into the outer room where no one could see, drew it from his breast, and held it stretched out between both hands for inspection. He stood stock-still. After a moment he thought: "I must have taken the wrong one." He turned back inside, lifted every corner of the bedclothes but found nothing; searched under the quilt and under the pillow and found nothing either.`,`A maidservant nearby asked, "Son-in-law, what are you looking for? Let me help." Yuanmao looked around and saw the room full of maids and old serving-women; there was no way he could say. He had to go out again to a place with no one about, and hold the handkerchief up for close examination. He saw a series of streaks that were not quite lacquer, not quite oil, and not quite ink. He truly could not guess what it was. He sniffed it: a faint oily fragrance, with a trace of perspiration; he gave a sudden snort of laughter and thought, "No wonder her younger brother is full of learned quotations, even inside his sister's parts such dark fluid abounds." Then he reconsidered: "That's impossible." He turned the handkerchief over and examined it again very closely, and at one spot, at the edge of the black oil, there seeped a faint reddish tinge, like diluted rouge-water, odorless. He thought it over carefully for quite some time, and was suddenly struck by enlightenment. "That's it, that's it! This faint red shadow is undoubtedly the original red. As for all the black, someone must have been playing a prank yesterday and smeared some dark oil on my hat or onto my head, and with the sweating I wiped my head by mistake with this cloth." He tucked it back into his sleeve. He went in and ate the morning-after wedding feast, and in the warmth of new marriage the two were to each other as brother and sister.`,"After a few days, when Yuanmao was making the rounds of thanks to the matchmakers and paying calls on well-wishers, Wang Xun and Zhongqing asked him how he found his new wife. He said nothing about anything else, but kept talking about hair, one person said it was white silk-fine hair, another said it was silver thread and crane feathers, and no one could make out what he meant. People saw how delighted he was and were thoroughly puzzled.","Then Yuanmao recalled the incident when Pincai had been cursed, the joke had touched on white hair and white intimate hair, and that was what had made the bride so furious. His suspicions began to stir. And furthermore: every day his own face was becoming stained with a dark oiliness, and there was the strange matter of the handkerchief; and she rose very early and combed her hair in a separate room, always behind a closed door. What did all that signify? He could not resolve his doubts and dared not ask directly. One day he came into the room and saw her in high spirits, bedecked in pearls and jade, her hair plainly full and black, blacker than lacquer. He tried to get close enough to smell her head, but she pushed him away. He suddenly thought: perhaps her hair was naturally black while her intimate hair was white, and that was what people's remarks had been about. He thought again: even if she had a few white intimate hairs, how would outsiders know? But if that were truly the case, it wouldn't do. Then he thought: I must not entertain such ideas. Tonight I'll pull one out and look at it tomorrow, which will tell me all I need to know. He waited impatiently for dusk, and when they had gone to bed, he felt about for that precious thing, a lush, woolly patch of growth, but he could not bring himself to pull any, for fear of causing her pain. So he went ahead with his usual carrying-on, and afterward, feeling about with his hand in hopes that something might have naturally come loose and would put his mind at rest, he suddenly found that he did indeed have one. He was overjoyed; he pinched it tight between two fingers, reached out one hand to grope for a piece of paper under the mat, wrapped it up carefully, and then, having nowhere safe to put it, tucked it into the fold of his braid at the very top of his head for fear she might find it.","Madam Sun could not imagine what he had been doing. Yuanmao had spent half the night at his maneuvers, and in the morning slept in. Madam Sun had long since finished combing her hair. When Yuanmao finally got up and was washing his face, he remembered the paper packet in his braid, hurriedly put on his hat, ran into the outer room, opened it up and found a single jet-black strand.",`Yuanmao was delighted. "I've been suspecting for nothing these several days, for those sharp-tongued gossips were just talking nonsense." And his mind was at rest. What a fool that Yuanmao was, spending half the night on his scheme and obtaining a single hair, which all along was one of his own that had rubbed off onto his hand. He attributed it to his wife's and considered his suspicions entirely dissolved.`,`About half a month passed, and then one day the truth came out. While Madam Sun was combing her hair she felt a chill and called a maidservant to fetch her a sleeveless jacket from the other room. Yuanmao was already up, and seeing the maid carrying clothes into that other room, he followed along without enough time for the door to be closed. There, seated, was a figure dressed in a scarlet close-fitting garment, with a cascade of silver-silk hair over three feet of it hanging loose, and two eyebrows of a pale gold color. Li Yuanmao's heart gave a tremendous lurch; he thought he had encountered a ghost and almost turned and fled. But then he thought: the clothes are clearly hers; could she really be some white apparition? He hurried forward, and Madam Sun herself was so startled that before she could conceal anything her face had flushed scarlet. Li Yuanmao looked closely, and a rush of indignation rose straight to his head. "So that's how it is!" he cried. "My luck has well and truly run out, for I've married a goblin. This is the Ageless Crone from Journey to the West. She wants a husband too. Laughable, utterly laughable!" Madam Sun heard this, and in a torrent of shame and fury burst into tears and shouted: "We've treated you so well, I am a young lady of a good family, and we opened our doors to receive you as a son-in-law, a man lacking rank or gleaming prospects, yet you remain unsatisfied and leap to find fault! So what if my hair is a bit white? In every other respect, am I not your match? Or are you too fine for a mere cross-eyed man to aspire to? If you find fault with me, you can just divorce me!" She flew into a rage, and pots and ornaments flew about crashing on the floor. Li Yuanmao retreated to the other room muttering and cursing, and the two of them kept it up the whole morning.`,"As it happened, Madam Sun had for some time been mixing sesame oil with lampblack and then adding persimmon lacquer. She would first comb her hair out neatly and then carefully paint on the mixture, and the effect was sleeker and glossier than natural hair. The process required washing once a day; otherwise the hair became impossible to comb and the lacquer would not go on again. For washing she used a bucket of soapberry water with borax and alum to clean it thoroughly, then dried it to half-dampness before combing and setting, which took the better part of an hour. That morning she had been running a little late, and so the truth came to light. Yuanmao stormed out in a fury and went to stay at Wei Pincai's lodgings for a couple of days. Pincai asked him what had happened, and Yuanmao had no choice but to tell him the whole story. Pincai was immediately enlightened: he now understood how his joke the other night had hit exactly on the mark.","Madam Sun, seeing her husband fail to return for two or three days, grew anxious and reported the matter to her parents. Lianggong was furious; Madam Lu too was displeased, and she sent someone to the Mei household to inquire, but he was not there. After searching everywhere, he was found at Pincai's. Yuanmao still refused to go back; only after Pincai pressed him earnestly did he agree to return with the messenger, though he still would not go into the inner chambers and sat in the study chatting idly with Sihui instead. When Lianggong returned that evening he gave Yuanmao a talking-to, and Madam Lu reproved him as well, though at heart she felt they had somewhat wronged Yuanmao, and between scolding and coaxing persuaded him to go in. Yuanmao had no choice but to comply, but the memory of that apparition was still with him and he could not bring himself to be cheerful.",`Madam Sun, seeing him come in, wanted him to speak first and apologize, and sat without stirring. In the lamplight Yuanmao looked again and saw her distinctly black and white, a real beauty, and his heart softened a little. He spoke first. Madam Sun slowly replied. Yuanmao sat with head bowed and eyes shut, pondering for a while, and then suddenly a brilliant idea struck him; he jumped up and grinned at her. Madam Sun, seeing him beginning to come around, made a show of playing hard to get, she regarded him coolly and said nothing, studying her own reflection in the mirror. Yuanmao could not help saying: "Why not just tell me the truth? What's the point of hiding it from me? Now that we're married it's not as if we can undo it. I see you have to lacquer your hair every day, which is a great deal of trouble and very inconvenient, and every day it rubs off on my face and people laugh at me. I've thought of a solution that's simpler, more attractive, and won't leave oil on my face. Would you care to hear it?" Madam Sun, curious about what scheme he had devised, asked, "And what would that be?" Yuanmao said, "Like a young female role in the opera, put on a hairnet and tie it up. Who would ever see through that silver hair underneath? Or better yet, just shave it off entirely, which would be cleanest of all." Madam Sun said, "Shaving it off won't do. A hairnet, as you suggest, would be convenient enough. And I am tired of all that lacquering. Tomorrow morning I'll send someone to buy one." Yuanmao said, "You ought to scrape your face with a razor every day too, otherwise a few pale fine hairs will come out. If you shave your down and wear the hairnet, you could easily pass for a great beauty."`,`Madam Sun was pleased by this, and her expression softened into smiles. She said, "It's still early, so why not send someone to buy it now so it's ready for tomorrow morning?" Yuanmao said, "Buy it now and we can use it tonight, saving my face from being smeared again." Madam Sun called a maidservant to go tell the steward to buy one hairnet, one hairpiece, and one swallow-tail ornament, and to bring them back promptly. In short order everything was purchased. Madam Sun was overjoyed, and at once she put a pot of soapberry water on to boil, then scrubbed the lacquered oil from her hair until the washwater ran dark as the blue vats in a dyer's shop. Not even waiting for it to dry, she let Yuanmao blot it with a cloth, blotting and then blotting again.`,"Yuanmao then suggested she trim and shave back the temple-hair and the hairline all around so nothing would show. Madam Sun had an old serving-woman take a razor and shave all around the edges, and shaved off the eyebrows entirely as well; the fine down on her face was shaved smooth too. Then the hairnet was fitted on, the real hair coiled once around the base, the false hairpiece pinned in place with a clasp, the swallow-tail ornament tied in, a jade kingfisher ornament set on the forehead, and eyebrows painted on, and she truly became several degrees more handsome.",`By evening she looked for all the world like the Tipsy Lady Yang. Madam Sun had two large candles lit and used two mirrors, one in front and one behind, to admire herself from all angles, and she found the effect breathtaking. Yuanmao, looking at her, felt his heart blossom with delight; he stepped close, smelled the top of her head, rubbed his cheek against her face, finding only the faintest trace of oil, nothing like before. In high good spirits he dismissed the maids, took her by the hand to bed, and under the quilt together they bloomed like the night-opening flower. Then Yuanmao suddenly recalled the hair-pulling episode of the other night and asked Madam Sun, "I've heard that those with albinism have white hair all over their bodies, so why is your intimate hair dark?" Madam Sun said, "It isn't exactly dark." Yuanmao said, "Dearest, let me have a look."`,`Madam Sun refused. Yuanmao said, "Can you still be keeping things from me? After all I've done to fix things for you, what is there to hide?" Madam Sun said nothing. Yuanmao climbed naked out of bed, took up the candle, folded back the quilt. Madam Sun tried to shield herself, but Yuanmao could already see that her body was truly white as frost and snow, which he found quite charming.`,`When he came to that most intimate area, it was as if he had ridden upon a silver-maned horse, and indeed it bore out Pincai's joke, looking for all the world like a fly-whisk held over herself. Yuanmao could not suppress a laugh, and gave her a pinch. Madam Sun said, "What do you think you're doing? Aren't you yourself a nearsighted man? Why not have a good sniff as well?" Yuanmao, moved by what he had seen, set down the candle and climbed back into bed. What followed between them need not be told. As to what happened next, listen to the following chapter for the explanation.`]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations39:r},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),d={40:["To return to our story: after the last encounter in which Xi Shiyi had been humiliated by Qinyan and went home in a fury, he had been racking his mind for some way to exact his revenge. But, unable to think of any clever scheme, he had to content himself with spreading gossip wherever he went — claiming that Qinyan had been performing at drinking parties outside, that Qinyan had treated him with utmost warmth, and had actually wanted to follow him home.","By concocting such slander, he managed to vent a portion of his bitter resentment. As for the matter of the bracelet, when Juhua cross-examined him, Xi Shiyi simply claimed he had been careless, dropped it, and smashed it to pieces; Juhua could do nothing about it. As it happened, however, Baying told her the truth, and Juhua raised a tremendous uproar. Xi Shiyi pleaded softly and promised to match it with an even finer one in the future, and only then was the matter settled. Xi Shiyi's character was truly laughable — he was forever abandoning the old to dote upon the new. Previously he had bought Chunlan and treated her very well, but grew tired of her in under half a year. Last year he had acquired Baying, whom he treasured like a rare gem; but now he had entangled himself with Deyue and Zhuo Tianxiang, and had already grown cold toward Baying.",`Baying, harboring a heart full of indignation, fell to chatting idly with Chunlan one day and said, "Chunlan, think of how the old country bumpkin used to treat us — and now it's one forgotten for the next. Can you really take that lying down?" Chunlan said, "I was foolish in the past. When he bought out my apprenticeship, I truly thought him a man of feeling and loyalty — little did I know he possesses no conscience whatsoever. Look at the things he does: completely indifferent to harming heaven and injuring reason. Take that barrel of his — I don't know how many people he lured with it. I heard that even children from respectable families were coaxed by him, and upon returning home, some ended up hanging themselves or throwing themselves into the river. What sort of retribution is awaiting him in the future, I wonder?" Baying said, "I've heard tell of this barrel too — what was it like, that it could so easily trick people?"`,'Chunlan said, "The barrel was constructed in a Western fashion — narrow at the top and wide at the bottom, with something inside that clinked and chimed like a bell. He would toss an item down into it and tell the person to fetch it out with their hands. There was a partition board in the middle with two holes — each hole only wide enough to admit one hand. If a person reached both hands in, he would trigger the hidden mechanism inside, locking both hands so they could not be withdrawn. Left with their buttocks hoisted in the air, the person was entirely at his mercy to toy with as he pleased. I fell for his trick the very first time. Later, someone reported it to the authorities, and only then was the barrel finally smashed to pieces."',`Baying said, "It would be one thing if he could at least show some constancy in his treatment of people. In the past he used to frequently bestow rewards, but now even such gifts have become scarce — it's almost as if we owe him our buttocks in tribute. And yet this man somehow manages not to break out in sores! If his parts rotted clean off, we'd all be well rid of the trouble." Chunlan said, "You still have your former master here. If Xi Shiyi grows too cold toward you, you could ask for leave and go back to serving Master Ji — I think that would still be better than staying with him." Baying said, "That Master Ji is even worse — if he were any good, I wouldn't have jumped ship in the first place. The man makes your skin crawl, and on top of that he's stingy — he'll wear a person out all day and then toss you a mere few dozen cash, mixing in a few base coins at that. That is why I was even more unwilling to follow him. When I was working my trade back home, how free and easy life was! Even braiding a queue earned me several hundred cash. Come evening after supper, a few close and likeminded friends would go out together — whether to the teahouse or the wine-shop, joking and laughing, happy as could be. Wontons, steamed buns, three-treasure noodles in great bowls — whatever you fancied, you ate. And at the gambling table, if someone won, they'd cheerfully throw out eight hundred cash or a whole string without a second thought — truly generous and flush. From the day I followed that man surnamed Ji, my luck went straight downhill." Chunlan said, "That Master Ji's appearance is genuinely unappealing to look at, too — when he talks to you, he bares two yellow teeth, a truly ugly sight." Baying said, "And his body has that foxy, musky stench to boot."`,`But let us set idle chatter aside. To return to our story: one day Xi Shiyi came alone to the Hongji Temple. Both the monk and Pincai had gone out; the young novice was in his own room, lying sideways on the kang facing the wall, fast asleep. Xi Shiyi saw him wearing only a close-fitting moon-white silk inner jacket trimmed with fancy borders, and green crinkle-silk leg-warmers, his head freshly shaved and gleaming clean. The sight stirred Xi Shiyi's desire; he stripped off his outer robe, lay down beside him, quietly untied his sash, and pulled his trousers halfway down, pressing close against him in intimate service. Deyue woke with a start, turned his head, saw Xi Shiyi, and said, "You mustn't do this." Xi Shiyi paid him no heed. Deyue said again, "I really mean it — you mustn't do this." Xi Shiyi still thought he was playing coy and deliberately pressed a step forward — when suddenly, from deep within Deyue's belly came a loud rumbling gurgle. Deyue cried out "Oh no!" his body gave a jerk, and a blast of hot air rushed straight out. Xi Shiyi felt as though his nether regions had been plunged into hot water and hastily withdrew. With a great splattering sound, a torrent of liquid stool shot out, spraying all over Xi Shiyi's abdomen. Xi Shiyi cried, "What is this!" and hurriedly rolled off the kang.`,"Deyue scrambled down after him and immediately squatted on the floor; with a great rushing sound he released a massive puddle — the stench was overpowering. Xi Shiyi held his nose and peered at the floor; among the filth there seemed to be something resembling pus and blood.",`Xi Shiyi found some paper and spent a good while wiping himself; the crotch of his trousers and even his sash ties had caught a fair amount, and he wiped them clean one by one. Deyue wrinkled his brow, shifted himself aside, and only then finished and rose. Since they could not call anyone in to clean it up, Deyue himself scooped some ash from the coal brazier to cover it and swept it clean. Xi Shiyi said, "What am I to do — hurry and bring a basin of water for me to wash up." Deyue said, "I told you, you couldn't do this, but you wouldn't listen." He found a small earthenware basin, ladled in some water, and handed Xi Shiyi a foot-cloth; Xi Shiyi made do and wiped himself off.`,`Deyue lay back down. Xi Shiyi felt his mood thoroughly spoiled. Deyue said, "My body is unwell and my stomach is unsettled; I'm too lethargic to speak. You had better go." Xi Shiyi had no choice but to emerge, and happened to bump squarely into Zhuo Tianxiang coming in. Xi Shiyi said, "The monk and Master Wei are both out; Deyue is sick and too languid to entertain — don't bother going in." Tianxiang said, "Let's go sit over in Master Wei's quarters then — even though he's not home, we can still sit there." Xi Shiyi had no particular objection, so he went inside with Tianxiang, had Pincai's servant brew two bowls of tea, and fell to chatting idly. Tianxiang said, "Today I came to find Master Wei because I need to borrow a few strings of cash from him, but as luck would have it he's not at home — I don't know when he'll be back." Xi Shiyi said, "Where have you come from just now? You're covered in dust." Tianxiang said, "I went to collect money from that Muslim butcher who sells beef, but I didn't encounter him either." Xi Shiyi said, "How much money do you need to use?" Tianxiang said, "I'm still fifteen strings short, and I simply can't scrape it together." Xi Shiyi said, "What matter is so urgent?" Tianxiang said, "Yesterday Cuiguan was swindled out of eighty strings by some people and signed a promissory note for it; they're coming to collect today, and we promised to pay them back by tomorrow." Xi Shiyi said, "Who was it that swindled Cuiguan?" Tianxiang said, "Who else but the grassy-radical crowd? Yesterday they called him over to serve for a whole day, and then ended up tying him up, accusing him of stealing a snuff bottle, and threatening to haul him to the north yamen. The attendants who went with him pleaded again and again, until finally those people's men played the peacemakers and mediated a settlement of eighty strings, with a note signed — only then was he let go.`,`"Today we pawned all our clothes and only managed to raise sixty strings; we borrowed another five, and the Muslim fellow still owes us a few strings, but as luck would have it we can't find him. So now I have come to beg the great master to bestow fifteen strings to settle this matter." Xi Shiyi said, "What is there to worry about — it isn't due until tomorrow anyway. Let's sit for a while, and then we'll go to Third Master Pan's shop and write a cash draft, and that will be that." Tianxiang thanked him, and the two sat together chatting.`,"As it happened, when Tianxiang had gone to find the Muslim butcher, the butcher was not at home; but the butcher's nephew, who had some entanglements with Tianxiang, kept him in the shop to eat two small bowls of beef and five or six steamed buns. They then engaged in a brief affair of no great consequence, for which Tianxiang was given two strings of cash. Little did Tianxiang know that the young Muslim had only recently contracted the syphilitic poison of the plum blossom, which had not yet broken out — and in this encounter, he had passed it directly to Tianxiang. Tianxiang at this moment was feeling a burning, scalding discomfort at his rear passage, and was hoping Xi Shiyi might help quell the fire. Xi Shiyi, seeing Tianxiang's passions aroused, was himself in high spirits; and so, by unspoken mutual understanding, the two had a vigorous tumble. Pincai had still not returned.","Xi Shiyi had originally intended to go with him to Pan San's place to fetch the money, but suddenly felt a rush of fire within his body; his temples throbbed and swelled with pain, and a general malaise washed over him. He hastily dashed off a quick note and told Tianxiang to go collect it himself. Xi Shiyi returned straight home, his head spinning and vision blurring, barely able to hold himself together.","He undressed and slept through the night, burning as if engulfed in flames, while his lower parts ached excruciatingly. He reached down with his hand — it was dripping wet all down one thigh, and the organ was scalding hot to the touch, having already swollen to the size of a wine cup.",`He groaned without ceasing. Juhua could not find a moment's peaceful sleep all night, and the next morning, when she saw the state of that organ, she was given a terrible fright. She hurriedly asked the cause, but Xi Shiyi refused to speak plainly, merely claiming he had no idea why it had suddenly swollen up. Juhua said, "Let us invite a doctor to come take a look." Xi Shiyi said, "Master Tang the monk is excellent — he specializes in curing exactly these sorts of afflictions." Juhua accordingly sent someone to fetch him.`,"Now, the monk Tang had for several days observed that Deyue's complexion was irregular and his fingernails were turning blue; knowing he had absorbed some toxic substance, he had administered a purging decoction to attack the poison and reduce the fever. Yesterday Deyue had purged several times — just as Xi Shiyi had come along to receive it, allowing the poison to enter directly from the kidney channel straight into the heart channel. Xi Shiyi's constitution was already hollowed out, and then he had gone on to dally with Tianxiang, who had freshly contracted the sore-poison of the young Muslim — which Xi Shiyi had also gathered into himself. The simultaneous eruption of these two poisons made his condition exceedingly grave. Shortly after, the monk arrived and inquired into the origin of the illness; Xi Shiyi recounted only the affair with Tianxiang. The monk took his pulse and prescribed another purging decoction. But the toxic humors had struck too deep and could not be dislodged; after three continuous days, the condition only grew more severe. The swollen and ulcerating area had broken open at the head; Xi Shiyi was in unspeakable torment and had to engage yet another physician, who demanded two hundred taels of gold to guarantee a cure. They applied treatments both internally and externally. Who could have known that this new doctor was even less skilled than the monk, and lacked any superior medicines; the ulceration spread wider and wider, until what had once resembled a small monk's bald head had rotted into a honeycomb, the stench defying description. Xi Shiyi found he could no longer sleep lying down; he had no choice but to sit all day without trousers, wearing only leg-warmers, perched on a stool with both feet splayed wide apart, propped up by two small footstools. Between them dangled that rotting, eggplant-like appendage, filling his heart with bitter misery and desperate anxiety.","Juhua, seeing his plight, was heartbroken, but did not dare voice her resentments. She could only pray to the gods and make vows, nursing him with the utmost devotion. He changed doctors two or three times, but the afflicted organ merely melted away like a dripping candle-stub. At last, it was the monk Tang who, having learned of the situation, applied the finest quality Zhibao Dan salve, and so managed to preserve an inch or more of that Pillar of Posterity. After the wound finally healed over, it was left a limbless, headless stump, the flesh withered and the skin slack, resembling nothing anyone could name. As for the matter of the bedchamber, it was clear he would be incapable of it ever again. Such was the karmic retribution for Xi Shiyi's lusts.","But as the saying goes, coincidence makes the story; and this tale is truly laughable to tell. In Pan San's shop there was a young assistant named Xu Laosan; he was only sixteen years of age, and possessed quite a handsome appearance. Pan San had long coveted him and had tried to coax and deceive him on several occasions, but had unexpectedly never managed to get him in hand. The boy, however, had one particular flaw: he loved to drink a cup, and when he drank too much, he would inevitably fall asleep. On the fifteenth day of the first month, all the other shop assistants had gone home for the festival; Pan San deliberately kept only Laosan behind, and sat drinking wine with him in the small back accounting room. Xu Laosan grew drunk and fell asleep on the kang.","Pan San had long since laid his venomous scheme. He had gone to the barber's shop and gathered some short-clipped hair trimmings and shavings, hiding them on his person. Taking advantage of the boy's drunken stupor, he forcibly violated him; he then stuffed the hair clippings inside, intending that once the irritation flared up, the boy would be forced to seek him out for relief. When Xu Laosan awoke and realized he had been violated by him, he wanted to cry out, but out of concern for his own face, he had no choice but to swallow the grievance and bear the humiliation.","Who could have known that from then on he would develop a persistent malady. Knowing perfectly well he had fallen prey to Pan San's trick and had something planted inside him, he harbored a deep hatred in his heart, yet suppressed it, ignoring the man entirely. Pan San, congratulating himself on his success, assumed the boat would naturally drift toward the bank. Little did he know that Xu Laosan nursed his grievance bitterly. He had a brother-in-law named Zhou Xiaosan, who drove Pan San's cart — a man of considerable hot-blooded spirit, the very sort of friend who would draw a sword to aid a stranger facing injustice on the road.","After falling victim to the plot, Xu Laosan immediately informed his brother-in-law. His brother-in-law was ready to go raise a violent clamor with Pan San right then and there, but Laosan held him back, and together they deliberated on a clever stratagem to exact their revenge.","The next day Laosan went home. He had no parents, only two elder brothers; together they ran a small wine-shop, selling smoked meats and sausages. The second brother was an idle vagrant who performed comic routines in a variety troupe — an acrobat named Xu Er. His sister also lived at home. He recounted the deception he had suffered; his hatred gnawed at his very teeth, and he swore to take revenge. His second brother, hearing the tale, immediately stripped off his jacket and made as if to dash out and start a brawl.",`The eldest brother pulled him back, saying, "This is not a matter to be settled with fists; let us discuss it first. Go and invite Uncle Li San — he's the one who recommended Laosan for the post. We shall go and reason with him, and see what he has to say." The third sister said, "Fighting certainly won't do, but trying to reason with him is no good either. There are no physical wounds to show; surely we cannot take this to the Board of Punishments for an official examination? As I see it, let me devise a method to make him suffer a taste of it too — force him to swallow a bitter pill he cannot speak of."`,`The eldest and second brothers said, "Our sister speaks well, but he is a man of forty or fifty; how do you propose to make him swallow such a bitter pill?" The third sister laughed and said, "Let me think on it slowly." Now, this third sister was barely nineteen years of age, exceedingly beautiful, and a thousand times clever and articulate, always ready with a smile and a word. Had she been born a man, she would have been capable of great deeds, but fate had cruelly cast her in a woman's body. She pondered for a moment, then laughed: "I have a brilliant stratagem — only it lacks one specific person." The second brother said, "To avenge our little brother, I am willing to brave water or fire!" The third sister said, "This matter has no use for you; furthermore, it cannot be discussed with you. If I were to tell you, you would blab it abroad."`,`The second brother flared up in anger: "What sort of talk is that? If we are planning to ensnare someone, do you think I would go about broadcasting it to others?" The third sister said, "It requires only this and that, such and such, but we lack the person to do it." The eldest brother pondered: "Your sister-in-law is useless, she couldn't entice anyone, and besides, she has returned to her maiden home. Perhaps we should invite Old Ba's wife? Or send for the Ge girl?" The third sister said, "That will not do. Those women of the professional gates are neither kith nor kin — they might not be willing to come. Moreover, Pan San is acquainted with such women."`,`The second brother laughed. "Sister, we are all flesh-and-blood siblings; since we are avenging our brother, we ought to exert our own utmost efforts. On that day, what harm is there in using you as the bait? It is not as if you will actually have any real dealings with him. As long as we keep a sharp lookout and rush in swiftly, that will be that — and naturally, your husband must be invited as well. If we extort money from him, it remains within our own family; isn't that better than rewarding an outsider?"`,`The third sister spat in disgust and scolded, "You spout dog farts! Why don't you wait for your second sister-in-law to arrive and serve as the bait?" The second brother laughed: "She isn't married into the house yet! Who has the patience to wait half a year or a year? If she were already wedded and in the house, I would surely employ her; would I still be begging you?"`,`The eldest brother, hearing this, realized they could exact revenge and extort money at the same time, and so he added his persuasions: "What Second Brother says is quite reasonable; aside from you, my sister, there truly is no second person capable of this. However, once it is done, leaving Laosan aside, your husband's rice bowl will certainly be smashed as well." The third sister said, "That is of no consequence — three strings of cash a month can be scrounged up elsewhere. Since we have agreed on this course, it is best to act early. Go and fetch your brother-in-law. We must all be clear on the plan, and he must be willing." They sent for Zhou Xiaosan, and the third sister explained the scheme they had just plotted. Zhou Xiaosan agreed to everything without hesitation, and they set the action for the evening of the day after next.`,`A night passed. The next morning, the second brother went to Pan San's establishment to remove Laosan's bedding. Pan San, sensing the affair had come to light, was gripped by fear and desperately tried to detain him with words. Through Zhou Xiaosan's forceful persuasion, the bedding was left behind, and the second brother was convinced to return home. Pan San was boundlessly grateful to Xiaosan and offered his profuse thanks. Xiaosan said, "Third Master, if you truly intend to elevate my brother-in-law, I will go tomorrow and persuade him to return. The boy is muddle-headed, but once I enlighten him with a few words, he will understand. Tomorrow there happens to be a fortuitous occasion — I wonder if Third Master might deign to grant us face?" Pan San said, "What talk is this! Though you drive my cart, you are like a partner to me. Since you understand the bonds of fellowship so well, is there anything I would not agree to?" Xiaosan said, "If Third Master is willing to grant us face, then it is easily arranged." He continued: "Tomorrow happens to be my wife's birthday. There won't be a single relative at the house; Eldest Brother and Second Brother both have business tomorrow and cannot come, but Laosan will be there. Tomorrow evening, I wish to invite Third Master to sit for a while at my humble home. With Laosan present, we can speak openly face-to-face, and I will simply instruct him to return with you — that will settle the matter." Pan San was overjoyed and declared, "Excellent! If you bring this matter to a perfect conclusion, I shall reward you heavily. I will add one string of cash to your monthly wages." Xiaosan said, "For this, I offer my deepest thanks to Third Master." When the appointed evening arrived, Xiaosan accompanied Pan San on foot to his home. Pan San took a seat in the main room; Xiaosan went inside and brought out a cup of tea. Pan San said, "Since today is your good lady's birthday, I ought to offer my congratulations; please invite your lady out so we may exchange bows." Xiaosan said, "Congratulations are more than we dare accept. Having received such boundless grace from Third Master, I shall call her out to kowtow to you."`,`He called out twice, "Third Sister! Third Sister!" A voice answered from within — a voice so tender and delicate it enchanted the ear at once. Pan San heard the sharp click-clack of high-heeled wooden soles; reaching the doorway, a hand rested upon the frame, revealing two silver nail guards, and a voice asked: "What is it?" Xiaosan said, "Third Master honors us for the first time; you ought to come out and pay your respects. Furthermore, Third Master is a man of mature years, like a parent to us — there is no need to be bashful." Third Sister gave a little laugh and said, "I have tasks in the kitchen and haven't yet washed my hands. Laosan's mouth is so greedy; not only can he not help me, but if I plate up a dish, he'll eat half of it." She laughed again and retreated inside. Pan San, hearing this, felt his limbs go soft and languid; he thought to himself, "What a glorious voice — I wonder what her face looks like? If she resembles her brother, that would be wonderful indeed." Xiaosan pulled out the table and set three places. Laosan first brought out a wine pot, two wine cups, and two pairs of chopsticks, followed shortly by four dishes. Pan San saw they were a plate of salted pork, a plate of smoked fish, a plate of sausage, and a plate of wheat gluten. Xiaosan poured the wine, and the two sat down. Pan San said, "Laosan should come out and sit with us as well." Xiaosan immediately called Laosan out; Laosan said, "I don't drink wine." Pan San said, "Laosan, come, come, come! Have a cup."`,`Laosan ignored him and went back inside. Xiaosan said, "He's helping his sister prepare the dishes; he'll come out in a little while." Laosan then brought out two more plates and two bowls: a plate of stir-fried pig's liver, a plate of stir-fried mutton, a bowl of braised silver-thread noodles, and a bowl of deep-fried pork slices.`,'The two had been drinking for a while when a pounding at the door was heard, followed by two urgent shouts of "Xiaosan! Xiaosan!" Xiaosan hurriedly went to open the door. Pan San heard a cry of "Disaster!" — which gave him quite a start — followed by a flurry of urgent words.',`Xiaosan said, "I'm coming immediately." The man said, "Let's walk together—don't dally."`,`Xiaosan came back inside and said to Pan San, "Third Master, please sit for a moment. I will tell Laosan to come keep you company. I must go out and mediate an affair, but I will be right back." Pan San said, "I will leave as well." Xiaosan said, "What's the rush? I'll be back in an instant." Pan San's true motivation for staying was Laosan; he thought that with Xiaosan gone, he could take the opportunity to accompany him. Though his mouth spoke of leaving, his body did not move. Xiaosan called for Laosan to come out, but Laosan stubbornly refused. Xiaosan muttered a curse, "Muddle-headed boy!" and then called out, "Third Sister, come out." Third Sister appeared at the doorway and asked, "What is it now?" Xiaosan said, "Your second brother has stirred up trouble again, and I must go and mediate. Third Master is here, and Laosan refuses to come out. I figure — Third Master is a man of fifty or so, you are young enough to be his daughter; just be gracious, come out and keep him company for a moment, and I shall return shortly." Third Sister said, "I don't know how to keep company; I am a woman of the inner chambers. If I am inattentive and slight Third Master, how will that do? Third Master will surely blame you." Hearing these few words, Pan San felt his very soul melt away. Desperate for her to emerge, he immediately interjected, "The lady speaks too kindly. I had originally intended to offer my birthday wishes to the lady; please, I beg you to come out!" Pan San had already stood up.`,`Third Sister emerged with a laugh; catching sight of her, Pan San's soul soared beyond the heavens. He saw she had a melon-seed face and a pair of phoenix eyes; her hair was combed into a large ingot-shaped coil, adorned with a single blossom. She wore an eggplant-purple cotton blouse — originally green cotton, washed until it had faded to that hue — and beneath it, faintly visible, a pair of moon-white silk trousers. Her bound feet were exceptionally small, barely three inches long. Her figure was neither too tall nor too short, neither too plump nor too slender. Her face, fragrant and wreathed in smiles, turned toward Pan San as she bobbed a curtsy. Pan San, his heart ablaze with lust, hurriedly returned the bow and invited her to sit. She declined, and instead said to Xiaosan, "Come back quickly, lest you make Third Master wait until he grows impatient." Xiaosan agreed, and from outside he could be heard saying, "At the very fastest, it will be the second watch before I return; it's a journey of five or six li." With that, he scurried off. Third Sister went out to bolt the door, then came back in and sat down. Pan San, his face plastered with a beaming smile, asked, "Lady, what is your honorable age this year?" Third Sister replied, "Nineteen." She then added, "Third Master, our Xiaosan is a rough man; if there are times he fails to serve you adequately, we are deeply indebted to Third Master's grace for constantly looking after him. We poor folk have nothing of value to offer — just this meager token of our feelings. The wine is not enough to make you drunk, the food not enough to fill your belly." So saying, she swayed gracefully to the table, took up the wine pot, poured a cup, and set it down.`,`Pan San was overwhelmed with delight; he said, "Lady, why not come sit over here? I am truly uneasy making you exert yourself so." Third Sister smiled and called out, "Laosan, Third Brother, come out!" Laosan replied, "I'm not coming; you keep him company." Third Sister laughed and said, "You refuse to come out and entertain your own guest, and instead demand that I keep him company for you — where in the world is there such a stubborn child! No wonder people plot against you." Pan San, catching the hidden barb in her words, immediately said, "Xiaosan is like family in my home; Lady, please just sit down — I am sure there is no harm in it." Third Sister said, "My sitting here is just the same." Pan San said, "Though the lady sitting there is the same, it is somewhat distant, making conversation difficult. Please, come sit closer." Third Sister rose slightly, smiled faintly, and sat back down. Pan San stood up, poured a cup of wine, and brought it to Third Sister's side, saying, "Allow me to offer the lady a cup." Third Sister said, "I wouldn't dare, I wouldn't dare! Third Master, please drink it yourself." Though her lips demurred, she took the cup, adding, "How can I let Third Master serve me wine!" and drained it in a single gulp. She then stepped to the table, wiped the cup with her hand, filled it again, and said, "Third Master, please drink this cup." Pan San's heart was already intoxicated; he gasped breathlessly, "How dare I decline the lady's profound affection!" He took the cup, and as he did so, he gave her wrist a squeeze. Third Sister lowered her head. Pan San drank the wine, and unable to restrain himself any longer, draped his arm over Third Sister's fragrant shoulder, saying, "Lady, please sit; do not tire your delicate little feet." Third Sister smiled, but did not move to sit beside him. Pan San said, "Xiaosan is out of the house every day; doesn't the lady feel lonely at home?" Third Sister replied, "Previously we had an old serving woman, but she left a few days ago, and we haven't hired a replacement yet." Pan San said, "To have the lady prepare everything with her own hands today — my fortune is truly profound!" He poured another cup and handed it over.`,`The wine was soon exhausted. Third Sister said, "There is still wine to be had. Brother, go and buy half a catty of good strong spirits. The wine we just had was too mild. Go to the main street to buy it; don't be lazy about the distance and buy from a small corner stall." Laosan voiced his agreement and, without lighting a lantern, departed by the light of the moon. Third Sister said, "I will close the door; going to the main street will take him some time." Pan San, seeing her go to bolt the door, thought to himself: "Now is the time to strike. This woman has been making eyes at me; I must not disappoint her." Third Sister returned and sat down. Pan San, by this time, was consumed by the fires of lust, his face flushed crimson. He stepped over and said, "Lady, drink one more cup." He sat down on the edge of her stool. Third Sister made a show of trying to move away; Pan San immediately seized her sleeve. Third Sister kept her head lowered, merely laughing continuously. Pan San's mind was in a whirl of confusion; summoning his courage, he set down the cup and threw both arms around her. Third Sister said, "Third Master, what are you doing embracing me so?" She cast a sidelong glance at him. Pan San hastily pleaded, "My mother! Your son knows not what he is doing." He pulled Third Sister onto his lap, intent on kissing her.`,`Third Sister blocked his face with one hand and said, "You must not! Third Master, you are truly acting improperly, taking liberties with a respectable woman. If I were to cry out, you would lose all face." Pan San pleaded, "My mother, bestow a little mercy upon me!" Third Sister said, "Have you truly taken a fancy to me? It's not as cheap and easy as that! You have underestimated me entirely." Pan San said, "Lady, if you are willing to bestow this grace, whatever you say shall be done." Third Sister pushed his face away with one hand, while with the other she grasped his arm, feeling for his gold bracelet. Pan San understood immediately and thought to himself, "She wants this; there's no helping it." He took it off and said, "Lady, if you are willing to do a good deed and take pity on me, I shall give you this bracelet. In the future, I will reward you handsomely, and increase Xiaosan's wages as well." Third Sister accepted the bracelet, slipped it onto her own wrist, and laughed: "Many thanks to you. Now that I have agreed to this, you must not go telling others." Pan San agreed repeatedly and moved to pull at her trousers. Third Sister quickly hopped down and said, "Come into the bedroom!" Saying this, she led the way, with Pan San following closely behind. Reaching the edge of the kang, Third Sister said, "Take off your long robe, and we will amuse ourselves right here on the edge of the kang."`,`Third Sister first sat down to one side. Pan San unfastened his long robe and pulled down his trousers; just as he was about to press himself against her, he suddenly heard footsteps behind him. Turning his head, he was given a terrible fright, and hastily tried to pull his trousers back up. Zhou Xiaosan had already stormed into the room; he gave a great shout, seized Pan San in a tight grip, and cursed, "You audacious son of a turtle! So you are no kind of human being at all!" Pan San was frightened utterly speechless. Third Sister hurriedly interjected, "Third Master was just asking for the chamber pot to relieve himself; what did you think was happening?" Xiaosan snapped, "You shameless whore! The moment you see a man you latch onto him, and you still dare to make excuses to my face! Is the chamber pot hidden on your person?" Third Sister shouted, "Don't you dare falsely accuse innocent people!" Xiaosan said, "He has violated you, and you claim I am making false accusations. The matter cannot be settled clearly here; let us go to the neighbors and ask them to judge the right and wrong of it. I invited you here out of goodwill to drink wine, and you repay me by violating my wife!" As he spoke, he dragged Pan San toward the door. Pan San grew frantic and pleaded, "Xiaosan, do not act so rashly. We can talk this over reasonably. I admit the fault is mine; I should not have entered your inner chambers. But considering our many years of friendship, please show some leniency; there is nothing we cannot discuss openly." Xiaosan retorted, "What is there to discuss! I no longer want this wife of mine. I shall deliver both of you to the magistrate and let the official decide. Whether she is awarded to you or to me, there is no need for us to argue here." Third Sister stood to the side, feigning tears. Pan San was utterly helpless and could only resort to soft pleas. Third Sister cursed him: "Have you gone mad from poverty! What have I done that you wish to divorce me? If you take me to the magistrate, I will have my own testimony to give." As she spoke, she shot a meaningful glance at Pan San. Pan San pleaded, "Xiaosan, let go of me. We can discuss this; there is nothing I am unwilling to talk about." Xiaosan said, "Discuss what! I no longer want this person. Bring me a thousand taels of silver, and I will spare you." Pan San said, "If you want silver, that is easily discussed; just let go of me first." Xiaosan sneered, "Let go of you? Not so cheap!" and violently wrestled Pan San to the floor.`,`Pan San cried out, "Lady, please speak a word of persuasion!" Xiaosan threatened, "Think it over! If you are willing to hand over a thousand taels of silver, then agree to it obediently. If you refuse, I will tie you up and deliver you to the magistrate!" Pan San desperately agreed, "I am willing! I am willing! But how can I possibly produce a thousand taels? I have promissory notes on my person worth three hundred strings of cash; I will give them to you." Xiaosan sneered, "What is three hundred strings of cash worth?" Third Sister interjected, "You should feel your own conscience as well! Third Master has treated you so well; even if he made a mistake today, you must consider past affections. If you know how to recognize grace and repay it, do you truly think Third Master won't know good from bad?" Pan San hastily agreed, "The lady speaks truly! I am a man who deeply understands the bonds of fellowship. Xiaosan, let us preserve our relationship; there is not a day I will not look after you. Why must it come to this today?" Xiaosan said, "Since it is like this, let us speak plainly. In any case, the person has been played with by you; whether once or a hundred times, it is all the same. This green hat of mine cannot be cast off. First, hand over the three hundred strings of cash; thereafter, you must provide sixty strings of cash every month. Do you agree or not?" Pan San cried out, "I agree! I agree!" Xiaosan finally loosened his grip. Pan San scrambled up, handed over the cash notes, and straightened his clothes. Third Sister said to Xiaosan, "Light a lantern and escort Third Master back to his residence; he has suffered a fright." Xiaosan laughed and said, "Third Master, do not be afraid; we were merely playing a joke on you." Only then did Pan San's heart settle, though it was still pounding wildly in his chest. He said, "A fine joke! Such a joke is best played only once." Xiaosan said, "From now on, we will act exactly as you, old sir, command; there will be no more jokes." Pan San finally regained his composure. While Xiaosan went to light the lantern, Third Sister tugged at Pan San's clothes from behind and whispered the word "tomorrow." Pan San, having failed to consummate the act, heard these words and his spirits immediately lifted; he nodded repeatedly. Xiaosan escorted Pan San out. Third Sister's plot had succeeded perfectly. Pan San returned home in high spirits, and the very next morning he dispatched Xiaosan to the countryside on an errand.`,"After eating breakfast, he arrived at Xiaosan's house. Finding the door unbolted, he pushed it open and entered. He saw Third Sister sitting in the room, teasing a small dog. Pan San coughed once, and Third Sister's face immediately bloomed into a radiant smile.",`Pan San said, "Yesterday I was nearly frightened to death." Third Sister scoffed, "He only wanted the money; did you think he truly intended to seize you?" Pan San asked, "Is there anyone else in the house?" Third Sister replied, "Who else would there be?" Pan San said, "I will go and bolt the door." Third Sister said, "The weather is warm today; it will be more refreshing to take off our clothes." She then added, "I'm desperate to relieve myself," and ran to the back courtyard to urinate. Looking back, she said to Pan San, "You strip naked first and get under the covers." Pan San dared not disobey. Just as he stripped bare, he saw Third Sister returning with a smile, holding up her trousers with both hands. His mind fully at ease, Pan San stripped stark naked, climbed onto the kang, and pulled the quilt over his body. Third Sister also walked over to the edge of the kang.`,`Pan San urged, "Come quickly!" and reached out to pull her. Third Sister laughed and said, "Close the bedroom door." Just as she turned around, a voice from outside roared, "A fine deed you're doing!" followed by the thunderous clatter of footsteps. Pan San, hearing this, felt his soul flee his body. He saw Zhou Xiaosan leading his two brothers-in-law, brandishing gleaming blades and carrying a thick hemp rope. They surged forward, pinned Pan San down, and dragged him off the kang. Xu Laoer delivered three or four heavy punches, cursing, "You dog-dicked son of a turtle! You violated my brother, and now you dare to violate my sister!" Pan San could do nothing but kowtow on the floor. Xiaosan shouted, "I spared your dog's life yesterday, and today you come seeking death again!" With that, they bound Pan San tightly. Naked as the day he was born, Pan San could only beg for mercy. Xu Laoer sneered, "You know well how to violate other men's rear passages; today the old master will violate yours!" Pan San grew frantic and begged bitterly for his life. Third Sister stood to the side, trembling with laughter. Then, the second brother extended a middle finger — thick as a small yellow radish — dipped it in a jar of oil, and ruthlessly gouged it into Pan San's rear passage. Pan San cried out "Aiyo!" incessantly. Xu Laoer then unwrapped a paper packet and stuffed the medicine and hair clippings inside, repeating the action two or three times. Pan San groaned in agony, his feet kicking wildly. Fortunately, his anus was aged and tough, and did not bleed from the gouging. Once Xu Laoer finished stuffing, he released Pan San. Pan San was left trembling uncontrollably. Xu Laoda declared, "Pan San, do you know your crime? My perfectly good brother was forcibly violated by you — a deed heaven's laws cannot tolerate! Furthermore, you planted things inside him, condemning him to a lifetime of illness, unable to be a proper man. Therefore, today we return the favor, making you suffer the same filthy affliction. Tell me, do you deserve this or not?"`,`Pan San hung his head, utterly bereft of words. He put on his trousers, shoes, and socks, and then said to Xiaosan, "Since you were seeking revenge for another, you should not have taken my money." Xiaosan retorted, "What money of yours did I take?" Pan San said, "Not only the money, but also an eight-tael gold bracelet." Xiaosan scoffed, "Go back and file a lawsuit against me, then!" Third Sister chimed in, "Pan San, if you wish to file a lawsuit, say so early; I need to practice my confession so I don't stumble over my words when I appear in court." Being but one man, how could Pan San possibly prevail against them? He had no choice but to swallow his rage. His rear passage was now burning with an excruciating, fiery pain, and he moved to put on his clothes. Zhou Xiaosan stepped forward and snatched them away, shouting, "Do you still expect to wear clothes out of here?" Third Sister intervened, "Give them to him; let him cover that dog's face of his." Pan San donned his clothes and scrambled toward the door. He heard Third Sister laugh and call out, "Pan San, turn around! When you have free time tomorrow, come visit again; I'll find something to scratch your itch for you!" The three men clapped their hands and roared with laughter. Pan San, consumed by shame and fury, scurried away clutching his head like a fleeing rat.`,`The four siblings and husband continued to laugh uproariously for quite some time. Third Sister said, "This Pan San has been thoroughly dealt with by us; it is all thanks to Second Brother's willingness to execute such a ruthless plan." Second Brother laughed, "I didn't even use my full strength; I was afraid of digging his intestines out!" Third Sister said, "As for those three hundred strings of cash, I have a proposal; I do not know if my two elder brothers will agree to it." Eldest Brother and Second Brother replied, "This entire affair was accomplished through our sister's merit; whatever our sister decides, we will agree without question." Third Sister said, "Give one hundred strings to your brother-in-law to serve as capital, so he need not drive carts anymore. Second Brother, you take thirty strings, and Eldest Brother, you take thirty strings. The remaining one hundred and forty strings we shall save for Third Brother to use as capital; find a shop and let it generate interest for him. Since this money came about because of him, it is only natural he should receive the larger share."`,`The two brothers both agreed, "Very right." Xiaosan had already exchanged the notes for cash with Pan San that very morning — a detail prearranged — and he now brought the money out and handed it to Third Sister. Once she had finished distributing it, she added, "However, Third Brother's ailment is the most pressing matter; we must cure him first and foremost."`,'Xu Laoda asked, "What method is there for this?" Third Sister explained, "I have heard that eating buckwheat noodles can expel pig or sheep hair swallowed into the stomach. Take this buckwheat flour, make it into dumplings filled with sugar, but do not boil them completely through. Serve them to him slightly raw for two days; perhaps it will draw the affliction out." The two brothers said, "This is easiest of all; we will return immediately and make some for him to eat." They sat for a short while longer, and then the two brothers took their money and departed for their own homes. To know what happened next, you must listen to the explanation in the following chapter.']},b=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,chapterTranslations40:d},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"}));export{b as _,p as a,w as b,y as c,f as d,m as e,c as f,g,u as h,l as i};
